      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Coburg Wedding.—The Duchess of Albany has been staying in Germany for her son's wedding, for which great preparations have been made at Coburg. Her Royal Highness is devoted to her future daughter- ' in-Jaw, Princess Victoria Adelaide, whose trousseau is on a scare of royal magnificence, the bride being a niece of the German Emperor. It is a mistake to , suppose that the young Duke is a mere ornamental figure head, in his miniature kingdom, or that he is sub- ject as vassal, to the Kaiser. The Duchy has its Diet or Parliament, by which it is really governed, but the Duke is as much an independent Sovereign as the King of Saxony, and can exercise his rights as such,includins the conferring of orders, and titles of nobility. He is in fact one of the fellow-sovereigns with the Kaiser, in confederating Germany. A Gifted Novelist.—The Ammemoration last week at Haworth, of the fiftieth year after Charlotte Brontes death, is an event of interest not only to the people of Yorkshire, but to all admirers of the authoress of "Jane Eyre." Charlotte Bronte was not born at Howarth, but she removed thither at the age of four, and her father I was incumbent of the parish for a period of forty-one I years. It was there that " Jane Eyre," and other works : from the same hand were written,after Miss Bronte'sfirst tale "The Professor" had been refaced by the publishers. Jane Eyre," which was given to the world as the work of" Currerllell," was an immediate success, but the name of the writer remained unknown,until a Yorkshire- rnan who knew Haworth, guessed and published the secret. -Char mte'</p>
        <p>life was</p>
        <p>Her Strenous .arlotto Brol. .es early life svo... one of joyless sombreness and asperity, for her father, the Rev. Patrick Bronte was both poor and of erascible temperament. She was born in 1816, and began writing I when she was fourteen. Many years of ill success I followed, some of which she passed as a struggling I governess, and it was not until 1847, that" Jane Eyre" I burst upon the world and was received with a chorus I of praise. Other works of equal brilliance followed. I but not many years of fame were left to the gifted , authoress. in 1854 she married her father's curate, the Rev. A. B. Nicholls, but her health broke down; and she died the following March 1851 Her father opposed her marriage, for one reason on account of the ill-health of Mr. Nicholls, but this gentleman is still living, and is now approaching his ninetieth year. The churchyard at Haworth. where the remains of the distinguished authoress were interred, is a favourite place of pilgrim- age to members of the Bronte Society, and indeed there are so many visitors to Haworth that the Midland Railway Co., still end it worth while to advertise excur- sions to "The Bront4 Country." The Antipathy to Domestic Service.—One of the I subjects discussed at the Women's institute this week was a motion "That the objection to domestic serivce is not irrational." No doubt there are some mistresses so tyrannical that it would be irrational to 'expect any' girl to work for them, but these persons, who are largely responsible for the unpopularity of domestic service, are happily not a majority. Ti is also a reason- able objection on the part of maidservants to hear themsels es referred to as " slaveys" and in similar dis- paraging terms. At the same time there are many cases where a girl who was fortunate in her employer, would have no reason to regret entering domestic service. Daring the past few years there has been an increasing ambition on the pert of girls to obtain positions as shorthand-typists. Where they have the necessary education this is well, but at the present moment the London offices are full of girls who have displaced men as typists, but have not the necessary qualifications for the work. They are employed because they are cheaper than men, but they are kept during long hours at sedentary (work which affords no variety, and when their railwayfares and other expenses are paid, their income is less than they wouid receive in domestic service, although they have to board and ledge them- selves. Among these girl typists are not a few whose health is far from satisfactory, and in a very few years, many of them break down. Fruit and Nut Luncheons.—An enterprising West End London fruiterer has hit upon the novel idea of pro- viding fruit luncheons, and judging by the way his little tables—dotted about beneath towering palms, and screened by bamboo—are patronised, a decided want has been met. Sixpence is the lowest sum charged, nine- penny, and one shilling luncheons being supplied. The fruit and nuts are served very daintily, and flower- printed serviettes, together with a fruit knife and fork, and a pair of nut crackers, are forthcoming, for every patron. So assured is the fruiterer that his scheme will prove a success, that he is enlarging his premises. and shortly, brown bread and butter, also coffee and cream, are tc be added to the menu. Furnishing the Fireplace.—Wrought iron curbs and flreirons are the most fashionable for dining room, library, and hall, but for the drawing-room and bed- rooms, those built of copper are preferred. Bress has bad its day, and no longer finds a place in the affections of the up-to-date furnisher. Undoubtedly copper is in many ways n better selection, moreover the warm tone of the metal, gives a pretty glow to the fireplace. In conjunction •aith oak mantelpieces and green tiles, the aluminium curbs and ilreirons have the most charm- ing effect, but save to the few, they will be impossible, on account of the prohibitive price asked for them. Concerning Waists.--In the modistic world, figure is an all inipoitant point, and waists are being cnitivated assiduously. The straight front is decidedly a fashion of the past, and the pointed bodice, which fits tightly has the affections of the highest authorities. To the girls and women who can afford perfectly cut and moulded corsets, an expensive item by the way, the new mode will have no terrors, but when the luxury of good corsets is denied us, it is unquestionably better to remain faithful to the moderately pouched •..!' e end blouse. By those who have once more cultivated the round waist line, the skirt cut in one with a corselet in high favour, A soft lace bodice, covered with a short velvet jacket, completes this delightful old world toilette.</p>
        <p>The Bead Necklet.—The fashionable necklace just now, is composed of coloured beads, very small, and known as the "cut garnet bead" uhich, however, is not a garnet,ancl may beef any shade. Usually the two or three lower rows are formed to hang in short loops, and often the gold clasp is studded with precious stones,</p>
        <p>This is to intimate that you can Furnish for Cash or on Easy Terms of Payment, at the Lowest Prices in the Kingdom,—That all Goods are marked in Plain Figures,—That the Stock is second to none out of London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
