      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>Sing with the colour of the necklace. This, of course, dould harmonise with the gown, and in the cat() of an all white toilette, with the, colour of the waistbelt. With an all black gown, jet beads and ornaments are much in evidence, but it should be remembered that to wear jet successfully one must be a blonde, and more- over possess a good skin and complexion. An Old-World Fashion.—The nightcap has once more returned to favour, and the Society woman is very particular that this dainty accersory exactly matches the nightdress with which it is worn. Anything more widely different from the hideous arrangement beloved by our grandmothers it would be difficult to imagine, the up-to-date nightcap taking the form of a Juliet cap, only finished off with becoming frills. It is usually built of silk or muslin, the cap portion and the frill edgings being of good lace. Baby ribbon, to match in colour that run into the lace of the nightdress, appears upon the fashionable nightcap, and tiny sachets, con- taining the wearer's favourite scent, are inserted among the frills.</p>
