      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HAVING</head>
        <p>HAVING</p>
        <p>BEEN CURED</p>
        <p>IAIYIS' PINK PILLS.</p>
        <p>When a woman suffers her eyes tell the tale. One , can see her strength is failing and her beauty fading. I It is a characteristic of Dr. Williams Pink Pills for Pale People that they dispel all womanly ailments and at the same ti-ue restore lost beauty. " I have been very ill, but am so no longer," stated 1 Mrs. Priest, of 16, Roberts-terrace, Wal " Dr. I Williams' Pink Pills have entirely cured me of liver and I kidney disorders. I used to suffer from constant head- aches, with an increasing dislike to food, however ! tempting ; then severe bilious attacks. Day and night , a heavy languid feeling oppressed me. Terrible pains in my back signified my kidneys were responsible. To ! rise from a chair meant agony. A clever doctor gave me medicine, but my stomach was too weak for me to take physic. I soon became a chronic invalid, unable to help myself or even dress." " One day, when I was suffering intensely, a lady ; persuaded me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. I did, and before long I began to feel like a new woman. 1 , could eat and sleep better than I had done for months, I and my household duties became a pleasure. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills were the sole cause of my cure. can safely say that I feel better now than ever before." I Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Palo People give rich and pare blood with every dose. They enrich the blood and strengthen the nervous system. They are good for adults, children, and old people, but indispensable to women of all ages. They cure Anmaila, General Weak- ness, Nervous Disorders, Sick Headaches, Neuralgia, I Rheumatism, Sciatica, Paralysis, Locomotor Ataxy, and the ills that women sutler. Of all dealers, also from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co.; Holborn-viaduct, London, post free, 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes. THE DUTIES OF AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>IThe current number of "The Accountant," dealing , with the audit of municipal accounts, says :—"The late Lord Russell of liillowen stated in Thomas v. the Cor- poration of Devonport that he did not subscribe to the ' doctrine that the auditor's sole duty was to see whether there were vouchers formal and regular justifying each lof the items in respect of which the authority sought ,to get credit upon the accounts. That, he said, was an 1 incomplete and imperfect view of the duties of such an auditor, who was not only entitled but justified and bound to go further than that, and to make a fair and 'reasonable examination of the accounts and see whether I there might not be among the payments made payments I which were not authorised or which were illegally made and if he so discovered such payments it would be nig duty to make them public, certainly to report them to the authority itself, and certainly to report them to the burgesses who had created that authority. Lord Justices Smith and Vaughan Williams concurred in I this view. But, although at first sight it may, perhaps, ! appear to considerably extend the scope of a municipal audit, as contemplated in section 27 of the Act of 1892, it will, upon due consideration, be found that there is nothing .abatever inconsistent in this judgment 'with our statement that the statutory audit is 'l'the audit not of the accounts of the Corporation, but of the accounts of the treasurer. It is the duty of the treasurer to see that all payments made by him are proper payments, and thus, although the late Lord Chief Justice's finding in the Devonport case is useful and explanatory, it cannot be regarded as in any way demonstrating the practical utility of the statutory audit. As a matter of fact, the audit con- templated by the Act of 1882 and the audit contemplated by the Court of Appeal in the case already referred to, is clearly not an "audit " in the ordinary commercial sense of the term, but merely such an inquiry as is usually conducted by the auditors appointed by the Local Government Board to examine the accounts, of n ch authorities as come under their jurisdiction.</p>
        <p>would suggest that our business girl invests in a plain, well-made twekl or serge frock, with, if possible, a coat to match; and, thus equipped, she can go through the autumn and winter with very little trouble on matters of Cire3S, and yet look neat and smart. The frock under our notice has a box-pleated bodice and skirt, with a front of embroidery to match, strapped over with the material, the elbow cuffs being strapped to correspond. A change may be rung by wearing different dark-tinted ties and belts, and various dainty lawn collars.</p>
        <p>LADY " FELLOWS."</p>
        <p>Among the candidates who come up for elec- tion to the fellowship of the Geological Society at the meeting on the 16th is Lady St. Helier, She will not, the Manchester Guardian points out, be the only lady " FelloW," for the oldest member in the Society's list is the Baroness Burdett-Coutts, who was elected in 1852, the year following that of the Prince Consort's presidentship.</p>
