      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CLERGYMAN AND HIS SINGER WIPE.</head>
        <p>THE CLERGYMAN AND HIS SINGER WIPE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Edwin Riley is the pastor of Grace Church, Philadelphia. His wife, who was Miss Harrison, last year won Mme. Nordica's prize for the best soprano voice in America—a £1,4110 scholarship at the Paris Conservatoire, but her marriage prevented her taking advantage of it. However, she has accepted an offer from Mr. Victor Herbert, a well-known musical comedy composer, to sing in a concert tour this winter. This circumstance has so annoyed the congrega- tion in the Quaker city that the pastor has been compelled to resign. A SIIART LITTLE HAT.</p>
        <p>Now that we are Well into October we may feel assured that fashions in millinery are pretty well established; and will not greatly alter in form during the autumn and early winter. The new millinery is very charming, and extremely varied in its styles. Velvet, beaver, cloth." and felt hats are equally popular; but felt, of course, is much the most popular, as well as the smartest wear for everyday hats. Sketched in our illus-</p>
        <p>tration is a smart and becoming little hat of white felt. Thu brim has a finger-breadth of green velvet binding it 8,11 round. On the left side it is turned up sharply, and is adorned by two " made-up " wings of green and gold plumage. One of the large new buttons, of green velvet and gold cord, holds these wings in place. The low, round crown is encircled by a swathing of green velvet, and a knot of the same rich material rests on the hair bohind. DRESS FOR AUTUMN TRAVELLERS. Travelling from Scotland to London, writes a correspondent. I was much interested in tho dresses - worn by ' the ladies seated in the long luncheon saloon. Everyone knows it is the end of the summer season, a critical time in dress, and many of the travellers were returning from 'a two months' holiday among the heather hills. I could not find the ideal travelling costume as I glanced round. One stout lady in brown cloth and good sables ruined the effect by wearing a huge chest of brown chiffon under her chin, en- tirely hiding her short neck. Another had chosen a blouse of an e,xtraordinarily bizarre blending of red, white, and blue. A girl opposite wore a largo floppy hat trimmed with scarlet flowers, which looked untidy. Perhaps the best result was achieved by a lady of middle age who wore a rough and simple coat and skirt of brawn and white check, with a linen collar, anti a motor cap and veil. ' It• was not becoming, but it was neat, and could not be hurt by the rather grimy carriage. How seldom, I thought, does one see the ideal costume for a big journey: which, to my mind, is a long, well-cut travelling coat, worn with serviceable gloves, and a small tidy hat. A coat of this kind, though expensive, is a capital investment. TO-DAY'S PECIPE. PUREE OF WOODPIGEON. Three pigeons, 2 quarts of stock, 2oz, ef butter, 2 onions, 2 carrots, 1 teaspoonful of salt, 2oz. of sugar, and the juice of 1 lemon. Cut the birds in half, and fry them in the butter with the carrots and onions cut in slices; stow in the stock for three- quarters of an hour; tako out the pigeons, skin them and cut the meat from the bones, putting the latter back into the part: continue stewing I until the vegetables are thoroughly done, then strain; pouna toe meat aim yegetatnes tri a mortar and rub through a wire sieve; when cool, remove all grease from the soup, and, when required, add to it the puree of meat and vegetables and the juice of the lemon before warming,</p>
