      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>IN some leprosy e%,„ __outs at Manila, twenty- five cases have been treated by Röntgen rays, and six are reporte(l actually, cured, while the disease has been checked in several whose bodies are partially gone.</p>
        <p>HEADACHE and other ills of overcrowded rooms are found by Dr. Paul, of Breslau, to be due to retention of the heat by the body, even 15 per cent, of carbon dioxide cluing no harp in a regulated temperature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SIMPLE HYGROMETER.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE HYGROMETER.</p>
        <p>An interesting hygrometer (an instrument for measuring Atmospheric moisture) is made by dipping a strip of calico in a solution of 1 part of cobalt •chloride, 75 of nickel oxide, and 20 oi gelatine, in 200 of 'water. The strip is green in fine- weather, fading as moisture appears.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LUMINOUS MICROBES.</head>
        <p>LUMINOUS MICROBES.</p>
        <p>The luminosity of certain tnarine microbes of the 13altic has been proven by M. Tarchanoff, a Russian physiologist, to depend on respiration and oxygen absorption. On injecting a culture of the microbes into the blood of a number of frogs, the latter, supplying the necessary oxygen, were made luminous at night.</p>
        <p>LIQUID AIR AND SEED GERMINATION. In a memoir read before the Academie 'des Sciences, Al.. Paul Becquerel has published the results of his investigations on the action of cold on seeds, making use of liquid air. The interest- ing conclusion is reached that the resistance of seeds at low temperatures depends on the quan- tity of water and gas contained. If the quantity is sufficient, the cold disorganises the protoplasm and nucleus and renders all return of life impos- sible. But if the protoplasm has already reached by desiccation its maximum of concentration, or maximum of activity, it escapes the influence of low temperatures, and the seed preserves its ger- minating power.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>RECESSION OF NIAGARA.</head>
        <p>THE RECESSION OF NIAGARA.</p>
        <p>Horseshoe Fall has receded more than 260 ft. within the memory of living men, and is travel- ling towards Lake Erie at the rate of fully 500 ft. a century. At present, says the Scientific Ameri- can, the crest of this fall, as its name applies, has a general outline of a horseshoe, and its length is about 3,000 ft., but if the present rate of erosion continues the length of the crest may reach B,oooft. or more within the next half mil- lennium. Now tho Canadian end of the Horse- shoe Fall is a few rods upstream from the spot whence Table Rock has fallen into the Gorge, but the indications are that this end of the cataract will gradually retire towards the Duf- fern): Islands, leaving a bare cliff as the apex cuts its way upstream.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>POISONOUS EFFECTS OF COFFEE.</head>
        <p>POISONOUS EFFECTS OF COFFEE.</p>
        <p>Professor Boltenstern, of Berlin, has been de- nouncing the use of coffee before a Congress of German doctors sitting at Moran. According to the German savant even the moderate regular drinking of coffee works injury in manyindi- vidual cases. The most active constituent part of coffee, he says, is caffein. But caffein is a poison particularly irritant to the nerves and the heart. Strong coffee is alwoys injurious, and its constant use leads to all kinds of diseases. The view that coffee is a nutriment is entirely false. It contains just as little nutrition as alcohol. Owing to its poisonous effect, coffee is not suitable for everybody. Invalids, weak or nervous persons, and those— suffering from heart affections ought never to drink cy-iffee. Nor should it over -be given to children. Jor healthy adults its use need not be absolutely forbidden, but in. all cases it should be taken moderately.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>NEW SMOKE-PREVENTION DEVICE.</head>
        <p>A NEW SMOKE-PREVENTION DEVICE.</p>
        <p>A now smoke-prevention device for boiler fur- naces has been invented by Mr. J. S. Pearson, of Glasgow. ' The system eonsists of discharging a combination of steam, air, and producer gas into the furnace. The three elements are com- bined and discharged on to the fuel in front of the furnace through nozzles fitted to short pipe connections. The resulting chemical action re- leases the hydrogen in the steam, and combines the oxygen with the carbon in the fuel. The de- composition of the supplied gases is thus com- pleted, and, by combining - with the fuel gases and the resulting new gases thus produced, creates great heating power, emitting heavy smoke. The latter, however, decreases in volume towards/ the tubes, in which there are only flames, and is completely consumed- before it reaches the chimney. The steam pressure does not vary with the stoking or cleaning of the furnace, and no 'ashes or clinker§ are formed. The system can bo applied to any type of boiler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TIIE EYELIDS AFTER DEATH.</head>
        <p>TIIE EYELIDS AFTER DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Paris Medical Congress some time ago arrived at the conclusion ' that' in most cases 'death occurs with the eyes and the mouth open. This is certainly so, the Lancet tells us, in cases of suffocation and in sudden deaths, and the fact is often employed to add a ghastly detail to the narration of the event. Where, however, the normal last sleep is continuous with its " twin- brother " death, or where terminal coma super- venes, the eyelids occasionally remain closed. It appears that the tissues of the eyelids are among the first portions of the corpse to undergo death-stiffening. Criminals have occasionally been frightened at the open-eyed appearance of their dead victims, and with thoughtful ignor- ance have closed the eyelids, imagining that by so doing a suspicious clue is avoided; as a. matter of fact, they are affording strong evi- dence against the apparent suicide which they wished the corpse to simulate. Ordinarily, shortly after death the eye exhibits a glazed, lustreless look, without the normal vital reflec- tion, and the cornea is dull, When the eyelids remain unclosed the, sclerotic is exposed to the air, and the moisture in the superficial tissue evaporates. Within a few hours a dark oval stain develops on the temporal side of each globe, a• condition which is absent when the lid's are closed shortly after death. This appearance, which is known as Larcher'S sign, is of medico- legal interest, as the stain will be conspicuous even if the lids have been closed some hours after the death occurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>AQUAMETER.</head>
        <p>THE AQUAMETER.</p>
        <p>An apparatus, the " aquameter," has been de- vised for assisting in the compilation of weather forecasts. In such work it is pointed out that a very important factor is not taken sufficiently into consideration. This is the exactitude of the percentage of aqueous vapour in the approach- ing winds. The barometer gives some such indi- cation. but the height of the barometer depends on wind pressure and temperature as well as on moisture. The wet and dry-bulb thermo- meters constitute ,an antiquated instrument and are not sufficiently reliable, as their variation de- pemli on erratic circumstances, and their indica- tions are not represented in actual percentages of aqueous vapour. Rain results when an atmosphere nearly saturated with aqueous vapour becomes lowered in temperature. The nearness or otherwise of a wind to its saturation point, is, therefore, a most important question. It has had to be determined hitherto by re- course to elaborate apparatus, including a chemical balance, and, therefore, out of the usual province of a meteorologist. By using the aquaxneter, however, which is a simple instru- ment, the exact percentage of aqueous vapour, says the Srientifie American, can be obtained. By: the opening and shutting of two taps and the raising and lowering of a mercury reservoir, a measured quantity of air is drawn into a glass vessel and placed in contact with anhydrous phosphoric acid, which is a rapid water ab- sorbent. The rise of mercury in the narrow glass stem of the vessel then gives the exact percent- age of the aqueous vapour in the air.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RADIUM AND SUN-HEAT.</head>
        <p>RADIUM AND SUN-HEAT.</p>
        <p>'Radium has been eagerly seized upon as a possible means of reconciliation between physicists on the one hand, and:. geologists and biologists on the other, on the great question of the ages of the earth and SIM. According to the calcula- tions of the former, based on the rate at which the sun is losing its heat, solar history had to be compressed into a period of twenty million years. The latter demanded a much longer time. It has been pointed out, however, that if radium exists in any considerable quantity, in the sun its heat- giving power may ,almost indefinitely pc&gt; longed. Radium, Professor Dar Win points out, is, perhaps, millions Of times more powerful than dynamite. It has been estimated that loz. of this element contains enough energy to raise 10,000 tons ono mile above the earth. Now if radium exists in large quantities in the sun, and if there are natural processes occurring there by which the radio-activity is converted into hem then the life of the sun may be estimated in ,hundreds' of millions of years. It , has not, how- ever, yet been shown that radium -exists in the sun in sufficient quantity for this. although Pro- fessor Darwin suggests that being one of the heaviest of the elements it would tend to gravi- tate in greater quantity to the greater mass of that luminary. Nor has it been proved that its energy is converted into heat in any natural tray. The einanations of radium as we know thorn are not heat waves, and are only con- -veined into light by failing on a fluorerocerit streen.</p>
        <p>1 THE Australian aborigines possess such thltflt : skulls that they aro in the habit of breaking ' sticks over them as w^ A" across our knees.</p>
      </div>
