      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P.' *Rnncorn Swinton Broughton R 1 620 Rochdale H.... 0 0 0 Oldham NVarrington 3 4 18 • Barrow 0 0. 0 Leigh 1 1 5 *Widnes 0 0 0 *Halifax *Keighley 0 3 9 Huddersfield ... 0 1 3 Batley *Leeds 1 ‘, 8 Hunslet *Hull 4 2 14 Wakefield *Bradford (Played on the ground of tho Club mlirked *). Warrington v. Barrow.</p>
        <p>Warrington wore only able to place a somewhat dis- organised team in the field on Saturday to oppose Barrow, but it proved strong enough to win by a con- siderable margin. Fish and Boardman were assisting Lancashire. while such men as Isherwood, Brooks, Kenyon, Shurgars and Naylor were all missing from -various caushs. Barrow had a full team. The weather was showery and only about 3,000 spectators assembled. Teams and officials : Warrington—Hallam, back ; Dickenson, Jordan, Hockenball, and Harris, three-quarter backs ; Garside and Davies, half hacks; Preston, Harmer, Thomas Jolley, Swift, Belton, Cook, and Lunt, forwards. Barrow—Gifford, back; Fox, Mellor, Proctor, and 'Coates, three-quarter backs ; Wharton and Garside,half- backs ; 1 revreek, Craig, Tophatn,Morgan, Trescartheric, Jardine, Lloyd, and Broach, forwards. Referee, Mr. B. H. Moore, Oldham. Touch judges, Messrs. T. Critchley, Widnes, and J. Edwards, St. -Helens. The opening stages of the game showed that the Warrington men would hold the'scrummages all right, and smart play by Dickenson and Garside almost caused. the downfall of the Barrow goal. Proctor and Whar- ton changed the venue,and a series of quick exchanges between Fox, Mellor, and Proctor were ably dealt with by Hallam. Eventually the Warrington forwards asserted themselves, and a fine round of passing initia- ted by Garside led up to Harris scoring. Barrow rallied slightly, and Fox came Dear scoring, but Warrington taking up the attack again, Dickenson and Hockenhall -dribbled through for Cook to score far out, Preston landing a goal. Turning round Warrington immediately became aggressive, and after Dickenson had been pulled op near the linea subsequent penalty witnessed Preston success- fully negotiate the goal kick. Warrington co:-tinued to force the pace, and a lengthy dribble led up to Jordan :scoring a try. Near the close. Hockenhall effected a brilliant run from centre field, and also landed the goal. Result :—Warrington, 3 goals and 4 tries (18 ,points), Barrow nil. MATCH ON MONDAY. • WARRINGTON v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS</p>
        <p>The cup finalists of last year renewed acquaintance at Warrington on Monday afternoon, when the cup. holders, who were poorly represented had •to stiffer defeat by 8 points to 5 points. Dull weather pre- vailed and the crowd only numbered about 2,000. Hull's only chance was Lofthouse for Phipps, while Boardman totik Lunt's place in the Warrington puck. Teams and officials:— Warrington—Hallam, hack; Hockenhall, Dickinson, Jordan and Harris; three-quarter backs' Davies and Garside, half-backs; Preston, Boardman, :Tolley, Swift, Cook, Thomas. Harmer, and Belton, forwards. Hull Kingston Rovers—Sinclair, back ; West, Jowett, Gordan and Road, three-quarter backs; Barry and Loft' house, half backs: Starks, Windle, Gath Spackrnon, Osborne, DatCEOll Mullineaux, and Reid, forwards. Referee, Mr. 'F. Renton, Hunslet. Touch judges, Messrs. Handock, Finneorn, and Edwards, St. Heleps. The Rovers had the advantage of a good breeze in the opening half, and immediately pressed. Jowett was early prominent in opening out the game towards West, who was finally tackled by Hockenhall. Harris -relieved, and a hot raid on Sinclair's ch.rge was well checked by the Hull custodian. The Rovers' backs dis- played smartness, and after Read and West had been checked on the line. Hallam missed the ball and Spank- man scored, West failing at goal. Warrington rallied, •and Harris came near scoring, Sinclair's fine defence saving the situation. Hull pressed as a result of superior back play, Barry displaying tricky work, and combined play between the whole of the threequarter libel wit- nessed Gordon 'polled imp a yard inside. Warrington relieved, and were immediately penalised, Starks placing a fine goal from near the centre. Warrington pressed, and improved forward work was met with capable defence. Turning round, Warrington with the breeze in their favour, immediately pressed, and Harris came near scoring after clever passing. West saved the situation, and the Hull forwards, combining splendidly, forced the game to the centre. Loose work on the right by the home backs was promptly taken adrtmtage of by Jowett, and the Welshman soon showed the opposition a clean pair of heels, scoring a fine try. Starks failed at an easy place. Warrington rallied, in the concluding stages, but their efforts lacked finish. After Harris bad been pulled up near the line, Preston placed a goal. Warrington pressed vigorously, Harris scoring from Jordan's pass. Hull played a sound defensive game to the close. Result :—Hull Kingston Rovers, 8 points ; 'Warrington, 5 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Lancashire v. Cumberland.</p>
        <p>FISH'S COLLAR-BONE BROKEN.</p>
        <p>Ou Saturday representatives of the above counties ,tuet on the Wigan Club ground at Central Park, and despite the inclement weather the fixture proved a .decided attraction, the " returns " showing that there were upwards of 12,000 spectators, the receipts amount- ing to £230 -</p>
        <p>Brisk play marked the opening stages, but Little, by brilliant kicking, soon placed the Cumbrians on the „attack. W Non; James, and Fish endea ;ou red to recover</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
