        <p>MARK TWAIN says :—" I had an eight years' persistent dispute with dyspepsia, but my doctor ordered Plasmon to be added to my food, and I have had no return of it since."</p>
        <p>the lost ground, but the visiting fowards indulged in a rush, and four minutes from the start the Limcastrians were placed in the rear by a try, which was credited to Jenkinson. Little was entrusted with the kick at goal, but his effort failed. Little continued to give the advan: age to the Cumbria:as until Wilson managed to break away, and later on Lomas secured possession, and worked a splendid opening for Fish,who scampered over in rare style, and scored right against the rails. As Lomas failed to put the ball over the bar the teams were again on level terms. Unfortunately for Lancashire, the Warrington representative seriously damaged himself in making his effort, and a medical examination revealing the fact that be had broken his collar bone, Fish was assisted off the ground, and took no further part in the contest. Rees as brought out of the pack, and occupied the position on the wing, half-time was reached without any further scare. In the second portion of the game the character of the hostilities sadly deteriorated. Passing bouts were at a discount, the failure of combined movement being 'especially noticable on the Lancashire side, and, taken altogether, the contest degenerated into a scramble, in which the only redeeming feature was the brilliancy of the work of the Halifax man, Little. Result : —La nun- shire 1 try (3 points), Cumberland 1 try (3 points). Teams : Lancashire.—Poole (Broughton Rangers), back; Leytham (Wigan), Lomas (Salford), Wilson (Broughton Rangers), and Fish (Warrington) three-quarter backs ; S. James (Broughton Rangers) and Preston (Salford), half backs ; Boardman (Warrington), Cheetham (Swin- ton), Silcock (Leigh), O'Neil (Leigh), Lee (St. Helens), Rees (Salford), Trotter (Broughton Rangers), and Tryer (Wigan), forwards. Cumberland.—Little (Halifax), back; Austin (M ill om), Fletcher (Oldham), Eagers (Hunslet), and Flynn (Stria. ton), three-quarter backs ; Dixon (Oldham) and Jenkin- son (Wath Brow), half backs ; Ferguson (Oldham), Marland (Hull), Kelly (Egrernont),Winskill (Broughton Rangers), Connors (Brookland Roverst, Clampette (Millom), Pinguey (Workington), ond Oaens (Oldham), forwards. _ . .</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. F. Renton, Hunslet. Touch judges, Messrs. J. Houghton (Lancashire) and Mr. W. T. Bennett (Cumberland).</p>
        <p>JUNIOR RUGBY LEAGUE. Results of Saturday's matches : Orford 6 points, Warrington Rangers S. Orford Hornets 9 points, IVarrihgton Rovers 0. Woolston 27 points, Padgate 0. Matches for to-day : Walker's 2nd v. Orford. Orford Hornets v. Victoria Park. Padgate v. Warrington Rovers. Warrington Rangers v. Wuolston ; on the ground of the first-named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>AsSOCIATION POOTBALL.</head>
        <p>AsSOCIATION POOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Goals</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. P. For agst. Pts. Warrington Albions Oughtruigton Park 3 2 1 0 12 10 4 Stockton Heath Vulcan A.P.O. Orford Barracks Widnes St. Ambrose</p>
        <p>Castner Kellner 4 1 3 0 9 14 2 Thelwall 1 0 1 0 2 4 ,0 Lymm 0 0, 0 0 0 0 0</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. Vulcan Res. Warrington Albion Res 3 3 0 0 6 1 6 Stockton Heath Res. Warrington St. Paul's Widne:St. Ambrose lies 4 2 1 1 5 4 5</p>
        <p>Daresbury Earlestown Y.M.C.A 3 1 1 1 4 5 3 Halton A.F.C. Latchford Christ Church Widnes St. Paula 3 1 2 0 4 7 2 St. Barnabas 3 0 2 1 5 11 1 Oughtrington St. Peters Castner Kellner Res. Results of Matches for October 7th. FIRST DIVISION. Stockton Heath 2, Albions 2. Oughtrington Park 4, Thehvall 2. Castner Reaper 2, Widnes St. Ambrose 3. SECOND DIVISION. Halton A.F.C. 1, Vulcan Reserve 4. Albion Reserve 2, Stockton Heath Reserve 1. Warrington St. Paul's 2. St. Barnabas' 2. Daresbury 2, Widnes St. Paul's 0. Widnes St. Ambrose Reserve 2, Castner g.elluer. Reserve 0. Played on the ground of the first-named. Matches for October 11th. Lymm v. Stockton Heath. St. Ambrose v. Orford Barracks. Daresbury v. Halton. Earlestown Y.M.C.A. v. St. Ambrose Res. Stockton Heath Res. v. Warrington St. Pauls. Widnes St. Paul's v. Christ Church. St. Barnabas v. Albion Res.</p>
        <p>;:, • e.vo, • , . s: "FOR five months," said Miss Field, of Angmering, near Worthing, Sussex, to a "West Sussex Times" reporter, "I was very bad with anlemia. My blood rapidly became thinner and weaker, and the colour of my skin was a pale slate colour. My chest ached after the slightest exertion; my breath became short; my legs ached, and I was alto- gether so weak that I often fainted. I lost appetite, and could not reach the top of the stairs without sitting on the steps to recover my breath when half-way up. I took medical advice, and sought everything I could think of to get cured of the AN/EM IA but only became weaker. My sister, who had suffered in the same way, had been cured by Bile Beans, and on her advice I a last tried them. The result was that my appetite soon returned. Then I grew stronger, and my colour slowly but surely returned, and after some weeks was as well as ever I was. Within the last week I walked from Angmer- ing to Littlehampton and back, a distance of eight miles, and did not feel the least fati: IPILE fib a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 14, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 14, 1905.</p>
        <p>tnat young man, mentally reviewing biS recent sins and more glaring Indiscretions. • "Oh, no, it's all right; it's not you this time." she reassured him laughing. t.! though I have heard one or two rumours, I must admit. What do you think of that ? " ,she asked, signifying Ben Nevis and Bina with a forward jerk of, her prayer-book. " What ? " asked Charlie blankly. "Oh, you goose, can't you see what's hap- pening under, your very nose ? " said Lady Grizelle, laughing. "What, you don't mean Ben and Bina ? " exclaimed Charliein unbounded surprise. "Yes, of course, 'I do, stupid • answered his aunt in elation. Charlie whistled. "Well,- I'm hanged, I mean I I nearly forgot it was Sunday I But what will the duchess say? " " She ought to be quite satisfied," said Lady Grizelle stiffly. "He is a man of ex- cellent family, and Bina will have a big enough fortune to make it unnecessary that she should marry money. Besides, he's a splendid fellow ; look at his record." "Rather," agreed Belsiie enthusiastically ; he's a ripper, and I personally should be delighted. He's the only chapl -know out of all her hangers-on who is worthy of dear old Bina."</p>
        <p>Lady Grizelle warmed under the glow of Charlie's enthusiasm. "I'm so glad to hear yon Teak like that, my dear boy, as I know how devoted you are to your sister. Her, happiness must be the first consideration in her ma rrit.ge, even</p>
        <p>though a king were tc lay his croWn. at her feet. You can take it from me that it won't be many days, perhaps only hours, before this great and obvious secret is confided to you and me With blushes from Pins and protesta- tions of his unworthiness from Ben. After we have given them our blessing and our consent, they'll have to face the duchess and the music 1 Your poor father will be called upon to play the heavy parent—n role he particularly abhors—and we shall see a tussle of strong characters and similar dis- positions." Charlie laughed. That's their business, not ours ; but we must see what we can do to give them a leg over the matrimonial stile. But fancy old Bina 'tuck on a love-limed twig at last Wc'op,ot so accustomed to her refusing man man, eligible ,or ineligible, that I had began o put her down as not of the marrying sort .</p>
        <p>"II you are' a true woman," • said Lady Grireile softly, letting her mind wander back to the days then she was young, " you have to wait till the right man comes along, and if he doesn't, well—you • just have ~to go. with- out ! " "My dear aunt," protested Charlie im- pertinently, " don't .give way to maudlin sentiment at your time of life I'; • " Here we are at the church," said his aunt, a little more sharply than usual, "So I perceive," Said Charlis, glmicing at her with a touch of curiosity " and here are our potential love-birds in the porch " "We walked fast because it was so cold," explained Bina, making an unnecessary ex- cuse.</p>
        <p>She could not exactly understand the warm squeeze which Charlie gave her arm as they filed into the old-fashioned pew, but ,it was pleasing and comforting in its suggestion of brotherly love. And she wondered why all through the service he sat thoughtfully kick- ing at a straw hassock on the floor and attending to his devotions mechanically. The rest of the clay was passed very pleasantly, and Charlie, gloating over his fact, preferred to smoke a big cigar after lunch to skating immediately. " You two young people run on," he said " and I'll join you in an hour. I have to look after my digestion ; and, besides, all really religious people snooze on Sunday afternoons." • Within the hour his self-abnegation yielded to a desire for company and skating,- and, all unconscious of his inopportune arrival, he came upon the scene just as Ben Nevis had screwed himself up to take the most important and unnerving step in a man's life. Baffled after all the tortures of anticipation and foiled of any further opportunity that day, Ben Nevis went to bed with his mind made up. The next morning, come what might, he would try his luck in the matri- monial lottery. If he drew a blank he would know the worst and could, at least go abroad again. If the winning number the thought made even his seasoned nerves tingle and his heart beat like a drum and fife band---,he would be the happiest man in the world, but he revolttd mentally against the utter inadequacy of the conventional and hackneyed phrase. It seem«l to suggest that he would only be one of an er ortnotts number of " happiest men on earth," wheteas he felt that it would place him on a plane. apart. She seemed to like him, and he had seen no traces of a heartless flirt .er -shallow society syren in the girl. On the contrary, he had found her natural and sincere, warm-hearted and un- affected, despite rumours and club gossip. If she had refused so many men it was only because she did not love them, and they had been crass enough in forra themselves upon her without encouragement. Had she en- couraged him, he asked himself ; but it was a question which after careful introspection he felt he could not answer with candour or certainty. She had always treated him with a frank friendliness and, perhaps, partiality ; but he said to lthaself that the ingrained conceit of a man to often led him to misinter- pret things. There was his comparative poverty and her great position, but that he felt would weigh little with her, even though it might with her parents. That he knew he could face, if she only gave him the right to. So he got into bed determined to run the risk adding • another scalp to Bina's already of overcrowded wigwam. However.' moan may propOste to propose, but Providence by the aid of telegraph wires often disposes summarily of us and our best laid plans.. Love, unless particularly young and strong in its growth, does pot as a rule flourish at breakfast time—in the male sex, at any rate. But Ben proved an exception the ,following morning, as he turned up to an early break- fast, full of determinatihn and hope. Bina was already in the breakfast-room, looking very charming in a plain coat aml skirt of blue cloth trimmed with white fur. " Ready to skate, you see," she said, greet- ing him brightly. " Aunt will be down in a minute; but Charlie, as usual, is lazy and sleeping in a bit. 1 looked round his door on my way down to call him, but was rewarded for my sisterly devotion by being fold in a sleepy voice to go and boil myself! " " Brothers are inure forcible than polite at times," said Ben, retaining her hand unduly long, as though in a fit of absent-mindedness. She drew it away rather sharply and turned round to bide a blush. " I wonder he did not say something even more poillted," she said to cover her confusion. At that moment Lady Grizelle entered. "Good children," she said, "to be so punctual I I wonder if it's all due to good manners, or whether behind it is a plot to. go skating again ? " They both laughed, as they greeted her, secretly pleased to be coupled so familiarly. Breakfast was a merry meal, and Ben Nevis belied the supposed habits of a man in love by eating very heartily. It was half-past nine before they rose from the\table. "Run along, you two, to your skating," said the complacent chaperone, dismissing them. " I'll stroll down later-and watch 'you, when that lazy Charlie puts in appearance." At that moment the butler entered with a telegram on a salver. "For Lady Sabina," he said, taking it to</p>
        <p>her. Bina tore it open, and a look of disap- pointment crossed her face. "What a nuisance 1" she exclaimed. "What's wrong, my dear ? " enquired her aunt anxiously. " It's from mother," explained Bina, '' and says, Return by earliest possible train this morning ; want you particularly. Mother.'" Ben muttered something inaudible under</p>
        <p>ms oreatn.</p>
        <p>"How annoying f" said Lady Grizelle. "That means you'll have to catch the 10.45, so we may as well all go up together. Go and pull Charlie out of bed, dear, while I order the carriage. I wonder what your mother is so urgent about at eight o'clock on a Monday morning ? "</p>
        <p>So 13-?si was left to smoke II solitary pipe and curse his luck, while the ladles attended to their packing. Twice he had worked himself up to the point, and both times he had been frustrated by circumstances. However, he soon made up his mind to accept the inevitable philoso- phically and hope for better luck the third time, though it is always hard for a man to be kept on tenterhooks when he is in love. After thirty," Ben murmured, thinking ruefully of his advancing years, "every day is of importance." At Waterloo he got his first chance of a word alone with Bina, as he insisted upon carrying an armful of wraps despite a waiting footman. . _</p>
        <p>" Shall I see you at Lady Swanage's dance to-night ? " he asked. eagerly.</p>
        <p>Bina nodded acquiescence. . "If not before ? " he added tentatively ; but' the girl refused to be drawn by his hint. "Will you keep me some dances ? " he asked, returning to the attack in the teeth of the rebuff.</p>
        <p>"How many? " she asked in her turn, smiling tantalisingly. "Tea or twenty !" he said impetuously with a new-born familiarity. Then he broke into a laugh at his own absurdity. "Greedy man!" said • Bina non-corn- rnittally, but she smiled back to him encourag- ingly, as the carriage drove away.</p>
        <p>One of the greatest factors for comfort in the house is to have the needful work done in the best manner and in the shortest time.</p>
        <p>There is no help to compare with Fels-Naptha soap towards furthering these ends.</p>
        <p>That is if . rightly used : not as other soaps are used, but in an easier way fully explained in the simple directions with every bar. With Fels-Naptha soap</p>
        <p>don't use any clothes-rotting</p>
        <p>chemicals nor skin destroying powders. Let Fels-Naptha cleanse</p>
        <p>a:id purify in the comfort- able, easy, rapid and absolutely safe way directed. Fcla-Naptha x; Wilson street London EC ,</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.' OPTIMISM, like pessimism, is largely a question of digestion. No WOMAN who has the most clothes in a neigh- bourhood can ever be really popular in it. IN EXPLANATION.—Ho: "I have only the most distent relatives." Erie " Has the family died out ?" : " No ; they have all become rich." " WHAT are pauses ? ' asked the school teacher. " Fing4 what grows on cateee," replied the boy at the wrong end of the class. GOVERNESS : " C 0111.3, Ethel ; it's time for good little girls to be in bed." Ethel : " Ytl, laith Murree ; hut you know I have been naughty t0(63,21 (63,21 " He says he likes to talk to his wife over the te!ephone.i' "I wonder why ?" "On, that's the only time he can shut her off when he wants to."</p>
        <p>"Tura coffee," remarked the boarder, "18 like the quality of mercy." "Yes ?" queried the land- lady, with a show of interest. "Yes ; Ws not strained." WOMAN'S WAY.—Ted "Ours was a secret engagement." Ned: "I suppose that Is why the girl didn't ktlep it?" OF ONE Mizto —Magistrate: "I see you are here again. I believe you have been tried and convicted seven times for stealing." Prisoner: " Yes, it seems to be nothin' but temptations and trials with rue in this life."</p>
        <p>TUE ruling instinct is strong in a thrifty house- wife. "My dear," said the husbsol, who had to toll his wits that he had failed, " the wolf is at the door." "Tell him to wipe his feet," said she, absently. A FAIR EXCEIANGE.—" I wish I had your head," said a young lady to a literary in‘n who had solved a problem for her. "And I wish I had your heart," responded the gentleman. They subsequently formed a partnership. "Sorross you succeed in owning the earth,', said the abrupt man, "what good will it do you I" " Well," answered the trust promoter, "I'm natu- rally a man c.f hospitable insticcis. There will be a kind of satisfactfon in fetling that I am permit- ting other people to inhabit this globe." "I DON'T dClibt that the readin',.f it's all right," said the customer, " but it ain't the hrok Ire' lookin' for—the cover don't suit me." "My dear sir," said the bookseller, "will you kindly describe just the book you're after ?" "Wlll, to be plain with you, I want a book that will do to put in a handsome new house."</p>
        <p>OFF OF CURIOSITY. —Prigoner : "Mv lswyer is ill. 1 am!: for a fortuight'a delay." I\lqtrietfate : "Put your case off for a fortuight ? Why, ycu rascal ! you were caupht with yrur hand hi a gentleman's pocket. What could your lawyer slay ?" Prisoner: "That's what I'm waiting to know."</p>
        <p>HE (after popping the question) : "Why do you cry: my angel ? Did I offond you by my pro- posal ? ' She (still sobbing) : "Oh, no, dear, it is not that. I am crying for joy. Mother always said to me, May, she said, you are such an Idiot that you would not get even a donkey for a low.r.' and no.w I 6'. VEI oat one sat.ar ANSWERED —" What would you 110 it you can a million pounds?" asked the dreamer. "Oh'" answered the philosophic citiz-n, "I suppose I'd be like the rest of humanity. If I were worth a million I'd probably worry mvrolf into dyspepsia trying to make it two million.." FROM FORCE OF HABIT,—Mrs. Hopper had been</p>
        <p>matching dress goods all the morning, qu'zz'ng the shopmen as to colour, texture, awl dw:sbility. Suddenly she bethought herself of a special sale In the toilet-article department awl hastened there. "Some soap," she said to the assistant. Carefully examining the dainty box and its contents, she asked, "It-e you sure this will wash?" A LADY from the country recently entered a bric-a-brac shop in town with a view to purchasing something odd, and, looking about, noticed a quaint figure, the head and shoulX.rs of which appeared above the counter. " What is that Japanese idol over there worth ?" she inquired, The shopman's reply was given in a subdued tone: "Worth about twenty thousand pounds, madam— it's the proprietor !" THOROUGH.—Mrs. Young: "A friend has sent MB a basket of quinces, and I don't know how to use them." Mrs. Old : " Nothing is simpler. Pare and core them, m tke preserves of the beet pieces, marmalade of the others, and jelly of the cores and peelings." Mrs. Young: "Indeed ! And what shall I make of the basket they came in?" . .</p>
        <p>A TRAcKEP. !eci.ntly gave hie pupila a lecture on patriotism. Ho p6intv,d out the high motives which move.l the volunteers to leave their homes and fight ft,r their cruntry. The Ec h 00l to.,cher noticed that one boy did not pay attention to the instruction, and as a teat question he asked him : " What motives took the volunteers to the war?" The boy was ponied f,r a moment, then retnem• bering the public " send,off " of the local reserves and volunteers at the railway station, he replied, "Loonnaotives, sir." -</p>
        <p>Tait teacher had been explaining what was meant by dniandam bullets. "Now, Tuniny, what bullet do you consider the deadliest ?" " The one that hits, sir." Another teacher dealing with domestirt economy had been inculcating cleanliness, the necessity for removal of dust anti of killiog vermin. "How can moths that destroy clothes be killed? eke asked. "Hang them on a line and beat them with a hesvy stick," was the answer received. "But will that kill the insects?" was the next 'question. "Yes, ma'am, If you hit them." A New South Wales country school teacher re- cently gave a boy a question in compound propr- tion for home work, which happened to include the circumstance of "men working ten hours a day in order to complete a certain work." Next morning the unsuspecting teacher, in looking over the little prtk of exercises, tlund "dined " sum on- attempted, and the following letter enclosed in the page :—" Sur : I refuse to let -.lnn do his sum you give him last nite has it looks to me to be a slur at 8 burs sista= enuy sum not more than 8 burs he is welcum to do but no more. Yours truly, ABRAM BLANK &amp;NM" A LITTLIG GIRL, who has just entered school, jubilantly antioubced to her father that she had beaten all the girls above her in the arithmetic class and gone to the top. "That was clevsr of you," said he, eecouragingly. " How was it?" "Well, you see, the tJacher asked the girl at the head how much was tight arid five, and she didn't know, and said twelve ; and the next girl said nine, and the next OLIO said eleven, and the next one said fourteen. Such silly answers! Then the teacher asked rue, and I said thirteen, and she told me to go top. 'Course it was thirteen." "That was nioe," Sail the father. "I didn't think you could add so wen. How did you know it was thirteen?" " Why, I guetsed it. Nobody said thirteen." Tun meter who gives his pupils " simple ruler " for determining questions which confront them, and particularly grammatical questions, is apt to find that ruch mules frequently disastrously fail to fit all eases. 0 toe an exrtniner was question- ing the pupils of a country school. Hi wrote on the blackboard the sentence, " The fly has wings," and asked a class what part of speech each word was. They parsed the " the" without any tronble. " What part of speech is ' fie ? " " Arl verb," shouted all the class in unison. " What I Fly an adverb?" " Yes, sir " shouted the boys with great positi‘enrst. " What makes you think it is an erlverb ?" "'Clause the master told us last week that all words that end in • ly ' are adverbs." Six new pupils had been enrolled on the infants' register one morning. The mistress, on looking over the roll, called the six names, and asked these children to come to her desk. ody five arranged themselves on the floor, and after succeeding in identifying each with his name, she found Richard Brown was the one not responded to. "Richard Brown', come here." No one moved. "Richard Brown" again was vociferated by the mistress. No suck person evidently was among the number of two hundred children. "sit straight up and let me look at all your faces," she said. On detecting a strange little one among the number the somewhat decidedly said : "Little man, your same is Richard Brown, is it not?" No," replied the child. "I'm Dicky Broon."</p>
