      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>THE...</head>
        <p>THE...</p>
        <p>WHITE ROSE.</p>
        <p>By GERALD BISS..</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVII.</p>
        <p>LADY GRIZELLE mustered her small house. party in good time on Saturday morning, Sc that the afternoon should be free for skating. Charlie again played truant, promising to arrive in time for dinner; but his absence was taken very complacently. iLady Grizellp settled herself cosily in a corner with a bundle of papers, ordering Bina and Ben Nevis into the opposite corner, so as not to disturb her with their conversation. They did not seen to miss Charlie very keenly, and Lady Grizelle watched them with considerable interest and amusement over the top of the "Times," obli- vious temporarily of leaders and debates. " It's what housemaids call ' a case,' " she murmured, re-adjusting her gold pince-nez with a well satisfied grunt. "Won't her dear mother be annoyed I"</p>
        <p>The Duchess of Hampstead and her sister- in-law had never hit it off too well, and the last quarter of a century had been a succession of metaphorical sparring matches between them —first one claiming a victory and then the other. This Lady Grizelle felt would be a veritable Waterloo to her score ; and, besides, she liked Ben Nevis particularly, and was devoted to Bina.</p>
        <p>"This is a one-eyed little place, Lord Ben Nevis," she said to her new guest at lunch. " By' the way, I think I shall call you ' Ben.' We always used to call your father Ben,' and when you put in an appearance it was always ' Big Ben and 'Little Ben.' " "Do by all means," said the earl with a laugh. "I was always Ben ',at Eton and in the regiment. It's nice and omfortable and friendly to have a nickname." • " All right," said Lady Grizelle ; " Ben ' it shall be. But to resume—there's nothing to do, except skate and Ow billiards, with a possible•game of bridge after dinner to please an old woman and pander to her vitiated tastes. You'll have to go to church to- morrow, as aF, 11 to Gerberus but you can skate afterwards . and all the afternoon. There's a nice discreet little pond in the grounds well out ef sight, where you won't df•moralise the villagers. You have to be very particular about these details in the country, or the yokels would soon get quite out of hand." • Ben Nevis laughed. "It sounds very jolly, and it's just what I'm longing for, I don't know how many years it is since I spent a week-end in the country like this, and it makes me feel quite young again." "Are you so fearfully old, Lord Ben Nevis ? " asked Bina demurely. " " I'm afraid I am," he answered half jestingly, half sadly. " But don't let us talk about it ; let's go and skate." And while they skated Lady Grizelle, the best of chaperones, dozed cosily in front of her bedroom fire, dreaming vague, delicious dreams about nothing in particular. A very good lunch and a comparatively good con- science insure the middle-aged against insomnia in the day-time. Meanwhile Bina and Ben Nevis skated to their heart's content. Next to dancing, there is no socially permitted form of amusement more conducive to intimacy than skating. For mere flirtation it is an admirable vehicle : and if oneof the two, necessary to a quarrel or a kiss, does not suffer from an insane desire to cut figures and scratch "eight's "upon the ice, for the more serious aspects of love-making nothing better could be chosen with due re- gard to the season of the year and the time of day. Thus Bina. and Ben were as happy as it is given to mortals to be: and the former was as pleased as the latter, if less candidly so, at the absence jof Charlie. When two people are skating together it is natural and within the bounds of .the strictest propriety to clasp hands, even both hands simultaneously: and Ben ingenuously tool: full advantage of thit sporting right in the social preserves without meeting with any rebuff from Bina, although she was calculated to be eminently able to look after herself. Darkness and tea-time came all too soon: and Ben Nevis was slow and clumsy in taking off Bina's skates, desirous of prolonging the pleasure of the afternoon. "Hasn't it been lovely ? " said the girl with glowing cheeks, they strolled back to the house across the miniature park. "Splendid," replied the man enthusiasti- cally; " I don't know when I have enjoyed anything so much for years. It's grand to get back to England and ice and—and home." He laid a little stress upon the last word in a way which appealed to the girl —;." How horribly lonely you must have been t.. 1: times," she said impulsively—" right away from everybody and everything.', "I was," he admitted, "but I was happy in my own queer way. But I'm glad to be . . . . . .</p>
        <p>back-again riow, thoug-h I didn't think at first that I should like it. Do you know I actually dreaded London and people and dress-clothes and civilisation ? " They both laughed happily and irrespon- sibly.</p>
        <p>"'But now you are home and have got over your dread of dinners and dances, you won't go back, will you ? " asked Bina, with ar un- conscious note of softness in her voice. "No, I shall certainly not go back," said the man with decision, as he regretfully rang the friput-door bell, " unless-. No, I mean I shan't go back. I've done with wandering about the world and mean to settle down." Their eyes met and dropped: and the butler :lame to the rescue by opening the door. Between tea and Charlie's arrival Lady Grizelle thoughtfully allowed them another hour alone on the threadbare excuse of having letters to write: but they were not inclined to be critical of the validity of her reason. It was only too pleasant to be left to themselves in the existing state of their feelings, and even Lady Grizelle was de trop. Then, all too soon, Charlie arrived like a whirlwind, banishing sentiment and solitude. With the manners of a brother he rudely pulled Bina off the pedestal to which Ben Nevis had elevated her, and translated her to the marking-board. " Come and mark for Ben and myself. --- girl, while we have a game of pills," he semi dragging her off the couch with an affectionate roughness. "You'll begin to grow fat and lazy if you sit curled up in front of the fire too long, like an old tabby cat." "I'm not lazy, Charlie dear," she answered, kissing him with a heartiness which aroused in the other man all his worst feelings of un- diluted envy ; "we've been skating all the afternoon like mad things, haven't we, Lord Ben Nevis ? " " Rather," he replied, answering her smile. " I haven't had so much exercise since I got back to England." Then he added speciously "You 'ought to have . been there, Charlie: it was splendid." "It's all very well for you young things to frolic in the country," said Belsize, laughipg ; "but I had more important business. In an idle moment last week I invite a dowager neaAy three times my age to a tete-a-tete lunch at Prince's, and she didn't give me a shadow of a chance of getting out of it." . _ _</p>
        <p>'Poor old boy,' sal Bina laughing, " you sha have a game of billiards and a whisky and soda to make up." What between billiards, dinner, palate billiards, music, and bridge, Ben Nevis and Bina did not have a chance of exchanging a word alone until on the way to church next morning. They walked across the park to the little Norman church: ..nd unconsciously and naturally- they paired off in front of Lady Grizelle and Charlie. In fact, it was partly the former's doing, as she took possession of the volatile Charlie and gradually dropped behind on the plea that she was growing tpe stout to walk so fast. •04 "I want to talk to you, Charlie," she began: " Nothing wrong. I hone. aunt ? " asked</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CHAPTER XVIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XVIII.</p>
        <p>BTNA made her way straight up to hei mother's boudoir. She found the duchess at her escritoire writing busily. "Well, mother," she said, as she kissed her, "what is the meaning of these urgent early 'morning wires, when a poor girl is taking a few hours' quiet in the country ? " " I was sorry to disturb you, dear," replied her mother, returning her kiss affectionately ; "but your father insisted. A visitor arrived this morning to stay a few days, and we wanted you at home for his visit." " Oh," said Dina, pursing her lips and pre- paring to be prejudiced ; " and who, pray, is this important personage in whose honour my presence is so urgently demanded ? " " Don't be silly, dear," said the duchess laughing lightly in an endeavour to minimise the significance ,of the situation. " He's not a 'particularly important person only a young roan who, has come to your father with unusually cordial letters of introduction from the Due d'Aurignac and Due de l'Oriol, two of his oldest friends, as you know." " What is his name ? " asked Bina, with a touch of curiosity. "Stuart," answered her mother. "He is</p>
        <p>of Scottish al?ccstry, but has lived nearly all his life abroad. However, he speaks perfect English and seems a most charming young man."</p>
        <p>The whole thing struck Bina as strange and Unusual, especially in her mother, who was notoriously unapproachable and. intolerant of strangers; but .she was not of a suspicious bent of mind. "If he's nice," she said, smiling reassur- ingly to her mother, " be very sweet to him. How old is he ? " " Thirty or a little over." nice age," said Bina thoughtfully. " I hate callow youths and men too young ; they are generally either cubs or coxcombs." "My dear child," laughed the duchess, secretly pleased, "what a fearful misanthropist you are becoming—at twenty I " Bina laughed, too. " Where is this paragon ? " " He is -in the library with your father and Mr. Frazer," said the duchess, rather rashly. "Oh. Mr. Frazer ? Is he a friend of that peculiarly objectionable person ? If so, I'm afraid I shan't like him." " What a child you are for foolish pre- judices ! Why, I think Mr. Frazer a very clever and interesting man." " I never could understand you taking him</p>
        <p>up, I must confess, mother dear. He always strikes me as a bit of an adventurer with a good dash of cunning in his disposition." "There you are again, dear," expostulated her mother. "I don't suppose you have spoken a dozen words to him in your life." "No," admitted Bina candidly, "I don't suppose I have, nor do I intend to, if I can help it without being positively rude. But I forgot," she added, "he's on your committee." " Surely if I receive him, my dear Bina, you can atior(i to he civil to him ? " protested the duchess in a half-chaffing tone, which covered a certain internal anxiety. "It is part of every well-bred person's daily life to dis- simulate their dislikes. What would society be without ? " "Forgive me. mother dear," said the girl contritely, kissing her warmly. " I'm afraid I give you a lot of bother and anxiety with my waywardness and moods at times." She felt in an unusually gentle mood that morning. Her mother patted her hand soothingly. "No, dear, you know I'm very, very proud of you and your success," she answered lovingly. " Now 'run away and dress your- • self for lunch." Bina turned away obediently, but at the door she paused an instant. " Mother, what is this mysterious com- mittee of yours ? " she asked impetuously. • "People are beginning to ask, and I haven't the faintest idea." A frown flitted across her mother's face. " It's something very harmless, dear," she answered in a light tone. "You will know soon enough; so don't worry yourself--or me I" Without another word Bina left the room, puzzled and unaccountably ill at ease. She bated mysteries, even simple little ones ! As the door closed behind her, a sigb escaped the duchess.</p>
        <p>'To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
