      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>OCTOBER 14th 1905.</p>
        <p>One of Mr. CHAMBER- THE PROSPERITY OF LAIN's main theses is ENGLAND AND that we are being GERMANY. beaten in our corn-</p>
        <p>petition for the world's trade by a strongly Protectionist nation like Germany. The argument is not to be effectively met by a simple denial. Germany has of late years made tremendous efforts to compete with us, not only in foreign markets but in vur own ; and to some not inconsiderable extent she has com- peted with us successfully. Her trade has not outstripped our own, as Mr. CHAMBERLAIN would suggest, but it has in proportion to its total extent expanded more rapidly than ours. A trades- man who is doing an annual business which can be expressel in four figures will, if he is reasonably enterprising, be likely to increase his dealings at a greater proportionate rate than a tradesman whose yearly turnover must be expressed in six figures. It is much more difficult to increase the area of large transactions I than of small ones. The new ground to be covered is immensely greater in the one case than in the other. Germany is a comparatively new competitor, and she exhibits great energy and business aptitude. There is no likelihood that she will overtake us; but undoubtedly she is doing her best, and is making headway. It would be foolish to deny facts so obvious as this. There are quite half a hundred explanations, all more or less sound, of the increase of the foreign trade of Germany, and they are all in conflict with the explanation of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. The best, most convincing, and the most practical of the whole series is to be found in a supplement ta the latest number of the "Statist," a financial publication which commended the hearty respect of the greatest Fiscal statesman of modern times— Mr. GLADSTONE, to wit.</p>
        <p>The " Statist " publishes a 13tter from Mr EDGAR SPEYER, an eminent banker, summarising and reflecting on a report which he has received from Professor PAUL ARNDT, of Frankfort-on- Main, concerning the extent to which of late years Germany has been investing capital abroad. The conviction of Mr. CkIAMBERLAIN, and the still deeper conviction of his disciple, Mr. RICHARD SEDDON, is that any excess of our imports over our exports signifies that our own industries are being ruined. Mr. SEDDON holds the simple faith that we pay for the excess by the exportation of minted sovereigns. This is a view of the case that need not be discussed. It is the error of an astoundingly innocent student of economics. The question of how far Mr. CLIAMBEILLA.IN'S less extravagant and childlike views are sound may be profitably studied in the light of Mr. SPEYER'S letter on the important document supplied to him by Professor ARNDT. It is disastrous, or in any way significent of declining trade, for our imports, to exceed our exports in an ascending scale? We have again and again explained, perhaps needlessly, that the excess represents tbe income on our foreign investments, the wages and earnings of our carrying trade, and the profits of our dealings with the foreigner. Now, the precise point of Mr. SPEYER'S letter and of Professor ARNDT's report is that for ten years past we have been restricting our foreign investments and that</p>
        <p>Germany has been extending hers. This neces• isarily means more business for Germany. She is I paid the interest of her investments in goods. The part played by Germanys foreign investments says Mr. SPEYER, in building up the wealth of' that country and in adding to its foreign trade is enormous. In a comparatively short period, it appears, Germany has invested abroad the great sum of £1,500,000,000. Her income from this source has now risen to 700 million pounds sterling She has a total income from foreign investments. shipping, commissions, &amp;c., of £100,000,000. This ' is not much in excess of our own income from shipping alone; so that we are not yet quite reined by the progress of Germany. This truth is further illustrated by the fact that Germany's foreign investments are not yet more than half of those of this country. But Germany is investing more whilst we are investing less. That 1 is the central fact of the position. We are spending our money on ourselves in increasing quantities, and our rival is till distin- guished for thrift. Whilst this situation continues the increase of the foreign trade of Gsrrnany must merely be a thing of course.</p>
        <p>The conclusion of the whole matter is that our national prosperity, accompanied by a more free expenditure, has operated injuriously on our transactions with foreign countries. Mr. SPEYER, by way of comfort, says that doubtless the rule that prosperous people usually become more lavish in their expenditure will also apply to Germany. In fact, he adds, there are already signs that the country is beginning to consume at home a larger portion of its growing wealth. If this tendency to spend more at home in order to live in greater comfort develops in Germany at the same time that Great Britain becomes more economical, Great Britain will again invest abroad the more largely, and Great Britain's export trade and income from her foreign investments will grow more rapidly than Germany's. This will seem to be a proposition of the incontrovertible kind to everybody but members of the Tariff Reform League, who will, by the way, find little comfort in Mr. SPEYER'S concluding remark that when a new spirit of economy and up-to-dateness animates Englishmen we shall hear less grumbling about the volume of British export trade, the con- dition of the labour market, the economic state of the country, or the growth of the foreign trade and prosperity of other countries.</p>
        <p>Quite the most THE MAYOR AND THE remarkable of a Courrcu. somewhat ordinary lot of post-prandial orations at the Mayoral dinner on Wednesday, was that of the Mayor himself in replying to the toast of the Corporation of Warrington. The evening had got somewhat far advanced, and His Worbhip had spoken more than once which may perhaps account for his remarks taking a some- what spontaneous turn. Some excuse is certainly required for the way in which he turned upon his inoffensive councillors, all of whom, by the way, had to stand while he replied to the toast and, as one of them expressed it, be made " perfect fools of." The mental calibre of some of the town's representatives seems to •be very lowly assessed by the Mayor. They have "no ideas of business," are put in the position of managing director, and possess as much know- ledge as would fit them to " take a job as labourer." The laughter was loud and long. This may sound very funny to gentlemen whose natural good humour has been added to by the exhilarating effects of Geisler and 1847 Hock, but in cold type it does not read very well. The "dignity that doth hedge" even Mayors was missing at this stage of the proceedings, and His Worshp would have been better advised to have kept such thoughts to himself. One expects the Mayor to be jealous of the fair tame of the Council over which he has the honour to preside, and be the last man to hold it up to ridicule.</p>
        <p>The most interest- BEWSEY'S BATTLE. ing coutest at the</p>
        <p>November elections seems likely to be in Bewsey Ward where the position of affairs is decidedly peculiar. Councillor Peter Peacock is the retiring Liberal candidate,his oppornot being Mr. Edwin Plinston, the nominee of the Labour party. Last week we alluded to the anomalous position Mr. Plinston occupied owing to the fact that he was one of the official secretaries of the Bewsey Ward Liberal Committee and a member also of the Liberal executive. At the meeting of the Trade 3 Council on Tuesday. Mr. Plinston explained, with tile result admirably expressed by Shakespeare's-lines :</p>
        <p>"And, oftentimes, excusing of a fault Doth make the fault the worse by the excuse." He repudiated the Liberal party in terms which made it appear that his connection with the Liberals had been nothing more than ' nominal for some years. And yet so recently as September 15th, Mr. Plinston attended a Liberal executive meeting, made a speech to the effect that he was not a prospective Labour candidate for any ward and, was the same evening appointed on a special committee formed:for the purpose of visiting the Liberal Ward meetings and assistine in the selection of Liberal candidates. Now Mr.Plinston poses as a Labour man pure and simple. We shall be surprised if the Bewsey electors do not speedily conclude that a man who vacillates so rapidly in his political faith is hardly calculated to make them an efficient representative on the Town , Council. Apart from the blunders Mr. Plinston has personally fallen into, we regret very much that Liberal and Labour should be in direct opposition. They have everything to gain by unity, and the Labour Representation Committee is proving itself a singularly misguided body by elect!ng to oppose the Liberals in Bewsey Ward.</p>
        <p>Lord Esher's notable CONSCRIPTION, prpouncenent against conscription co m- bined with his close intimacy with the King leads to the supposition that Royalty does not look with a favourable eye on compulsory military service as a remedy for army deficiencies. Whatever His Majesty's views may be, however, Lord Esher is entitled to speak for himself and with the weight of a highly-qualified authority. As a member of the South African War Commission, and the chairman of the committee of three which reported on the re-organisation of the War Office, he has had unrivalled opportunities of studying the whole problem of army reform. In an interview by the" Review of Reviews "Lord Esher does not despair of either real army reform or the placing of the auxiliary forces on an efficient footing. " The Volunteers," he says "instead of being carefully fostered, are anxious and worried. The principle of a great volunteer army should be encouraged and maintained." This is in itself a cold douche for the conscriptionists, but Lord Esher speaks even more plainly and to the point. "On moral, politic, and every other ground," he adds," the British public would be very unwise to approve of conscription. For it would be no solution at all of the problems whica confront an empire like ours." His Lord- ship concludes by speaking very hopefull of the Defence Committee, " the most startling constitu- tional development of the last half-century." At the Mayoral dinner on Wednesday, Mr. A. H. Crosfield also alluded sympathetically to the Volunteers and gave-no hint of any leaning to com- pulsory Eervice. His remedy for the general lack of recruiting power is to popularise the auxiliary forces and in our view also there is no question that tbe persistently cold treatment of the Volun- teers, militia and yeomanry—and which has been accentuated since Mr. Arnold-Forster's advent— is largely reponsible for the shortage in numbers and the want of enthusiasm in both the regular and auxiliary services.</p>
        <p>The fact that the WARBING'TON AND THE London and North- NORTH-WESTERN Western Rsilway RAILWAY. Company ore by far the largest rate- payers in Warrington has probably not been generally realized. Mr. I. T. NVilliams,the district goods manager, laid some stress upon the point in his speech at the complimentary dinner on Saturday evening, as showing in what a great degree the prosperity of the company and of the town are bound up. It therefore goes without saying that it is to the interest of the company to foster and develop the trade of War- rington in every direction. There is, how- ever, justice in Warrington's complaint that it has not received in the past the attention from the company whinh such an important owndesPrves. Recently the N.W. have bestirred themselves and, in addition to a considerably improved trait' sprvice to and from the Metropolis, Warrington can now boast of some of the finest goods offices on the company's system, a fact which must be of advantage to the traders of the district.</p>
        <p>A century ago it A JOURNALISTIC STORY/ was considered</p>
        <p>sign of exceptional smartness to perpetrate a colossal hoax on some innocent and onsnspectiug, law-abiding citizen. There is the well-known story of the notorious joke perpetrated by Hook on an unoffenaing householder in Berners-street, the wag having ordered goods in the name of his victim from some scores of business houses, the result being that the traffic was entirely blocked for half-a•mile around with brewers' drays, coal carts, hay waggons, hackney coaches, mixed up with barrows and men carrying sacks' to the consternation of the luckless martyr, whose residence was besieged by an irate crowd. Ti play such pranks at the expensa of others is less reputable than to per- petrate hoaxes for one's own pecuniary profit This feat has lately been achieved by a canny Press reporter in Paris, who sent a startling paragraph round all the morning and evening journals— collecting the money as he went—its purport being that no fewer than 18,000 rats had been k:lled that day by an army of workmen construct- ing one of the new electric underground tubes. From the French papers this announcement was copied and headlined into some London cbulies.Bke a snowball, it gathered in magnitude the further it travelled ; and when the Tratuatlantic news- paper hustlers brought their magnifying-glasses to bear on the subject, the Pied Piper of Hamelin had entirely lost caste as a rodent mesmerist. Even Baron Munchausen, imaginative as he was had a certain sense of the proprieties in making exaggerations. Although in his day the world was more credulous than it is considered to be nowadays, it appears that few who published the latest j )urnalistic whopper took the trouble to make a mathematic calculation as to the time necessary to exterminate 1,000 rats, to say nothing of 18,000, within the period allotted to the working man's day by the trades unions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Meeting The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee will be held at the Town Hall, on Monday evening when the following among other business will be considered : SCHOOL MANAGENIENT. THE OBSERVANCE OF TRAFALGAR DAY.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that consent be given to the appointment of the under-mentioned teachers : Silver-street, Wesleyan, Miss Lois Pitt, certificate assistant £65 to be revised on receipt of the result of certificate examination. Trinity National, Mrs. Amelia Gorton, certificate assistant (supply) £9O. The following additional assistant teachers were appointed to the Evening Continuation schools men- tioned, viz :—Fairfield,Miss Ellen V. Roddy ; Hamilton street, Mr. John Thomson ; Heathside, Mr. Andrew Malcolm ; Warrington British; Miss Elizabeth Arnold ; Warrington British, Mr. James Walsh. It was resolved thatthe year ended the 15th Septem- ber, 1905, be counted as a year of service to the under- mentioned teachers at the Secondary school and Tech- nical Institute, and that their salaries be increased in accordance with the Committee's scale as follows,viz : Miss Margaret B. Morton, L.L.A., assistant mistress, £ll5, to £l2O ; Mr. Richard H. Albery, M. So., physics master, £l5O, to £l6O. It was resolved that the year ending the 20th October, 1905, be counted as a year of service to Mr. George S. Pencllebury. assistant master at the School of Art, and that his salary be increased from £l2O to £135 per annum from that date, and, at the end of a further year's service, to a maximum of £l5O per annum. The Committee decided that in future, books, materials, &amp;c., be not sold to the students at the Technical Institute. A letter was submitted from the Chairman of the Bradford Education Committee, suggesting a Confer- ence with other Education authorities to consider the question of the provision of Pu?il Teachers' Centres, and it was decided to intimate, in reply, that the Com- mittee would be glad to take part in such a Conference if it was arranged. . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>it was resol;ed that a circular letter be sent to the managers and head teachers of all schools, drawing attention to the 100th anniversay of Trafalgar Day which takes place on Saturday, the 21st October next, and that they be requested to do what they can to make appropriate reference to the occasion in the schools on the previous day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NEW SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee a notification dated the 21st September, 1905, from the Board of Education—conveying the approval of that Board of the plans for the erection of the " Bolton " Council school as providing accommodation for not more than 360 girls and 360 infants—total 720, and for a Cookery Centre for 18 scholars at practice and 54 at demonstration—was submitted and noted. The architect was instructed to prepare the specifications, bills of quantities, &amp;c. It was resolved that the tender of Mr. C. W. Daven- port, amounting to .‘13,784 10s., for the erection of the "Beamont" Council bchool be accepted; that an advertisement be issued inviting applications for the post of Clerk of Works for both the " Beamont" and "Bolton" Council schools, at an inclusive salary at the rate of £3 10s. per week.</p>
        <p>TILE SENIOR MISTILF.SS AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL. Mr. Edwin Plinston will move :—That the public and press be excluded from this meeting of the Education Committee at this stage of the proceedings. (a) That the resolution of the Education Committee at their meeting on the 28th August, 1905 (No. C. 1133),appoint- ing Miss Elizabeth Frost senior assistant mistress at the Secondary school, be rescinded. (b) That the School Management Sub-Committee be instructed to re-consider the appointment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH</p>
        <p>OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEER ENDING OCTOBER 7th 1905.</p>
        <p>During the week, the following births and dssths were registered:—Warrington Registration District births, 43, deaths, 16. 'The excess of births over deaths was 27.</p>
        <p>• The birth-rate was equal to 32.3 The death-rate from all causes was equal to 12.0 per 1,000 against 178 the average of the previous 4 weeks and against 19.0 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotie diseases was equal to P 5 per 1,000, against 4.6 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against 8•3 the corresponding period of 1904. The ages of mortality were:- 0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65—up. Total</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from diphtheria, 1 Mom diarrhcea, 3 from phthisis, 1 from other tubercular diseases, 3 from lung diseases, 1 from heart disease,6 all other causes. _</p>
        <p>The following cases of infectious diseases have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever, 1 case, diphtheria 4 cases, erysipelas,. 2 cases, phthisis, 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending 7th October, 1905, 31; Newton, 23: Borough, 8. IVIETEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 16 Fah. and therefore 4 deg. lower than the preceding week,The maximum temperature 57F., was recorded on the Ist inst., and the minimum temperature 34F., on the sth Inst.</p>
        <p>The rainfall nleasured 1•13 inch. The general direction was W.N.W.</p>
        <p>J. GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health. ,</p>
        <p>• Warrington P.S.A. Society. An open meeting of the above society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday, there being an excellent gathering. Mr. E. Osborne occupied the chair. Mr. Martley (Widnes) sang "0 song divine" and " The Promise of the King," the accompanist being Mr. B. Wadsworth. Mr. G.Davey- Smith gave a capital address,his subject being " Slaps that pass in the night." -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>"A hair on the bead is worth two on the brush" is Mr. W. IL Lever's version of an old proverb.</p>
        <p>Ms Worship the Mayor (Tntertained a large company to a right royal repast on Wednesday, but the after- oratory, with very few exceptions, was feeble. Public speaking like public morality seems at a very low ebb) in Warrington. . .</p>
        <p>* * In a case at Chester on Wednesday, in which John Mitchell, Digby Farm, Bretton, was fined 20s. and costs for selling watered milk, it was stated that an "appeal to the cow " was made, and that animal obliged with" over-proof" milk.</p>
        <p>* * * One of the compliments paid to Alderman Bennett at the Literary and Philosophical Society's meeting on Monday was that "his rapidity of speech staggered humanity." We might add, also, that the M.S. of his "paper" staggered the local reporters. There is an odd resemblance between Mr. Bennett's handwriting and his speech.</p>
        <p>* As Trafalgar Day falls on o Saturday (October 21st) the school children will not be able to celebukte the day itself in the schools. The School Manage- ment Committee, have, however,issued instructions to teachers to make appropriate references to tbe notable centenary during the lessons _on the Friday.</p>
        <p>* Mr. D. Lloyd-George, M.P., is about again after undergoing a slight operation for tonsilitis, but he is forbidden to speak in public for a while, and is about to join his brother in the south of France for a rest. Mr. Lloyd-George has passively resisted ' payment of his Education Rate in the borough of Wandsworth, and one of his gold presentation keys has been seized by the overseer. . .</p>
        <p>*s* " I believe that stays are essentially a hygienic garment. Not only do they give useful support 0 the figure, but they protect all the vital organs against cold. I have often thought of getting a pair myself not for the sake of appearances, but in order to enable me to dispense with an overcoat, and possibly other garments. As for the testhetics of the question, I arn quite certain that stays are a necessity of modern costume. I would not engage a cook who did not wear them.—Mr. La,bouchere in " Truth."</p>
        <p>* * * The influential committee of prominent c:ltton manu- facturers haslpresentecl its report in answer to the, recommendations of the Tariff Reform "Commission on the cotton industry, and it gives the members of that august body something to think about. The report states that " Free Trade is one of the chief causes of the predominating position held by the Lancashire cotton industry among the industries of the world, and is a vital condition of our future prosperity reaching its highest attainable level." * * * . . . _</p>
        <p>A large and excited crowd assembled is Horse- market-street on Thursday evening, attracted bY rumours of a serious fire on the premises of a well- known dealer in fireworks. All manner of strange stories were told and one gentleman seriously assure us that the fire was discoTered through "crackers popping off, and that there was an attractive hut impromptu pyrotechnic display to be witnessed. ne was greatly in error. The fire had broken out on the premises of Messrs. Crampton, through the overheating of a stove, and was quickly extinguished by the members of the local fire brigade who made a record turn out.</p>
        <p>Does dining inculcate : *love of the beautiful ? any rate at two dinners this week, the " Beautifal Warrington " Society was the subject of enthusiastic advocacy. Mr. I. T. Williams, the popular Loudon and North-Western District goods manager, in the course of the complimentary dinner to himself on Saturday evening spoke highly of the new society, and foreshadowed the beautification of the square in front of Arpley Station. There is ample space here for a welcome spot of green. At the Mayoral dinner. Mr. William Long, J.P. also voiced the claims and objects of the society, which has a most encouraging outlook before it.</p>
        <p>* * * An amusing picture of Warrington as a newly - discovered health resort was presented by lir. W. P. Rylands at the Mayoral banquet on Wednesday. The enterprise of the London and North-Western Rallwa3r has, he pointed out, led to the posting of bills in the metropolis announcing the " shortest and quickest route to Warrington.' This, said Mr. Rylands, had given rise to the idea that Warrington was a newlY- found watering place, and no doubt a visit to Bank Quay station during August would have revealed paterfamilias surrounded by disconsolate youngsters carrying buckets and spades and looking in vain for the sea ! Warrington's attractions, however, are of s commercial order and the Beautiful Warringtoe Society has much to do before the town will become famed as a " garden city" or a health resort. 1* * *</p>
        <p>" Truth " makes a reference this week to the recent motor-car fatality in Warrington when a little girl was killed, in the following terms :—" Police and other witnesses testified that the car was being driven with the greatest care, and that the accident WO unavoidable, and the jury agreed that no blame what- ever could be attached to the driver. On this the Warrington Guardian sapiently remarks that the motor-car, in the most favourable circumstances, ia danger to the public, and the helplessness of those wile use them on the public thoroughfares to prevent disaster only proves the more conclusively that the use of the vehicle should be rigorously regulated b 7 Parliament.' What utter nonsense ! It might as well be said that the use of horse-drawn vehicles should be rigorously regulated because they sometimes run over people • indeed this might be said with far more reason, for, as every intelligent person recognises, a motor-car can be guided and controlled by the driver much more effectually than any horse." * * * Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P., ventured on very unsafe ground at the Junior Conservative Club on 'Tuesday evening when he referred to Chinese labour. It is no use him telling the public that Chinese labour W 35 not introduced by the initiative of our Government, but by the will of the Transvaal Government, or that the white man can not and will not undertake tb,e, labour the Chinese are now doing, because he W not be believed. Mr. RI. Outhwaite knocked sue' fanciful notions on the head a few weeks ag° and gave actual, undeniable proof of the complicitY, of the home government in the Rand mine-owner 3 scheme to get labour at Is. a day. lie made it clear also that white men could do the work better thas Chinese, but they would want a living wage sncl possibly a vote—two impossible conditions from the mine-owners' point of view. The history of the great conspiracy as detailed by Mr. Outhwaite should not be forgotton, and we hope the public will read carefully. the reprints of his speech now issued in pampillev form.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Edwin Plinston, the Labour candidate iT Bewsey Ward, has commenced an active advocacY ° the claims of labour upon the workingmen of the ward. His first open air meeting was not encourag- ing. At eight o'clock, the advertised time for cots- mencing no one had put in an appearance. A fe° minutes later the principals walked up, and having completed the arrangements for their oratorical efforts' they commenced a patient wait for the audience t° appear on the scene. In a short time tits übiquitous urchin made his presence known and ate!' a brief absence returned strongly re-inforced alla indulged in a game of hide and seek round the Plat" form from which the public were to be enlightened. A,,t 8.20 Mr. Edward Horrocks opened the meeting addressed an audience, excluding irresponsil juveniles, of four. He stuck manfully to his task all when he made way for Councillor Wright there were about a dozen electors present. Under the circum- stances the speeches were condensed and there sbeing no response to the chairman's repeated request for questiohs the meeting closed half an hour later.</p>
        <p>Workman Claims Compensation. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday. before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., $ claim was made under, the Workmen's Compensation Act,lby Edward Heesom, Wash-lane, Latchford,againsi the Atanchester Ship Canal Company, for damages lot injuries sustained whilst following his employment About nine months ago Heesom, who was a foreman platela3er, was at work when a:piece of a hammer- head flew into his left eye and destroyed the sight' Since that date he had been in receipt of 135. aII week compensation. Plaintiff stated that be was unable to take the position which he formerly held a! he could not see properly with his right eye t;liich been damaged as the result of the accident. The can was adjourned to enable the plaintiff to see if he coula work.—Mr. Browne appeared for the plaintiff, 311.• Newman, Manchester, representing the respondents.</p>
