        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>'Notice. The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on :Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will tthdertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after mbort,without extra charge. Address all communica- etions to "Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington Index to Inside Pages. PACE. 2. Women's Chat and Fashions. The Duties of Auditors. Harvest Festivals. 3. Football. "The White Rose " (serial). G. New Railway Offices : Complimentary' dinner to Mr. I. 'P. Williams. , Our Local Caurches. Trades Council Annual Meeting. Police News. 7. "Some Types of Artiste' Models," by Mrs. Frank Elliot. Children's Corner. notions ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. 'Coming Events., The seventh annual show of prize poultry, pigeons. :r abbits and cavies is to he held in Belle Vue Gardens on Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday October 17, 18, and 19. The entries number 4,220 and are from . all parts of the country. Visitors to the show on Wednesday will combine the attraction of the wellknown Belle Vue fireworks. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection With Emmanuel Church, will be held to-mort•ow when the preacher will be the Rev. F. Vaughan. A special service in memory of the late Mr. Job Swales will be held at Legh-street,Primitive Methodist ' Church, to-morrow murning. The preacher will be the Rev. L. Stowe. The people's services in the Parr Hill, will be con- -tinned on Sunday evening when the Rev. H. Dunnico will preach on " The Church and its relation to labour and labour questions." The anniversary services in connection with Latch- ford Baptist Church will be held on Sunday when the preae—or will be the Rev. J. F. Matthews. Special music be given by the choir. The annual meeting of the Warrington and District Property Owners' Association will be held in Tort's Assembly Rooms, Bridge-street, on Tuesday evening. The second of a series of university extension lectures will be given by the Rev. W. Lawrence Schroeder, M.A., Victoria University, Manchester, on Friday evening next, in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street. -The subject of the lecture will be "Charles Lamb." The first of a series of popular concerts will be given in the Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on the 21stinst. A charge of one penny only will be made for admission and doubtless the public will appreciate 'the 'provision. The annual performances by the Warrinetnn Dramatic Society will be given in the Parr Hall ko:n November 15th to 18th, when "Dandy Dick" will be 'produced. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection With Wycliffe church will be held to-morrow when the preacher will be the Rev. John Yonge. WASHING AND WRINGING MACHINES try J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55 Bridge-street, 'Warrington.— ADVT. Personal. • Mr. R. -A. Naylor, the Conservative candidate for •the Carnarvon Boroughs, opened a new Conservative 'Club at Glanadda, Bangor, a wholly working-class "district on Saturday evening. The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. W. Bolton) will be present at a ball to be given this (Friday) evening by the Lord Mayor of London. PUBLIC BE.NEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—AdrL. Presentation. On Sunday afternoon the members of the Young ken's Bible Ciao connected with St. Barnabas Church presented Ulf. W. P. James with a handsome ebmbrella as a mark of appreciation or the services he -has rendered to the class. Mr. W. Turner made the Presentation and Mr. James briefly returned thanks. Warrington Wesleyan Harriers. Numbering 19, the members ;,urned out from the Unicorn Inn on Saturday for the opening run of the season An enjoyable run of five miles over stiff otintry, followed by a mile break, provided an interesting race. First six home were : —l, Bolton ; 2, Massey ; 3, Worsley ; 4, Savage ; 5, Beswick ; 6; Gill. MAIL Cawrs,and Flassinettes. the latest designs. 4. S. GARLAND. 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. —Anvr. Sale of Work. A two days' sale of work "in connection with St. 'George's Mission was opened this (Friday) afternoon, )drs. J. C. Parr performing the ceremony. • The sale is in aid of the extension fund on which there is a debit balance of about £7OO. The stalls which con- tained a large number of useful and ornamental articles, were in charge of the following ladies : Rectory and St. Clement's stall, Mrs. Willis, Mrs. Ilalber and friends ; congregational stall, Mesdames Wilsnu, Smith, Knight, Forster and Miss Sumner; mothers' meeting stall Mesdames Ryland, Wadsworth and Taylor ; refreshment- stall, Misses Willis and Brookes ; girl's sewing class stall, Mesdames White and Barrett and Miss Hetherington ; stationery, toys and china stalls, Misses A. Buckley, E. Bowles and J• Altman ; flowers stall, Mrs. and the Misses Davies. MP. T. J. Down's Biography. There has just been issued from the press a !:fiography of the late Mr. T. J. Down and a review of „Zia ;work in the formation of local military and string bands. The author is Mr.H.P.Hughes, of Stockton Heath, Who has obviously devoted a large amount of pains- taking and affectionate labour to the task, and has I.nibbeededin producing a work which should be valued 41? Warrington by the late Mr. Down's many admirers. he book, which extends to 144 pages, contains ani `admirable portrait of Hr. Down, as well as a reference to Miss Annie Down, who was for many year a Prominent performer in the bands ; the history of the l'tring and military bands; their public performances, .anecdotes, ST.; an account of the re-assembly of the bands in February, 1935 ; the presentations .t,.? Mrs. D own ; an appendix containing Mr. own's "Trip to the English Lakes," and a paper on - Landscape Photography" read by him at the annual Meeting of the Warrington Amateur Photographic c'ejety in March", 1887. The book is published by Ur. Percival Pears e at 2s. 'Catholic Young Men's Church Parade. The first annual church parade in connection with i!le Warrington Catholic Young Men's Society was ueld on Sunday evening. Over a hundred members 'assembled at the new rooms in Horsemarket-street and walked in procession to St. .A.lban's Church where 'late 'lad been specially reserved for them. The Rev. a • Wilson, 0.5.8., of St. Peter's, Liverpool, preached special sermon to the members of the Society, dwell _n - Upon the valuable work which such organizations Could do. While attending to the social side of their ork they must not neglect the religious side of the _bciety's life. Solemn Benediction was given by the V)aplain (Rev. Father Swarbreck) assisted by the „ev• Fathers Whittle and Buggins. At the close the service the members sang with great eti. "br " Faith of our Fathers." Afterwards meeting was held in the Society's rooms. -?r other Owen Toole presided and an able address was e •u_ livered by the Rev. Dr. Wilson. A vote of thanks 7,oe.7accorded to Dr. Wilson on the proposition of Bro. a ior neY seconded by Bro. Si. Cunningham. The _esident was also thanked on the motion or the chaplain seconded by Bro. J. Boyle. Bro. Toole ex- be • sed great pleasure that forty new members had en enrolled since Thursday. The membership now stands at about 250.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>Kingsley Minister goes to Gaol. On Monday the Atev. Henry Richard Didcock, Primitive Methodist minister at Kingsley, ,near War- rington went to gaol for seven days for non-payment of 2s. die educational porticin af the poor-rate, for which he was summoned at he recent Eddisbury Sessions. He had previously been in gaol at Sheffield Apollo Choir at Blackpool. The Warrington Apollo Prize Male Voice Choir took part in the Blackpool Musical Festival on Satur- day. As the result of a preliminary . test they were selected to compete in the male voice choir (tenor lead) competition. and were awarded the fourth place with 249 points to their credit. The test pieces were " The old soldier's dream "and " From the sea."</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.-lINDTIR THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington Woman's Mysterious Death. An inquest was held at Woolston, on Wednesday evening,on the body of Mary Ann _bridge, of Eldon street, Warrington, which was recovered from the Woolston Canal, near Paddington Lock on Monday night. The evidence showad that the deceased was seen by the lock-keeper (Mr. Rathbone) at about seven o'clock on Monday evening, walking along the canal bank. Later her apron and shawl were found on the bank. Dragging operations were commenced and the body was recovered from the canal. A verdict of" found drowned" was returned. Warrington Constable's Capture. Police Constable Turner, of the Warrington Police, Force, was patrolling Sankey-street late last Friday' night when he saw a young man' who afterwards gave the name of John Williams, riding a .smart looking bicycle in the direction of Liverpool. There was no light on the machine and the offi3er being suspicious, stopped the rider and questioned him. He at once admitted having stolen the bicycle from Manchester. On Saturday the Borough Bench handed Williams 'yver to the custody of the Warrington police. St. Mary's, Latchford. As will be seen from our advertisement columns, the famous Jesuit preacher the Rev. Father Jagger is to preach the Annual Charity Sermons at • St. Mary's' Latchford on Sunday, October 22nd inst. It will be remembered that on , the occasion of the re- opening of the organ in February last, he took the place of the Right Rev. Dr. Allen, the Bishop of the Diocese, who had promised to preach the re-opening sermons but was at the last moment stricken down with a serious throat affection. Those who heard Father Jagger then have anxiously looked forward to his next visit, and many, non-Catholics as well as Catholics, have asked to be apprised of his re-appear- ance in Warrington. He has, despite his many engagements kindly consented to preach on the date named, so the opportunity so much looked forward to may be taken advantage of on next Sunday week. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feelins.—(Advt.) Fatal Dizzy Bout. Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at the Coroner's Court on Monday afternoon touching the death of John Kelly (70), who resided at 66, Lyme-street, Haydoek. —The Coroner informed the jury that deceased died so long ago as the previous Monday at the Workhouse Hospital, but through some misunderstanding the facts of the case only came to his knowledge on Saturday morning. He had made a very careful inquiry and found that sad one was to blame in the inatter.—Ellen Jones, wife of John Jones, miner, 156, Church street, Haydock, said that on Monday, September 25th, she was informed that the deceased had fallen downstairs at his lodgings, and on going there she found him bleeding from a wound on kis head. Dr. Dowling was called, and he attended to the injuries. He ordered his removal to the Warrington Union Hospital, and he died on October 2nd. She asked deceased shortly after the accident how it happened and he said he was going upstairs to bed, when he was seized with a dizzy bout and fell to the bottom. , She had known him for about twenty-five years, and ' he had always been a steady man.—Dr. Peacock stated that Kelly died from a fracture of the skull.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>- - H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE Tomo takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Beautifying a Church Yard. The " beautiful Warringtan " movement appears to be making its influence felt, if we may judge from the following interesting paragraph in ' St. Ann's Parish Magazine":—"On two Saturday afternoons(up to date), that is September 23rd and .30th, the churchyard was alive with busy helpers who came together to set in order the trees and ground in general. Under expert advice and leadership it was found that 'drastic measures ' had to be taken with the overgrown elders and privet to ensure in the future better growth and neatness, and so cutting back ' had to be performed. Already the changed appearance is a great improvement, and if the plan already pro- posed be followed out we shall be helping forward very materially the movement—newly started in the town—of beautifying Warrington. The plan arranged is thus : —to make good the gaps ' in the rows of trees alrpady planted ; to plant additional ornamental trees as needed ; to plant according to a pre-arranged system, climbers to grow up the church and boundary walls. To carry out the abive success- fully we need assistance ; this can be given in several ways in which everyone can take a part. You can help :—by supplying trees or climbing plants ; by gifts of money to litly what is needed and by undertaking to help from time to time in the general work con- nected with the church grounds,, The Vicar and wardens will be pleased to receive contributions and offers of personal service."</p>
        <p>UNDER-THE CLOCK for Good Booxs.—Advt. The Held Club.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Fairhurst presided at the first !nesting of the winter session held in the Museum Lecture Ro.iin last Friday evening. It was the entomological even- ing, at which it is he custom for those members who are specially interested in the study of insects to dis- play the results of their season's collecting. Mr. W. Mounfield had acted as organizer, and in addition to describing the splendid series of moths and butterflies which he was exhibiting, commented upm the results of larva-hunting and sugaring during the spring and summer, and also pointed out that there had been an unusual prevalance of melanin forms, which in view of the fine dry weather during the early summer months militated against the theory that melanism was due to humidity. Mr. Robinson also exhibited a very fine display of lepidoptera. Mr. F. Wright, in addition to the display of his captures, contributed some interest- ing and useful notes, the results of his own observa- tions, on their:habits. especially in the larval stage. Mr. Dunlop exhibited on behalf of Mr. Dutton a case of beetles from Orkney, collected during the summer by Mr. G. Ellison, of Liverpool. Dr. Markel brought a large number of boxes of insects, mostly illustrative of mimicry or of protective reemblance. These were much admired. Mr. J. Smith contributed. some notes on the reproduction of lepidoptera and the possibility of hybridisation in that order.—There was a lively dis- cussion in which Messrs. Greening, Madeley, Jolley, Mounfield and Dunlop and Dr.- Markel took part- Many of the beautiful illustrations of the wonders of theinsect world which l'vere displayed at the meeting will in due time enrich the collections of the Museum, to which the Field Club has added so largely. 11 KITCHEN RANGES, Fire Grates, Tiles Scc., large stock.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 10 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—ADvx. The Roman Remains at Wilderspool. _ _ . . . _</p>
        <p>On Saturday at the kind invitation of Mr. Thomas May, F.S.A., the members of the Manchester and District Branch of the Classical Association and a few Warrington friends visited the Roman station at Wilderspool. The association take a deep interest in Roman remains, and excavations are being made by them at the present time at Melandra Castle, near Glossop. The opportunity of seeing the remains at Wilderspool was taken advantage of before the trenches were filled in once more. The party, which numbered about 40, included the Secretary. (Miss Dora Lirnebeer), Professor Conway (chairman of the com- mittee), the Rev. C. Harvey Cook and Mr. C. Xladeley (librarian). The visitors proceeded to Messrs. Greenall's offices where Mr. May, who conducted the narty,showed the plans of the ground. They afterwards visited the camp, which lies several feet below the turf of an almost level paddock on the south side of the Mersey, adjoining Messrs. ...;-'reenall's brewery. Perhaps one of the most interesting of the remains was an iron purifying furnace, and several large stones were placed around it, evidently for the purpose of standing upon while at work. An anvil stone was shown as well as a number of nails which had been found in the hollow. Mr. May also pointed out sections of the rampart, the end of the Roman road, a clay pit, a Roman highway, and a well to the depth of 101 feet, A quantity of pottery, a fine collection of fragments and old materials were also shown, and his remarks were very interesting and valuable. The party after- wards proceeded to the King's Cafe, where tea was served, after which a visit was paid to the Museum. Mr. May was heartily thanked for his kindness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 14, 1905. EARL SPENCER.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 14, 1905. EARL SPENCER.</p>
        <p>" Y" Branch of the B.W.T.A. A " social " in connection with the above society was held in Cairo-street schoolrooms on Thursday evening. The members thought they deserved a little recreation after their efforts at the recent sale of work, and they took it with tho same amount of vigour. Mrs. J. odds welcomed the members at 7.30, and the folawing ladies assisteel4o a prozramme, which was interspersed with genies Plerpoint, Miss A. Beswick, Miss Ilibbert, Miss a Browne, Miss Fieldhouse, and Mrs. F. Griffiths, who accompanied the soloists. As for Cuh'NDIGHAM'S HERR-CLASS MINERAL</p>
        <p>WATERS.—Advt. • Stockton Heath Garden Society. The first monthly meeting ;of the Winter Session was held at the Institute, Victoria Square, on Tues- day evening last, when the Committees arrang meats for having shows of flowers at each of the meetings was inaugurated and proved a great success. Three prizes were offered by Mr, Woodcock in three classes, the first for six trusses of outdoor grown chry- santhemums, the second for three, and the third for a single bloom. 'There was an excellent competitive show which was jiidged by Mr. Tandy, and the prizes were awarded to Mr. J. Blinston first class,Mr. Mottram second, and Mr. E. Vernon third, There were also large exhibits of blooms, not for competition, by Mr. Vere B. Davies (the president) Mr. Tandy, Mr. Baker (gardener to Mr. W. Bainbridge), Mr. Howard, (gardener to F. Mr. Monks,) Mr. Moore and Mr. Rose. Some very good hybrid tea roses were shown by Mr. Ryan, Mr. F. Broady and Mr. Moore ; dahlias by Mr. Rathbone and Michmlmas daisies by Mr. Wood- cook. A. large number of members were present and the chair was occupied by Mr. C. Plinston. Mr. Wood- cock gave an exceedingly able and practical paper on chrysanthemums.. Primitive Methodist Convention.</p>
        <p>A convention was held at the Legh street Primitive Methodist Church on Thursday under the auspices of the. Liverpool district of the connexion. The theme for discussion for the day was "The Revival." The afternoon meeting was well attended, the chair being occupied by the Rev. C. L. Stowe, who said the Rev. IL R. Didcock,who should have read a paper on" The Revival in Wales" was unable to attend being at present in gaol as the result of a firm stand, which he had taken with regard to the Education act. Mr. Stowe read the paper which Mr. Didcock had forward- ed. A discussion followed, which was introduced by the Rev. S. G. Delafield,;Widne4. Mr. C. Stage Widnes; gave an address on "The revival in put. Churches." Mr. G. Liudon, St. Helens, presided over the evening meeting at which there was a good attend- ance. The Rev. J. H. Rollason; Earlestown, read a paper on "The relation of our young people to the revival." while Mr. J. Illidge, Runcorn, addressed the gathering on "The relation of the individual to the revival.' The proceedings terminated with a conse- cration service which was conducted by the Rev. A. S. Targett, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS</p>
