r of replying on their behalf. reached the millenium by a good deal, and the day of (Applause). universal peace was yet a long way off ; but that at least could be done in the meantime, and while pro- posing that toast, he would like to express with all honesty and fervour his admiration for his own country- men who in the different branches of the services played their parts so patriotically and so well. (Applause.) In responding, Colonel EDELi'ES: expressed the intense satisfaction of the members of the imperial forces present that night that there was a hope of the chief magistracy of the Borough remaining unchanged for another year. (Applause.) He would like to say on behalf of those in whose name he was entitled to</p>
        <p>"TIE MAYORESS."</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Ronnars said he had been entrusted unexpectedly aadto his great personal pleasure with the task of proposing " The health of the !ilayorea, Mrs. Bolton.") (Applause.) A good deal had been said that night—and properly said—in regard to the responsible duties of the Mayor of an important town like theirs and he thought he could say without hesitation that while they could claim to have been specially fortunate —except perhaps in his own case.—(Cries of "No, no,") —in its Mayors, they had been more than specially fortunate—and in this he .would make no exception in his own case—in its Mayoresses. (Applause.) Although those ladies did not appear before the public as fre- quently as the masters of ceremonies did, he could assure them from his personal knowledge and experi- ence that there were almost innumerable duties to perform. There were the courtesies they had unvari- ably extended to all and sundry alike, the many and very great calls upon their time and some- times upon their health, and he had no doubt they would all agree with him enthusi- astically, that while their present Mayoress was one of those unobtrusive ladies who never pushed herself before the public unnecessarily she had on each and every occasion worthily assisted the efforts of her husband ' and done everything a woman could possibly do to main- tain with efficiency and credit the duties of the office which her husband had been honoured to hold (Applause.) He sincerely hoped that she might live to see each member of her family occupying if not a public position, at any rate, an honourable position in the town and district. (Applause.) The toast was received with enthusiasm, and the Ms.vost briefly replied. 1" THE TOWN AND TRADE OF WARRINGTON." Mr. WILLIAM LONG proposed this toast and said it I would commend itself to the enthusiasm of all, for it was the next thing to proposing the toast of their noble selves. (Hear, hear and applause.) As to the town he did not intend to say very much about it. except to point out the immense changes be had seen in it, com- ing pretty regularly into the town from three miles away. His first recollection of Warrington was of the time of the incorporation of the borough when Mr. Beatnont was the Mayor. Those who could claim acquaintanceship of those times, would remember great changes which had taken place in the town, changes which had always tended in the direction :of the enlargement of its borders. He could not say very much about the beauty of the place, which was more apparent in those early days than to-day but he hoped, with the new spirit which was growing up in the town, that War- rington might some time become beautiful Warrington (Hear, hear.) Whatever might be their position in life, they would welcome the day when there WM less smoke in the atmosphere and more beauty in the surreundings of Warrington. They mnst remember that it had a splendid past. (Hear, hear.) It had been called the Athens of the North, and he trusted that that:fact would be a stimulation to make Warrington a place to be proud of. With regard to the trade of the town he was glad to see that the iron and cotton trades were improving. He wished he could say the same of the tanning trade. He askes' them to drink the toast and he expressed the hope that Warrington would go on flourishing for a good many years to come. i WARRINGTON AS A WATERING-PLACE. Nlr. W. PETER. RYL ANDS, in reply, said he Was sure Warringtonians must agree with him when he said that Warrington was the hub of the universe. (Laughter.) lt might be that the idea was not generally shared by the other towns, but if that were so,it was partly owing to the innate diffidence and modesty of Wsrringtonians and partly because of the lack of appreciation of their importance by the London and North-Western Railway Company. (Laughter.) It was a remarkable thing that though Warrington was the most important town on the North-Western main line between London and Glasgow he believed that up to two years ago no North- Western through train was ever known to stop there. Some years ago, speaking to friends in the South, and he told them he came from Warrington, he was always met by the same reply, " Where is Wad.rington ?" When he had described where it was they said "Oh yes, I think I remember mining through a smoky town with a name something like that on the way to Scotland " (laughter). He was pleased to say that all that had changed and Warrington was coming to its own. He bad no doubt that many who had been in London,during the last two years would have seen in the whole of London, covering the placards, and in large letters, the announcement that the London and North Western Railway was th 9 shortest and quickest route to Warring- ton.(Laughter). This sudden notoriety given to a com- paratively unknown place gave rise to the idea that Warrington was a newly discovered watering place. (Laughter). He dare say some of those present had . noticed during the month of August, at Bank Quay "THE CORPORATION.' station, puzzled looking men and women with large MAYOR'S CRITICISM OF COUNCILLORS. families armed with spades and buckets looking discon- , Sir GILBERT GREENALL, Bart proposed the toast OI solately for the sea shore—(laughter). He understood</p>
        <p>speak that they would do the best they could to give expression to that satisfaction. They, in their small part, would be glad to take his worship to church, to minister to his welfare and . . .</p>
        <p>personal security, so far as lay in their power, and to offer him in their little Volunteer mess a elle retreat</p>
        <p>if it should ever occur that the atmosphere of the Council chamber became over-charged or a little super-, heated. (Laughter). The navy, continued Colonel E.lelsten, was ever the dearest portion of the service to the English mind, and•it was particularly appropriate that the navy should be uppermost in their thoughts beciusa a hundred years ago that night Nelson and Collingwood and about forty sail stood oft the bay of Trafalgar waiting the coming out of the hostile fleet from the harbour of Cadiz. Ten days later the mem- orable battle was fought and England became mistress of the seas. (Applause). Colonel Edelsten went on to refer to the good feeling which now existed been England and France as evidenced by the rejoicings on the occasion of the visit of the French fleet</p>
        <p>The present was an anxious time for anyone with any responsibility, however humble, in army matters. Their own local Volunteer Corps—which he was happy to say was most highly reported upon this year for efficiency, thanks to the generosity of the employers in allowing the men to go to camp—was threatened with a very serious reduction in its income—why, as yet, he could not learn. The question of military efficiency was one for the nation, and if the nation wished to speak effectively it must speak quickly. Of course, there were some people who said that force 1 was horrid and out of date. It was easy to talk in that strain—he called it gallery talk—bnt the world had ever been to the virile and the strong, and force would ever have the last word after argument. He thought 1 the imperial forces were justified in asking the nation for a little more interest in their movements and in their constitution. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>"THE MAYOR." Mr. H. PIERPOINT, M.P., in proposing this toast, said the Mayor of Warrington had now been Mayor a few days short of two years, and he hal filled the office with ability, good humour, and an nuswerving fueling of duty during that time. He congratulated the town that it was not unlikely that he would serve another year to complete his three yelrs. (Applause). Alderman William Bolton had had no easy task to fill, partly because—and it was a cause of pride—that he had followed a long list of very worthy Warrington men whom they had delighted to honour. He had also a difficult task to perform because he had to preside over a Council where sometimes municipal thoughts and municipal words were particularly free. He had to sit often in a Chamber where the municipal therometor stood at 98—at blood heat (laughter.)—and it must be a great pleasure to the Mayor when he left that super- heated chamber and found himself among his orchids rat home. (Applause.) In conclusion Mr. PlEneolyr said his Worship had not only extended to them his generous hospitality, but it had been given in a way which they had thoroughly enjoyed. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in responding said he considered that every townsman owed a duty to his fellow-inhabitants, that if he could do anything for them he ought to do it. He had tried to do his best so far us his abilities</p>
        <p>allowed. Probably some of those present did not understand whether it was his best or not, in fact he was told the other night that he had not done his best. (Laughter). To discharge the duties of the Mayoralty was no light task if one wished to do it well. If a Mayor tried to plese everyone he would probably end by pleasing no one. During his term of office he had had to deal with the Ship Canal people. He did not want to say that they or he had come out first but he thought Warrington had got a great deal more than it thought it would get. The traders of Warring- ' ton, who were really the men who understood what Warrington was as a business community, would know that. If one of them who was as capable of doing the business as he was would step into the breach and relieve him of the office he would be glad. He thought there were plenty of men in Warrington who could fulfil the duties without fear or favour, and stand all the kicks they could possibly get from everyone who did not agree with them, and come out as well as he had done.</p>
        <p>fthat it was owing to this fact that a Society had been formed, and he was informed that many members were , present that night, which had as its object, to make I Warrington beautiful—(laughter) He was not quite sure, bat he believed he had seen it in one of the papers that the Warrington Corporation were in some way or other emulating the Corporation of Blackpool with the idea of rivalling the attractions of that town.(Laughter.) He was sure teat Warrington was quite sufficient for them as Warringtonians as it was. They were proud of their town. (Applause.) The town he had no hesita- tion in saying, had a greater variety of manufactures than any other town in England, certainly any town of anything like similar size. It remained for them to see that Warrington continued to hold the place In the markets of the world which it held to-day, and he was sure that so long as Warrington produced the class of men such as were the leading citizens to-day, there was no fear of the future. (Applause.) The proceedings were brought to a close by the sing- ing of "Auld Lang Syne " and the playing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER TRE-CLOCK.BRIDGE•STREET.—Advt. Crosfields Fire Brigade. . _</head>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER TRE-CLOCK.BRIDGE•STREET.—Advt. Crosfields Fire Brigade. . _</p>
        <p>This (Saturday) afterwnoon this brigade will hold its annual tournament, commencing at 2.30. In previous years this event • has been a great success, and the various evolutions have bean witnessed with great interest by enthusiastic crowds. The programme for this year is of a very inviting character, and as there is the greatest rivalry between the two competing brigades, which represent Messrs. Crosfields' Liverpool and Warrington works, each event will no doubt be very keenly contested. As is usual, the competition does not follow the beaten track. For instance, one of the items will illustrate a change of front, the com- petitors being forced to make up all their appliances and shift to another position, recommencing opera- tions at considerable distance. Another attractive item will be the life-saving display, which lain embrace Tompier, escape and smoke-helmet drills. Then, of course, there will be the usual amusing turns. The Ambulance Corps too will give a series of demonstrations. As, on account of the lateness of the season,it will be necessary to hurry the programme through, visitors will be assured of a performance full of interest from start to finish. There will be a joint display, and the tournament will conclude with a full turn-out. During the afternoon selections of music will be given by Crosfields' Prize Band and the pro- ceedings will end with the distribution of prizes. As in former years, the firm will welcome any visitors to the tournament. Tickets of admission may be had gratis on application at the works tithe office, Bank Quay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MR. R. L. OUTHWAITE "KIDNAPPED." --- Peculiar Adventure at Leeds.</head>
        <p>MR. R. L. OUTHWAITE "KIDNAPPED." --- Peculiar Adventure at Leeds.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. L. Outhwaite has this weak been taking an active part in the Barkston Ash election, where polling takes place to-day (Friday), and chance brought him an amusing adventure on Wednesday. Coming up to Ls's(is, from London. in order to speak at Wetherby, Mr. Outhwaite had in the ordinary course of events to change stations at Leeds and drive across the city. This, however, he did not know, but, on alighting from the train, two men rushed up to him. " Are you Mr. Outhwaite ?" he thought tkey asked. "Yea," said that gentleman. " Corns on, then," they said, and without more ado bundlei him and themselve3 into a cab. Ilalf-way across Leeds one of them leant forward and mysteriously remarked, " you know you've got to he says."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>' SEIZED WITH A STROKE WHILE our SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>' SEIZED WITH A STROKE WHILE our SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>Condition Very Crave.</p>
        <p>Politicians of all shades of opinion will hear with deep concern the sad news th It Earl Spencer, the veteran leader of the Liberal party in the House of Lords, lies in a grave condition at North Creake, Fakenham, Norfolk. His lordship was seized with a stroke while out shooting at his box on Thursday morning, and at a late h,ur was still unconscious. His lordship is suffering from a smsll clot of blood on the brain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>'THE MAYORAL DINNER. _- LARGE GATHERING AT THE PARR</head>
        <p>'THE MAYORAL DINNER. _- LARGE GATHERING AT THE PARR</p>
        <p>HALL.</p>
        <p>On WedneSday evening His Worsbip the Mayor (Alderman W. Bolton) entertained about 130 guests to idinner at the Parr Hall. The large building had been very tastefully decorated with plants from the Town Hall and Park, while the tables themselves were bright with many-coloured orchids from the hot- houses of his Worship. During the dinner, which was in Mr. Powys' best style, selections of light music were given by Mr. R. H. Pearson's orchestra. The following is a list of those who accepted invi- tations, and nearly all of whom were present : Council :—Aldermen Bennett, Fairclongh, Francomb, Stnethurst, Tinnion, Councillors Barton, Brough, Burton, Clegg, Craik, Ellison, Evans, Forshaw, Fulton- ' Smith, Hulse, Joseph, Maginn, A. Peacock, P. Peacock, Roberts, Sankey, Wilkinson, Wilson. Magis- trates :—Messrs. J C. Ridgway, John Fairclough, R. T. Fairclongh, Dr. Fox, E. J. Guest, J. W. Hayward. James Hepherd, H. Roberts, John White, b. Wright; C. S. Burgess, C. J. Holmes. George Pilling, William Richardson, William Roberts, Edwin Woods. Education Committee :—Messrs. W. Bibby, J. D. Buckton, A. J. Jolley, Thomas May, E. Plinston . Rev. Canon Steveoson,Hessrs. J. Upson,6l. Walker,Rev N. Wilson, H. Kingdon. Officials :—Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle Thomas Longdin, Jo‘an Fairhurst, Dr. Gornall, Luse Talbot, Dr. Smith, W. S. Haddock, Charles Madeley, James Dens, F. V. L. Mathias, Mr. J. Telfer, .1. Moore Marray,A. T. Hallawiiy, W. H. Woodcock, A. M. Ker,l E. Place, F. G. Ruddock, C. B. Aylward. Press: - The Editors of " Guardian," " Examiner," "Observer," and Messrs. R. Pier- point, M.P., Rev. Canon Willis, John Lister', A. H. Crosfield. Sir G. Greens'', William Long, Henry Greenall, C. E. Parker, Colonel Edelsten, Captain Crosfield, 3. Bolton, J. Longla.nd, F. R. Roberts, J. Monro,Vere B. Davies,R. L.Wynne Jones,W. B. Roberts, Ernest Parry, Rev. B. Percival, I. T. Williams, W. Fairhurst, Rev. E. C. E. Carlton, Rev. J. P. Whittle' W. Maddsn, Major Appleton, A. Browne. Dr. ialarkel, Peter Rylands, W. EL J. Jenkins, J. Yonne, G. Carruthers, Hugh Ferguson' T. Locker, E. Braide, Thos. Birtles, C. Bunch, Thomas Cooper, G. S. 'endlebury, W. Shirlavv, Wm. Fletcher, Walter Milner, J. Shillcock, Captain Avery, J. T.'"Foard, J. A. Hampson, A. Jollev, Sidney Bolton. Frank Bolton, Trinmas Cargill, D. Aukland, Ed. Draper, T. S. Steel, R. Wyllie. E. A. White, J. Wilkinson. Revs. H. B.. Firth and H. V. Pigot, Messrs. W. Rogers,Williara Skinner, W. R. Neil, Andrew Rath bone, Joseph H. Clarke, H. Bolton, John Sampson, M. Chambers, and J. Charles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LOYAL TOASTS.</head>
        <p>LOYAL TOASTS.</p>
        <p>In proposing the tunst of The Ring" the MAYOR referred specially to his Majesty's efforts on behalf of peace. The toast of "Queen Alexandra the Prince and Princess of Wales and other members of the Royal family" was also honoured on the proposition of the MAYOR.</p>
        <p>BISHOPS, CLERGY, AND OTHER MINISTERS OF RELIGION.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. PIERPOINT, M.P., who proposed this toast, said he would confine himself as regarded bishops to their own Bishop, the Bishop of Liverpool, who was a broad- minded bishop worthily filling the high office which he held. (Applause.) His own personal knowledge of Bishops was, he confessed,not very great. His know- ledge consisted in looking at them from a respectful distance in the House of Lords—(laughter)—which he sometimes visited in order to hear steady ant proper debates. (Renewed laughter.) As regarded the clergy of this town, there was not much difficulty in singing heir praises—(hear, hear) because he knew, as they all knew as well as he, that the clergy of this town to whatsoever denomination they belonged, worked well and amicably together for the good of all. (Applause.) There might be differences in the sects,but there was no difference to their desire for good. (Applause.) As an example of this, there was the Education Act which he believed had worked very smoothly in this town through the co-operation of men of all sects of religion, who had striven with the proper give and take on all sides to make the Act work well. (Hear, hear.) He thought they might say without undue pride that Warrington was in this respect, a pattern which might well be followed by less enlightened towns than theirs. (Hear, hear). The clergy worked amongst the poor quietly and whilst giving them the help they needed morally and spiritually he really believed they gave bodifylhelp as well. There was one point upon which there was absolute concurrence amongst ministers of all denominations in this town. That was in holding constant bazaars—(loud laughter)—and never failing sales of work—(renewed laughter)—and in never hiding the fact that they wanted subscriptions. (Continued laughter.) The RECTOR, in responding, said they had received with enthusiasm the toast of the Ring. They liked to think of the long line of his Majesty's predecessors reaching back a thousand years. Englishmen liked antiquity and he would remind them of the antiquity of the Episcopate. For centuries before the Conquest they knew that there were Bishops in the land. It was not, however, upon the antiquity of the Episco- pate that he wished to dwell but upon the adaptation of the Bishops to the neels of the present time. He did not think their predecessors would perhaps recognize or quite like the Bishops of modern times, so gnat had been the changes to meet the requirements of the day. The clergy all had their clearly defined spheres and tried to do their work to the best of their power. They recog- nized their brethren's rights and never interfered with them. There was a most cordial understanding between them all and the best possible feeling. With regard to the ministers they had not perhaps th 3 same ecclesias- tical and long spiritual lineage that the clergy had, but they admired them for their ability. piety, earnestness. and kindness towards the clergy. It was a great delight to him to believe that the English layman had a very high respect for the ministerial office. (Hear, hear). The layman knew what a man had to give up when he took holy orders. He might have advantages but he had very great disabilities, and the fact that men shrunk from taking holy orders showed that they felt there were things they had to deny themselves of when they became clergymen. The layman, he believed had also a very deep regard for the minister in whom he believed, when he saw that the minister was doing his best and working. (Applause.) The Rev. Father WitsoN also responded. He said it was certainly the desire of their superiors in the Church, of the clergy,and indeed of the Catholic laity, that they should come forward and take their part in the public life of the civic, community to which they belonged— (hear, hear)—that they should bear their share of the burdens and take their part in the labonrs, the objects of which were the general good, the welfare and pros- perity of the community in which they lived. He was delighted to be able to acknowledge that at least in recent times that co-operation on their part had been heartily welcomed by the local authorities and by all those agencies which had for their object the amelioration of the com- munity. He was very glad the Borough -member referred to that point at which the contact between the local authority and the ecclesiastical was perhaps most intimate, namely, the administration of the Education Act. It was a great pleasure to him (Father Wilson) to have that opportunity of expressing the satisfaction of the Catholic community at the manner in which the administration of the Act had been carried out in Warrington. (Applause.) It had been administered under their large minded committee and its tactful and considerate director in such a way that none of them could say they had a grievance. It seemed to him that if the Act had been administered with the same smoothness, and in the same conciliatory spirit throughout the country, it would have been a very happy thin.'' indeed. (Hear, hear.) It would have been good for education and a great delight to those who were truly aud firstly educationists. It was cer- tainly his desire—and he thought he could speak with all confidence for his coal reres—that the kindly feeling between all the members of the civic community should if possible, be intensified and extended as well as main- tained. (Applause).</p>
        <p>THE ARMY,, NAVY, AND AUXILIARY FORCES." SPEECH BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Mr. CRO.3FIELD said he felt that these were others present who, with their expert knowledge would be much better qualified to do justice to this important toast than himself, in fact a few minutes previously he hinted this to his Worship, adding that he did not yet know enough about the subject. " That, replied his friend, is exactly why I have put you down for the toast." (Laughter). Well, he (Mr. Crosfleld) would do his best, though it would be entirely out of place if he were to attempt to do more than touch in a very general way on the great topic which formed the subject of the toast, and to which they all attached the utmost import- ance. (Rear, hear.) It struck him that speaking even in general terms it would be difficult to deal with the question of the Army, Navy and Auxiliary Forces on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>TAKE IN EVERY WORD</head>
        <p>TAKE IN EVERY WORD</p>
        <p>Who says?" asked Mr. OaChwaite. .. _</p>
        <p>"Why, Oiithwaite—every wOrd-O-atliwaite says," said the man in surprise. "But I AM Outhwaite," said the candidate for West Birmingham. Surprise does not aptly descrity the e^aotion which affected the two worthy Conservatives, for such, of course, they turned out to be, and after they had recovered sufficient presence of mind they bundled out of the cab a deal faster, even, than they had bundled into it. "Of course, you don't expect us to pay for the cab?" was their parting remark, and they tore back to recover the right man, the man who, it began to appear, had been sent up from London to attend the Wetherby meeting and "take in every word" of Mr. Oathwaite's. The story does not end here. After nearly getting into a carriage sent to meet our mysterious Tory at Wetherby, Mr. Outhwaite at last actually reached the place of meeting. Here a Liberal came up to him and said: "I have heard that the Tories are sending up a man from street, London, to interrupt you, and break up the meeting."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>TOM; THE WHOLE TALE.</head>
        <p>TOM; THE WHOLE TALE.</p>
        <p>_ This, indeed, shed a new light on things. The street named is the headquarters of some of the great mining firms of the Rand.</p>
        <p>But now the meeting was waiting, and the speakers ascended the platform. In a little knot of men near a door Mr. Outhwaite spied a man who hut travelled by the same train as he. lie told the audience of his Leeds experience ; they roared with laughter ; he told them the whole tale, how the mine owners had actually taken the trouble to send a man all the way from London. Then he said, "He is here in this hall—there."</p>
        <p>There WU an uproar: " He's not Yorkshire, Bring him out," " Kick him out," " Nieto, him get on the platform," and such like suggestions were made. How- ever, Lkfr. Outhwaite continued the even tenor of his way, even answaring an enormous list of questions handed up anonymously by our London hero, who, however, when the name of the questioner was called for, dared not reply. •</p>
        <p>A London firm has had returned to them through the Dead Letter Office a letter addressed to a customer who had died. On the front was written " Dead." On the back there appeared "Not delivered; present address not known."</p>
        <p>A dispute has arisen in New York about the sex of angels. A sculptor was commissioned to execute for a cathedral an Angel of the Annunciation and an Angel of the Resurrection. He sent in two female figures and these were objected to, the cathedral authorities point- ing out that angels have always been held to be mascu- line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>"THE MIKADO."</head>
        <p>"THE MIKADO."</p>
        <p>Mr. Therniey Dadge and Next Week's</p>
        <p>Performances.</p>
        <p>There is every indication that Warrington people are looking forward with considerable pleasure to the performances of "The Mikado" to be given in the Parr Hall each evening of next week- by members of the Musical Society. Of all the Gilbert-Sullivan collaborations " The Mikado" probably ranks highest in popularity. It is a delightful comic opera, brimful 'of life and gaiety, and a capable production keeps an audience in a state of continual merriment. Toe two notable successes which the Warringtoa amateurs have already achieved in "The Yeomen of the Guard "and The Gondoliers" have inspired them to equal enthusiasm in the rehearsals for next week's perform- ances. On Wednesday evening an " Examiner" ' representative had a chat with Mr. Thornley Dodge who, as stage manager and dramatic instructor, fulfils an important part in these operatic enterprises. The society is to be congratnbated on again securing his services, for unquestionably a large measure of the credit for previous triumphs is due to him. Mr. Thornley Dodge appeared delighted with the progress made at the rehersals. In answer to a pre- liminary remark he said the opera was "going very strong indeed." While the principals who had played before were quite up to their previous standard—if anything he thought they excelled it—he believed the new-corners would acquit themselves most satis- factorily and give very great pleasure. On the efforts of the entire company Mr. Thomley Dodge had nothing but praise to bestow. " They have been as willing as ever " he said " and have given me the right good time I have had here on former occasions. They have done everything I wanted them to do, and they are not the slightest bit nervous about the performances. Of course, there is a little natural anxiety, but as hr any fear of anyone not: coming up to form, that can be entirely ignored. They are excellent this year." And what can you say as to the cast ?" " It is of course, a very small one " was the reply " and wants a tremendous amount of careful playing, but the company have got into that very quickly." Mr. Thoraley Dodge naturally took occasion to express his unqualified satisfaction with last year's production of" The Gondoliers." He declared that it was really charming, one of the chief factors of its success being that the vivacity, which was the first essential, was so well sustained throughout. " Do you think the production of 'The Mikado' will equal last year's opeia ?" asked the interview.r. "Yes I believe it will —in fact lam hoping that it will excel The Gondoliers.' I should like to find this year's production go one better than the other two!' _ _ .</p>
        <p>Incidentally Mr. Thornley Dodge meationed that he had recently appeared as Ko-Ko in the Halifax Society's per °nuances of " The Mikado." The part is one of which he is particularly fond. The experi- ence gained in producing the opera at Halifax has, of course, proved of great assistance to him in the reheorsals at Warrington. Again alluding to the Warrington company Hr. Thontley Dodge said that in his opinion "the voices are finer this year than they have ever been.l am again prepared to yield the palm to the Varriagton operatic company for the finest singing of any amateurs it has been my lot to coach" Mr. Thornley Dodge expressed his great regret that owing to the painfully sudden death of her father,kliss Mabel Henshall would, of course, be unable to take part in this year's performances. He was sure the sym- pathy of the whole town would be with her. They were much indebted to Mrs. C. Garton Stone who had come forward to take Miss Henshall's place and had proved an entirely capable and satisfactory substitute. Our readers will no doubt be interested to know that Mr. Thornley Dodge is now devoting his time' h.ore particularly to concert work and in regard to amateur opera, is limiting himself to the coaching of six societies this season. He will shortly hove in hand " Merrie England" at Bury, "San Toy" at Manchester, and " Dorothy " at Halifax.</p>
      </div>
