      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WYCLIFFE COCKHEDGE MISSION.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE COCKHEDGE MISSION.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last the Varvest Thanksgiving Services were held in the above Mission. A children's service was held in the morning conducted by Mi. C. Eccles. In the afternoon a sacred caatata, "Gathered Grain," was rendered by the choir. The chair was taken by Mr. Ziba Armitage, who also delivered a short address. The preacher at the evening service was the Rev. J. S. Hughes, of Hal Cliffe, who preached on the parable of the unfruitful vine. At both the afternoon and even- ing services the mission was filled almost to overflowing. The collections were taken on behalf of the mission fund. The proceedings on Mon- day evening took the form of a fruit banquet and entertainment. The Rev. H. Wall presided over a large and appreciative audience. At the close, a hearty vote of thonks was accorded to all who had !taken part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Order of Druids.</head>
        <p>Order of Druids.</p>
        <p>-- --- The quarterly meeting of Warrington Equalized District was held at the Blue Lion Hotel on Thursday night. District Grand Master William Hankey pre- sided, being supported by the Deputy Master John Woods, the trustees, William Bibby, George Rough- ley, and Samuel Hankey, the treasurer, Robert Perdue. secretary Edwin Allen, and representatives of 13. Lodges forming the District.—The Secretary, in pre- senting his third quarterly balance sheet, congrata-- lated the District upon its splendid financial position- Never in the Society's history had the funds been in such a healthy condition. They now amounted to nearly £2,500,and were steadily increasing quarter by quarter, the present one showing an increase of .03. Heltad one 'egret only, and that was that the 'lumen- ! cal strength of the District did not improve as he should like to see. However, he hoped for the best.</p>
        <p>Inquiries at Wigan, Leigh, Hindley, Bolton, and Manchester show 'that the Lancashire coal trade is now steadily improving, fewer than 15,000 miners and surface workers out of 70,000 and odd employed in the County Palatine being employed less than five days per week.</p>
      </div>
