stealing. by means en Downstairs a book room, where e c of a trick, a pair of boots of the value of Bs. 11d., the documents, &amp;c., will be stored, a stationery room property of the Public Benefit Boot Company.—The where letters will he opened and dispatched, and a Chief Constable said the case was a very painful one. large agents' meeting room complete the accommoda- tion. To the public as well as to the Company the The prisoner would he known to sevaral of the magis- o trates as a respectably connected man. He had not offices must prove a great convenience, and it is a resided in Warrington for 16 or 17 years, except during source of additional pleasure that the company hope, the last two years. On Saturday evening he was in the Seven Stars public-house, and when he had rim short of in co-operation with local effort, to beautify the square money he deliberately forged an order in the name of , which faces the station. his brother-in-law, who was a solicitor, and sent a man! From the Warriugton centre, the working is con- named Higgins to the Public Benefit Boot Company trolled of all goad stations west of Warrington up to with it. The result was that the boots were obtained Huyton and Runcorn, on the east of Warrington by. means of the order, there being no suspicion what- -rozi 13 dheath and Altrineham, on the north Wigan, Preston, ever, as the solicitor was a customer. The prisoner Blirkpool. Fleetwood, Lmgridge, Chorley, then sent Higgins to pawn the hoots, but Mr. Domville Bolton, Blackburn Leigh and Bedford, Tyldesley, and the pawnbroker, refused to take the boots in, and the police were communicated with. Inquiries were made, i Atherton, and the N..rth Western interest at East Lan- and the prisoner was arrested. The solicitor was cashire Stations; such as Burnley, Acerington,Darwen, astonished when the facts were told to him, its he had : &amp;c. All questions relating to rates, claims, accounts, not seen the prisoner to speak to for a number of years. i- traders season tickets, cartage, and the employment of The prisoner admitted that he conceived the idea of the I staff, arc dealt with from this centre. theft in order to supply himself with more drink. He had, however, been very honourable, if one might use DINNER AT THE LION HOTEL. the term, as he had taken all the responsibility upon PRESENTATION TO MR. I. T. WILLIAMS. himself by saying that Higgins was not awaee that the Although he has only been in charge of the work at letter was not genuine. The solicitor had not Warrington for a comparatively short periodNir. I. T. given the prisoner any authority whatever.—The Williams,the district goods manager,has succeeded in gain 1 , ing the cordial esteem of his statf.The opening of the now prisoner 'admitted that he had written the note. but ' offices presented a fitting opportunity of giving practical that he did so to obtain money for lodgings and food. e —The magistrates fined the prisoner 205., or in default expression to the good feelings entertained towards of payment 14 days' imprisonment. _ him, and on Saturday evening Mr. Williams was the T, -</p>
        <p>I'HE SINGULAR CASE Or ATI'EMPTED SUICIDE. At the Borough Police Court, on Tuesday morning, before Dr. Fox (presiding) nnd other magistrates, William Hewitt, 11, Albert-terrace, was charged on remand with attempting to commit suicide by throwing himself into the Mersey, at Howley, on ctober 2nd. —The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner seemed to be more collected in his mind since he had been in custody, and also appeared in better health. If their Worships would recall the circumstances, they would remember that it was more a freak than an attempt to commit suicide. If any of his reditivei would take care of him he would withdraw the case. The prisoner's wife said she was prepared to take her husband back. again and look after him. Prisoner, who promised to be teetotal, and try and get work at once, was dis- charged.</p>
        <p>guest at a complimentary dinner at the Lion Hotel. During the evening he was the recipient of a handsome present, consisting of framed photographs of every member of the staff, while hp also received, on behalf of Mrs. Williams, a pair of solid silver candlesticks. The gathering was a highly successful one, the attend- ance being large, and representative of numerous depsrtments of railway work. Mr. D. Banks presided and the vice-chair was occupied by Mr. J. B. Parkhouse Amongst others present were :—Messrs. W. Williams, chief store-keeper, St. Helens; W. H. Williams, Wolverhampton; T. Al. Fowke, dist. engineer, Liver- pool; .1. Tarver, Wigan ; H. Yates, Preston; H. Town- ley, Warrington ; J. Berry, Tyldesley; H. Nickson, Preston; D. Haalem, Earlestown ; W. Trafford, New- tot ;A. H. Tinnion, Prescot ; P. 'l'. Newbold, J. Heaps, Wigan ;_ W..Heid,Burnley; H. Hunt,. Atherton; T. A.</p>
        <p>EMBEZZLEMENT BY A CARTER. On Tuesday at the Warrington Police Court John Makin. who resides at the back of 113, Orford-lane was charged with embezzling the aura of 7s. 10d. on October 2nd, 3s. Gd. on October 6th and 7s. 10d. on October 7th, moneys of Mr. William Henry Bailey, coal dealer, Forster-street and Orford-lane.—Mr. Steel prosecuted on behalf of Mr. Bailey, and said the prisoner had been in prosecutor's employ for about sixteen months as a lurryman and it was the custom of Mr. Bailey to have waggons of coal sent to his order at the siding of Messrs. Fletcher, Burrows and Company in Dallam-lane. So much coal had to to be weighed during the course of the day and when Makin returned at night he had to state how much coal he had taken out. Mr. Bailey had suspected from time to time a shortage of money and he asked the prisoner about it, but an execuse was always made. On Saturday last Mr. Bailey's sus- picion was again aroused and he compared the amount of coal taken out by the prisoner and the amount accounted for by him. On October 2nd, the prisoner took out four tons nine cwt , but he only accounted for four tons, and if he had sold the nine cwt. he must have pocketed the money, which amounted to 7s. iod. On October 6th he took out two tans fifteen cwt., but he only accounted for two tons eleven cwt.. pocketing 3s. Gd. On Saturday he took out four tons fourteen cwt., and only accounted for four tons- five cwt., thus making 7s. 10d. for himself. Prisoner admitted that he had been getting the coal for his own use, and said he had been doing so for a fortnight. Mr. Bailey was extremely sorry to prosecute, but he had been robbed so much that he was bound to bring the case into Court as a warning. He had reason to believe that the money had been spent in gambling, as he understood the prisoner had once lost a business of his own through tlie same cause.—Prisoner was fined 40s. or in default one month's imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
        <p>_ , Smith, Leigh ;W. Scholefield, Hindley; T. Fortune, Bolton ; H.Eyden. St. Helens ,• R. Bolton, T. flor- rabin, A. Wilson, A. E. Cresswell, and the following members of the Warrington staff : Messrs. R. Parkinson, R. Bell. E. Robinson, J. T. Whittle C. Eccles, B. W. Penson, T. R. Seddon,F.G. Ellison, d. Taylor, R. Sutton, C. Parkinson, R. F. Littlewood, F. Atkinson, W. Hall, W. 'Burdett, J. Callow, A. Allman G. G. Easketter, W. G. Limb, A. Porter.r H. araide, J. H. Williams. C. B. Chappell, A. NV. Anderton, and J.Dalan. Mr. J.Bennett was prevented at the last moment from attending owing to sudden illness in his family. An excellent dinner was served by the proprietor(Mr. John Lee) and afterwards a smoking cnnoert was held. On the proposition of the Chairman the toast of" The King "was heartily honoured and the musical pro- gramme was proceeded with. THE PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>Later in the evening, the CritlßM \N, in fitting terms, proposed the toast of the evening " Our guest." He ! said that when Mr. Williams came into the Warrington district a little over two years ago, a complete stranger 'tn them, his first speech to the staff created-a good ' impression. Amongst other things Mr. Williams said that" whilst safeguarding the interests of the North- Western Company, which always must be first, he would not be unmindful of his duty to those under his charge." (Hear, hear.) After the lapse of that time he (Mr. Banks) ventured to say, without the least fear of contradiction, that the promise then given had been fulfilled to the very utmost. (Applause). In the first place Mr. Williams began by making improvements at home, where all good things ought to start. Of course, at home. he (the speaker) meant in his own offices. On the first opportunity which presented ttself —that was lon the retirement of his (Mr. Ranks's) predecessor— important changes were made which benefited nearly ' the whole of the members of Mr. Williams's personal stall.—(hear, hear),—besides providing additional assist- ance. The next step was to look about for better office accommodation and now,after several months of anxious and untiring effort on the part of their guest, they had, in the words of their chief goods manager who had visited the place, " premises which were a credit to the North Western Company." He might further say that if th 3 offices were not the finest, they were second to none on the North Western line. (Hear, hear). In addition to all this, Mr. Williams had helped in the arrangement of their annual picnic, and they had also been entertained right royally at. Lea Hurst on two occasions. (Applause). Having received so many acts of kindness, they thought the commemoration of the opening of the new offices a fitting opportunity of show- ing their appreciation of all that had been done for them, and he would now, on behalf of the staff, ask Mr Williams to accept the framed portraits. (Applause) In deciding to make that presentation, the staff had not been unmindful of Mrs. Williams. (Applans:?..) They all knew how kind she had been to all of them on all occasions, and had seconded the efforts put forth for their benefit, lie had very much pleasure in asking Mr. Williams to accept for her 3 beautiful pair of solid silver candlesticks. The little mementoes were accom- panied by the best wishes of the staff,and they hoped Mr. and Mrs. Williams might be spared in the enjoy- ment of good health,for many years to come. (Applause) The CHAIRMAN then handed the gifts to Mr. Williams amid loud applause. The picture contains twenty- seven separate portraits of the staff and is an admirable specimen of the photographer's art. It is the work of Mr. T. Birtles and bears the following inscription : "Presented to Mr. I. T. Williams, district goods manager of the London and , North-Western Railway at Warrington, by the members of his personal staff as a mark of respect ane esteem and also in commemoration of the opening of the new district offices at Arpley Station. September 1U05." MR. WILLIAMS'S REPLY. THE BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. I. T. Wurtams, in accepting the gift.;, said the generous reception they had given the toast,together with the many kind things Mr. Banks had said, made it very difficult for him to thank them as he would wish. That he was keenly sensible of the kindness which underlay all that they had said and done went without saying. When he received the invitation , siened by the members of the staff, to dine with them this evening Iho must confess he was taken somewhat aback. The thought which prompted them to approach him in that way appealed to him more than he could say, and al- though personally he did not much care for public work of that character there were times when a man must put aside his own private feelings,and he felt that that was one of those occasions. As the Chairman had already pointed out, it was about two and a half years</p>
      </div>
