      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 14, 1905. NEW RAILWAY OFFICES.,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 14, 1905. NEW RAILWAY OFFICES.,</p>
        <p>ago since he (Mr. Williams) came to Warrington to take charge of this district. He was then a complete stranger to them all. He too, remembered the !sleeting to which the Chairman alluded, which com- prised a large representation of the staff, and he then asked those present for their assistance in working what was admittedly a large and intricate district. That they had given not only readily, but cheerfully, and if only for that reason, he was only too pleased to have the opportunity of acknowledging their loyalty, and thanking them tor their assistance. They had during the period mentioned initiated and worked out. many schemes, having for their object the welfare of the public as well as the interests of the Company, and from the numerous letters he had from time to time received from various sources, they had good reasons for saying that their efforts had been attended with some measure of success. At the same time he recognised that this could not have been accomplished without the co-operation of the staff. He thanked them sincerely for their hospitality, and especially for presenting him with such an artistic and interesting souvenir of the occasion, which would fled an abiding place in his home. He likewise thanked them for the handsome present to his e ife, which had come upon him with so much surprise that words failed him to express what he felt. The gift appealed to him most keenly because he knew that any little sudeess he might have achieved in life was in no small way due to the help he had received from her. (Applause.) The photos, which were excellent, and reflected every credit upon the artist, would in the digtant future, if he was spared, bring many pleasant memories, of which that evening would not be the least. lie trusted he might see many of those, whose faces were represented in the group, occupying some of the high positio is in the service of the Company. The younger members he would urge to take every advantage of the present opportuuity of qualifying for those appoint- ments which were ahead. There was no royal road to success, and without work they could not hope to succeed. The way might ho crowded, and would become more so as competitors increased, but they should remember there was plenty of room at the top, and any help be could render in that direction would be readily giemi. It was scarcely necessary for him to say that he was only too pleased to have been the medium in obtaining for them the improved office accommodation, which was very much needed. It had taken a long time to effect, but the result more than compensated for the apparent delay. The work through- out, thanks to the district engineer, Mr. Fowke, and to Mr. Williams, St. Helens, had been done most thoroughly, and the appointments all through the building were such as would allow of their now work- ing'uncrer conditions both healthful and pleasant. in fact, the arrangements would compare most favourably with any of the District Manager's offices throughout the system. (Applause.) He would like to add that the Chief Goods Manager from Easton, Mr. Frank Free, after making his inspection, uot only expressed his satisfaction with all that had been clone, but his pleasure in seeing the staff so comfortably provided for. To that gentleman they were greatly indebted for the personal interest he bad from the first taken in the work. Looking at the change from the old to the new offices from a traders' point of ' view—and this he could assure them had been very carefully considered—he ventured to think that the public would bathe first to recognise and fully appreciate the conveniences and facilities the . company were now offering to them in the conduct of ' their business. It would at least, he thought, satisfy I them that the North Western were not unmindful of the wants and requirements of this important town with which they were so closely associated, and in which they had a big financial interest, it might not be generally known that the North Western Company were by far the largest ratepayers in the Borough of Warrington, to say nothing of their contributions to the counties of Cheshire and Lancashire. It therefore followed that it was to their interest to foster, build up, and develop in every possible way, the trade of the district generally, and Warrington in particular The interests of the one were inseparably bound up with the other. In fact, they were mutual, and it was in that spirit the company desired to conduct all their negotiations with the trading public and in that respect I he knew he could rely upon the hearty co-operation of the staff. There was one other matter to which he would like briefly to allude. They were all aware that I a Society had recently been formed in Waraington for the purpose of improving the appearance of the streets as far as could be done by the planting of trees, shrubs, &amp;c. That movement had his earnest support, and he invited them to give it theirs. If each man would individually do his part however small, the object in view would be attained. (Applause.) They had he thought, made a very good start by beautifying the interior of an old but imposing building, which was closely associated with the early history of railways in Warrington,and before long they hoped to see the exterior suitably renovated, and made much more attractive to the eye than it was at present. (Applause.) Opposite the building there was one of the few open spaces which the town possessed, and in his opinion it could at a minimum cost be materially improved by the adoption of the scheme in question. The space referred to was specially ada?ted for the planting of trees, and he had already put himself in communication with the town authorities, who would, he was quite sure, be only too ready to co-operate with them in the matter. They had likewise in hand the question of providing evergreen shrubs for placing retind the bate of the guns in the centre of the open space, which were placed there some years ago by a. family whose name had been prominently and honourably associated with the history of Warring- I ton for generations past. In this and other ways they I hoped to shew in a practical manner their readiness to I respond to the lippeal of those gentlemen, who were to be commended for taking the work in hand. In con-, elusion, be would again, on behalf of Mrs. Williams and himself, thank the staff for the hononr they had paid ' him that evening, and ho would also like to acknowledge his indebtedness to those gentlemen who had come from the distant parts of Lancashire to take part in the meeting. He could assure them he no longer felt a stranger in their midst, but one whose good fortune it was to possess their confidence and good will, and he trusted it might he his privilege to retain these to the end. (Applause). "THE LONDON AND NORTFI-WESTERN RAILWAY CO." Mr. WILLIAMS, chief of the stores department at St. Helens Junction, proposed the toast of " The London and North-Western Railway Co." lie thanked the staff very much for allowing him to join with them in pay- ing atribute of esteem to Mr. Williams. From what he (the speaker) had seen of him, Mr. Williams was well worthy of being supported. (Applause.) He was to be heartily congratulated on having persuaded the directors to allow them to oscupy the premises at Arpley. It ook a good deal of persuading, especially at the present time, to induce directors to spend any money, and he did not know how Mr. Williams managed it. (Laughter.) In referring to the toast,elr. Williams pointed out that the welfare of those present was bound up with the progress of the Company who,he believed, were the finest employers of labour in the country if not in the whole world. (Applause.) To manage the London and North-Western Railway Com- pany was like governing a country, and Uo thought the mere fact of being in the service of such is great con- cern was something to be proud of. (Applause.) The toast was he, rti I y honoured. In responding, Mr. EYDEN ( St. Helens) said they did not need any statistics to prove that the Company was not defunct or that it was not even in a moribund state, notwithstanding all the criticisms they had heard recently. The speaker referred to the benefits which scorned frost the training which boys received in the service of the company. They should not look at the batter altogether from the salary point of view but weigh all the other advantages and reflect &amp;St° what their position would have been if they had cot entered the employ of the company. (Applause). " AD ENITS AND CANVASSERS."</p>
        <p>This toast was proposed in suitable terms by :klr. P. Bell of Warrington; who referred to the kindly feeling existing between tips agents and canvassors and the district manager end his staff. In responding Mr. Fortune, of Bolton. said the agents and canvassers were really the backbone of the company. (Laughter.) He proceeded to pay a tribute to Mr. E. Braide, the late manager, and referred to several kindly acts he had experienced at his hands. Mr. Fortune concluded by stating that the good conferred by the removal of the offices to Arpley was incalculable. He was glad that Mr. Williarn3 had not allowed his rapid stops to high office to stunt his interest in those around</p>
        <p>him, and he (the speaker) was voiciog the feelings of the entire district when he stid that while Mr. Williams's advent amongst them was heartily welcomed, his stay had kindled a feeling not only of regard, but of loyalty and love. He hoped that before long they would be called to celebrate Mr. ‘Villiatus' promotion to a higher office which they were satisfied his ability and tact would fully merit. (Applause). Mr. Sm Int (Leigh and Bedford) also responded.</p>
        <p>'" Tait VISITORS."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. B. ParacßousE, Warrington, proposed this toast and expressed regret that Mr. Turnbull, the dis- trict superintendent,was unable to be present. lie (the speaker) was a new corner in the district and he desired to thank Mr. and Mrs. Williams and the staff for the kindly way in which he had been received. (Applause). Mr. BOGTON, stationmaster at Warrington, responded, and took the opportunity of thanking Mr. Williams for the valuable assistance he had given in helping him to obtain much-need alterations in the passenger service. Mr. CRESWELL, of the locomotive department, also spoke and expressed the hope that there would be in- creased trallic for his department to deal with.</p>
        <p>" THE CHAIRMAN."</p>
        <p>Mr. T. T. WELLTAMi submitted in eulogistic terms the toast of the Chairman (Mx. D. Banks), and con- gratulated the staff in having at the held of the office arrangements a gentleman who believed in example rather than precept; one whose word was his bond, and to whom all of them could look with confidence and respect. (Applause.) His stability of character, and high sense of honour, were the distinguishing features of his life, and whether he (.11r. Williams) was present c.r absent from the office, he always felt that the busi- ness of the company was being well and closely attended (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The toast was accorded with musical honours and Mr. Banks,who on rising to respond was given a most cordial welcome, briefly returned thanks. An admirable musical programme was submitted, all the items being well received. Songs were given In a highly creditable manner by Mr. T. linmphreys, Mr. E. Robinson Mr. J. H. Draper, Mr. D. Crossley, and Mr. J. T. WhAtle, and a well-executed pianoforte auet by Messrs. R. Sutton and F. Atkinson was much appreci- ated. Mr. W. H. Williams is an accomplished violinist and his solos, which were most artistically given, delighted the assembly, an encore being accorded. Mr. A. Grounds provided the humorous element and created great mirth with his songs and sketches. Mr. F. Atkinson was an able accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>OPENING OF THE L. &amp; N.W. RAILWAY CO'S. ARPLEY PREMISES.</head>
        <p>OPENING OF THE L. &amp; N.W. RAILWAY CO'S. ARPLEY PREMISES.</p>
        <p>Complimentary Dinner to Mr. I. T.</p>
        <p>VlilHams.</p>
        <p>I The opening of the handsome new district offices of the London and North Western Railway Company, over Arpley Station marks an important development in the local history of the company. For sometime the need for improved accommodation has been severely felt, the old offices near Bank Quay bridge having become inadequate to cope with the great amount of work which has to be dealt with. Largely owing to the exertions of the district goods manager (Mr. I. T. Williams), the company have been induced to transfer the offices to the rooms above Arpley Station where the most ample accommodation is provided. For about twelve months workmen have been engaged in fitting the premises for occupation and the staff are now comfortably installed in their new quarters. A finer suite of rooms for the purpose it would, indeed, be difficult to obtain. The decoration and furnishing have been admirably carried out and, what is of greater importance, the alterations ensure that the buildings are thoroughly well lighted. It is interesting to note that the new offices were origin- ally built by the \Warrington and Stockport and the St. Helens Railway and Canal Companies combined, which two Companies were taken over by the London and North-Western Railway Company about the year 1861. -Arpley Station was the headquarters of these two old companies, hence the construction of the corn. modious offices now brought into use again by the Warrington and District Goods Manager's Staff. A representative of the "Examiner " has had the 'pleasure of inspecting the newly-opened offices this week. The principal departments are on the upper ff. yor. At the far end of the corridor is a comfortable _ _ _ _</p>
      </div>
