      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The uswil meeting of this society %vas held on Sunday. Mr. O. Owen presided and urged the brethren to be followers of Christ. Mr. J. Layfield, of Manchester, spoke upon "He delighteth in mercy," His happy illustrations of mercy and delight were greatly enjoyed Rey. John Yonge read the lesson and led the devotional exercise. Mr. W. J. Bains sang in splendid style "Cathedral voices "and "By Jesus' grave" Two new members were welcomed. There was a fair attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street.</head>
        <p>Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street.</p>
        <p>On Sunday large congregations attended the anniver- sary services in the above church. Mr. B. Richards° (of Liverpool) was the preacher, taking for his text i the morning "What think ye of these things?" and in the evening, "Thou shalt he saved." Sp-mial hymns and anthems were rendered during the day by the choir under the leadership of Mr..N. Cook. Miss Fairhurst sweetly rendered "The raft." To the afternoon a inmical service was held, the speaker being the Rev. J. F. Matthews, who gave an address to a large congregation urging them to take a firm stand for Christ and to go out and preach to the people in the streets. Solos Were given by Miss L. Fairhurst, Mr. S. Millington and Mr. S. Hassell. and were highly appreciated. [he organist was Miss Plinston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Legh7street Prim Wye Methodist Chapel.</head>
        <p>Legh7street Prim Wye Methodist Chapel.</p>
        <p>Special services in celebration of the Christian Endeavour anniversary were held in the above place of xvorship oh Sunday. the special preacher was the Rev. J. Wigley, of Liverpool, who in the morning took for his text" And upon the top of the pillars was lily work" (I. Kings vii. 22). The pillars referred to, said the speaker, were those of Solomon's temple. They were there not only for support but for ornament. They combieed beauty and strength. And had nut God wisely ordained that these two great qualities should be combined in this earth of ours ? The rugged rocks wera decked with soft mosses, and the mountains and hills covered with grasses and delicate flowers. It was true also of tte home life. They saw the brawny toil- ing man and the graceful woman. These qualities were typical of the higher realm. Christianity was a beauti- religion, but it was essentially a religion of strength. In the evening the rev. gentleman delivered a discon rse on "Daniel : his obedience to the dictates of his conscience." The choir rendered the chorus " And the glory." Another meeting was held on the MondAy nicht at which Hr. VVigley delivered an address, the Rev. C. L. Stowe presiding, while Miss A. Woodland rendered a solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Missionary Meeting in the Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>Missionary Meeting in the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening when the annual public meeting in connection with the Warrington Branch of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts was held. Mr. John White presided over a good attendance and was supported on the platform by the Rev. Canons Willis and Morley Stevenson, Rev. S. J. Sykes (Liver- pool,. organizing secretary for the Archaeaconry of Warrington) the Rev. Dr. Marks (late of Rangoon), and the Revs. J. R. Jones, W. Pollock-Hill, T. T. 11,ee Jones, J. H. G. Hollingworth, E. C. E. Carleton, B. Percival, H R. Wild, T. W. Barber, A. Inglis, H. A. Lester, C. A. M. Evans, G. Vale Owen, and W. J. Ridpath.—The Chairman said the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel, had existed, for more than 200 years. He thought he was right in saying that this great society and its kindred society—the Church Missionary Society —sup- ported no fewer than 1,500 clergymen abroad, and also some 16,000 lay workers, to say nothing of the various missionary stations, churches and other appurtenances of missionary work. There wore many di qiculties to be contended with. There was the indifference and apathy which existed on all sides as regarded missionary work. Then there was the man who said that we ought not to interfere with the religion of other people ; that it was cruel and unkind to upset their religious! beliefs ; and that for all we knew the Mohammedan and the Hindoo might be KS good men as :we ourselves were. There appeared to be only one , answer to give to the men who spoke in that way and ' called himself a Christian. and that was to use the words of the Saviour himself, when be commanded His ' Apostles to •" Go ye into all nations." (Hear, hear.) Another'argument was that so long as there were so many heathens at home we need not go abroad to work. Undoubtedly the calls at home grew greater and greater and there was no doubt there were many people here at home who were on the very verge of heatheudom, and who required every possible attention that could be given them. But there was this in it. The people here at home had en opportunity of hearing the Gospel if they desired, hut on the other hand the heathens abroad had never seen a church in most instances and never heard the word of our Saviour. We must not allow those arguments to interfere with our missionary enter- prise because if we did, it was a sign that we were losing our hold and that our faith was growing week. —lnteresting addresses were delivered by the Rev. S. J. Sykes and Dr. Marks, the last-named relating his ex- periences during 43 years' mission work in Burmah.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SPENT HIS MASTER'S MONEY.</head>
        <p>SPENT HIS MASTER'S MONEY.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Saturday, Martin Rowan, hawker, SVarbarton street was sentenced to fourteen days in the second division for embezzling £3 135., from his employer, Fred Bailey, gieengrocer, carrying on business in the Market place. Rowan was sent out with a horse and cart to hawk vegetables in the Warrington neighbourhood, but did not return to hand over the proceeds. Lite at night the horse was found in the stables with its knees broken. Rowan had decamped to Liverpool, where he spent the money in drink.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Annual fkating.</head>
        <p>Annual fkating.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Podmore, jun., pregided over the annual meet- ing of the WArrington Trades and Labour Council which was held in Boid.stree:. \Vesleyan schools on Tuesday evening. There was a fair attendance.</p>
        <p>PAYMENT OF LABOUR REPRESENTATION COMMITTEE DELEGATE'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. GrtEILEv said he did not think it WKS fair that the Council should expect two delegates to attend every meeting of the Labour Representation Committee and waste valuable time without being paid. Ho moved that the delezates be paid at the same rate as the mem- bers of the Executive Committee, uaruely, sixpence per meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. IYCLIACK seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
