      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>:APTAIN DUCK.</head>
        <p>:APTAIN DUCK.</p>
        <p>[Y DEAR CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>I have received several interesting letters this week, and I have had a few printed below. I fear I could not print any of Jessie Bond's drawings, but I am always pleased to receive them, or work of any description that any of you like to send me. I have sent a beautiful box of Fry's chocolates to J. A. Knight, as he eent in correct solutions to puzzles. I will give another box of chocolates to the one who sends in correct answers to the puzzles given below. I must not write a long letter this week, or there will be no room for the letters of my\ nephews and nieces. With love to all,</p>
        <p>From your afiectiotatz</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>Dear • Uncle ed,—Thank you for the prize you sent me. I am very pleased with it. I have shown it to my friends, and they all think I am a very lucky girl. I do not wish to have tale dray:hip returned, as you wile:id. Will you please try to print the best drawing sent in each week, as I should like to see • some of them? I will send you in a week or so some of my original designs in brushwork.</p>
        <p>I remain, Your Loving Niece, Jessie B. PS.—I was very pleased to see my last letter in print.</p>
        <p>Burton-on-Trent.</p>
        <p>A.ptain Duck was a Modoe Indian, with the Jrtest possible legs. His lees were so short at when he walked he waddled along like very fat duck. And that is why he was called tptain Duck at the stage station, which was the foot of the great white mountain in the Lear. of the Modoc oeuntry, Mount Shasta. lonze said his legs had been shot off in a )attle. And then some said his legs had been .aten off by a bear. But I do,not very well see iow that could be:• for his feet were there, all right. And very big feet they were, too; wide and big and flat like ducks' feet. So I think he must have been born that way. , • Poor Captain Duck could not hunt very well, or go on the war-path with the other Indians, and so he came to the stage station, to hire out, with the few rough men who kept )ne old log, fort and took care of the stage horses there. These men did not like the old Indian but' as They were a lazy set, they were glad to have him at the fort to rub down and water the stage horses when the sun was hot or there was frost in the air. But they made all sorts of sport of the poor Indian. And, indeed, ' they laughed at him so much, and made so much fun of his short legs and big feet, that ' he often wished he was dead. For he was very sad and sensitive.</p>
