        <p>became of Ezr.mA Of SKIN IRRITATION? If so. One Box of GRANNIE OINTMENT will effect a Speedy Cure. .A little at sight is baby's delight. A mother at Levtonstone, E,ei, writes :—" Granule Ointment has quite cured Baby's eczema. I have tried siVeral other creams, but thec only relieved, whereas Geared Oint• ment quite ,oretl." AISO Cures Bad Legs, Wounds, Ulcers, sc., like magic. Boxes, 14 Stamps, Poet Frf GRANNIE CO.. NOW'</p>
        <p>"Do you keep a full supply of football requisite here?" asked a gloomy-browed young Ulan the other day, as he entered Barlow's shop. Yes, sir; everything in that line." "Then you 212aY wrap me up a bottle of arnica, a paper of court plastv, and an arm sling. I'm going to Play iii a match this afternoon."</p>
        <p>A great crowd being gathered around a poor cobbler, who had just died in tke street, a gen- , tleman asked a bystander, who happened to be the well-known G. A. Stevens, the facetious author of the Lecture on Heads, what was to be seen? The wit, with more humour than the circumstances allowed, replied, "Oh I only a Gobbler'e anti-"</p>
        <p>Shopkeeper: "What can I show you, sir?"</p>
        <p>Absent-minded Professor: "I want—let me see, what do I want? Dear me! I can't for the life of me remember what it is Well, well, it doesn't matter, give me the nearest thing you have to it."</p>
        <p>"Is ypur horse perfectly gentle, Mr. Dabster?" "Perfectly gentle, sir. The only fault he has got, if that be a fault, is a playful habit of extending his hinder hoofs now and then." "By extending his hinder hoofs you don't, mean kicking, I hope?" "Some people call it kicking, Mr. Green, but it's only a slight reaction of the musclea.".</p>
        <p>Keen jeulonsies lire as rampant in the proles- shill of model -as in :my other, The eckhow !edged " Trilby " bolds herself as superior te the (4:mama-or-garden model as ,a star actress, does to a " on." She is a conndisseur in figures. and does not hesitate to criticise a rival's attractions in merciless. and plain,spoken language. Some models are born to the busi- ness. Their mothers mid grandmothers before them knew iiii other life than that of tile studio, mid their children leant to pose as sbon as they. eau toddle. Pad this is by no menus always :he case. Yoting girls, tired of the ill-paid drudgery of the unskilled occupations, are tempt «1 by the seven and sixpence a day to be earned in it stadia, and if they are gifted with exceptionally good 1:-.uks they may earn their wing litendly 0/1, their heads.' I can call to »dud it few who graduated from this studiom to the photographers' windows as professional iii ities, an„d from the• photogrwpbers windows tbe stage. Miss Dorothy lieue, for instance, viol; " discovered.". by the late Lord Leighton, :lid it was somewhat amusing to observe at Academy soithes the greeting between the Pre- -aleut, and the. professband beauty, who perhaps followed 011 the heels of it Bishop. The twinkle ii the eye was reserved ior the ex•model. :/rdinary good looks are, however, not suf- ficient to obtain reguiar employment. Tie figure .s equally, or perhaps more useful. And here comes the ordeal fOr the would-be model mit lirought up in the studios. She finds site has I', sit for the ." altogether !" Well ! there it ! Mrs. Grundy may hide her face, but the pictures of Lord Leighton, Sir E. J. Poynter, Marcus Stone, Waterhouse, Blair Leighton, and 'any number besides, could not have been painted without the altogether,' so we have to chcose• between Mrs. Grundy end halt the world of pictures.</p>
        <p>NUS. GRUNDY AND THE "ALTOGETHER."</p>
        <p>However difficult it may be for that excellent lady to believe the fact, the aver- ay. professional English model is both re- peeta bl e and self-respecting. Especial I y is this the case with models who have been born and brought up in the atmosphere of the studios, and who unconsciously imbibe frein their surroundings much of the artistic feeling for. and love of, beauty in the abstract whieh makes for an absence of self-consciousness tin- possible except under such circumstances. I know ,se mere babies of girl models 80 brought up, whose mothers and grandmothers sit,' and who themselves ask for and receive full models' %rages of seven and sixpence a day when en- gaged. These children pose quite natinally and fall into pretty attitudes whatever they may be doing. I recall some mites who used to rail often at my house to know if they were wanted to. sit, mid who when allowed to play in the garden and pick some flowers; were most fas- cinating to watch, so picturesque were. their poses. One of these juvenile models was each a mere baby—though quite at home in studios and accustomed to ' sit '—that taking a fancy to me or to zny house, she flatly refused to leave toe at the end of the day, expressing her inten- tion ot living with me always. Only after much persuasion and the gift of several toys was she induced to allow herself to be taken home, weeping, by a sister very little older, but, with the precocity of her class, already quite a self- possessed little woman, accustomed to take bev baby sister round the studios, and arrange for both their engagements in sitting I</p>
