        <p>The ordinary individual, on passing in the ire t a young woman of pictme,qne mkempt appeanince, whose ;,•;,:ntsils 1,51.1.7 in liuging folds and owe In the rrvvitil- whose hat is ot iii ile tiiii,ii.ii,.lls, dit would be sconied by any self-fespecting and whose feet appear shod more for outiort than smart appearance, would probably einark with intnitivd conviction, " There goes n artist's model !" If the entire costume bears he unmistakable stamp of " home made " .the dentilleation would very probably be correct. Although many other women scorn fashion dates and dress on the rational or testhetic ninciple there is generally no fault to be found xith the workmanship of the garments. As a .ule eccentricity is a fad of the fairly .well to do. ;Mt the artist's model has no puktlic to dress or. Her home is in the studio, where so long IS she is picturesque 110 one cares to pry into details.</p>
        <p>VIE NECTSSITT FOIL MOMS.</p>
        <p>Although it is the aim of every artist to -cm- hody his ideas in preliminary studies, done, as a •hild would say, "entirely out of his own head," here are very few who can work up a drawing ir picture without the assistance of a model. A 4culptor especially, who has to make his work perfect from every point of view, would never itteuipt such an =possible feat. One of the reasons to which is ascribed the perfection of the old Greek sculpture is that the artists had continual opportunity, in .every day life, of studying the nude, untrammelled bunion birm. Men and women of the present day suggest by their mode of dress little of their natural titpires. The former present am arrangemont in tubes:— two -tubes below a tube on either side-; a small tube at the top—l refer of course to the conventional but most inartistic ' topper ' —and a large tribe connecting the others. A wonian represents an egg. holier draped frOITI the lower bulb, and to divine the form of the limbs as fashionably clothed would he sheer guess-work. It is all very well ftir such an artist as the late F. 0. Corbould, who found— so lie said—a broom with it coat on it sufficient to suggest a man in armour, but the average draughtsman is MOM exacting. .Where then does lte find his models ? Relations and friends are often so tar obliging as to sit for a head, but he has to suit their convenience, not they his, and he can only victimise them up to a eel t.,in point of boredom. The professional artist's model is the stand-ty. t•lie has not to be sought after, but goes the roubd ot ate studios in search of cio-agewents, although when she hos obtained Irie reputation of at " Trilby " she its only for a thocat few.</p>
        <p>TIM STATUS ANI) rizospEcTs OF, A. 'MODEL.</p>
