        <p>Much more difficult subjects are what x may call. impromptu-models, who don't know what posing means, but who are, for some purposes, more valuable on this account. Poor Phil May, io his inimitable sketches, of Cockney life, used these almost entirely. One of his funniest ho.ch sketches depicts a typical London slum child—a girl of about fourteen, in tattered short skirt, the remains of a maternal shawl, with which she vainly tries to cover the in- evitable baby, but still preserving some traces of her sex's regard for appearance and fashion in the form of it wisp of hair screwed up into what would appear to be a small knot at the back of liar head (this was in the days of enormous " buns ")—solemnly appealing to the artist who has lured her to his studio for sketching pur- poses " t4hall I tike down me bun ?" _</p>
        <p>4 In Mr. Haddon Chambers' play, " The Golden Silence," in which two acts take place in n culptor's studio, there is a delightful model, with aspirations stagewards. She inforins. the sculptor and an artist who shares his studio of her ambition, which she fosters by ' means of t-Ittitespernin recitations to her mothsr, mid gives them au example of her • histrionic talents reciting in the most excruciatingly wooden fashion Portia's beautiful ' speech beginning The Quality of 'Mercy," which is so well- SVI: II 1 stock test for recitation. Ile tirti,t, ..:fter malty anguished writhings, succeeds in stopitiug her in tie middle, and feels impelled to temerk afterwtolls •• you vo home and recite tied; ti tin nit tier every day, you'll soon Ito an orphan !" Tbe tntstels who sit for the figure classes: in the lieyal ~''‘.;idenly and other art schools are naturally wore lighile remunerated for their servi,es there thee ii, ri,ete studios, especially as it Is by no means every good figure mom who will consent to sit for a class. The wor; also is more arduous. Although a pose is giveh as little fatiguing as posgible, it has to be main• baited for fifty-five minutes, and utter ten minutes' rest for another ay-five minutes. It is not an nncommon occurrence for a model to collapse ist ti fainting fit under the prolonged strain of keeping one position combined will the heat of the room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>MODEL'S PERSONALITY.</head>
        <p>THE MODEL'S PERSONALITY.</p>
        <p>The great drawback in the case of good models who are much sought after is their want of i ility i keetkinst anDointinents. 11,./</p>
        <p>When the female model becomes too old to find engagements easily, if she has nit already married, and is obliged to earn her living, she can generally do 60 by trafileing at studios in art photographs, oriental stuffs, and bric-a- braC. in which her knowledge of what form good paintable accessories stancls her itt good stead. She can also undertake the making of ,costumes, and frequently she is able to intro- duce any particular type of model required. With regard to wale ritatep, the regular , studio 'type of young man is often Italian. Italians are as it rule more picturesque and more plastic than the average English model, although the latter may be found seriously cultivating his muscular system at the Halle TurnverPin. Old men are mom en,y to find of a picturesque type, and steal n, long hair and a pltrirelt:ll botird forLi it great part . of their stock riff 6 many of these more or levs -yener,.l;le lignres— of te» with it bundle suggesting e. 0.0 me ca Mittel. the ern— haunting studio P.ipiii.,eLri;oo(l4. When, .with the accessmies of ilowiug hair mid beard, an old model po,sesses it • tot in u lii ligitre. and clear-cut aristoeratic leanirce, in etill be pretty sure of a constant living. in the sludies. One such old 01f111-0 lii vu ii itt to riollo.oll model— figures in many of cer po:“tl!Ir pictures. Tyres of tAlreme old age hiet favour with tny artist 4, 1,0 that, roundly speaking, the laoression of model is mid the only cue so far r • all il.uhy be practised " from the eratlle to the gEaVe.."</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURFS.—According to " Kemp's MErcantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, October 60), was 17S, viz. : New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, 92; deeds of arrangement registered, 86. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 80; deeds of arrangemant 70—total 150; being an increase of 28. The total number of commercial failures recorded during the 40 weeks of the present year is 6,823 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 40 weeks of last year was 6,796 shtwing an increase of 27.</p>
