        <p>fet•tud, att.'s ~;:e is: Cra. 1,1" 11.. i 11,013vy 1.-i .or the part easily earned. and thev :•.;:etel trelfy, Illit there 111,1. liot Wanting ivt t 'teal ILD4I, the thoughtful types, who leal:e their own Male,: an:L save up their earnii:gn for a. rainy city, end they will also make their value felt in the shun° in making and repairing costumes and even oc- casionally by bfl:ering valuable suggestions for working methods picked up by them in their career. _ . _</p>
        <p>But while • the Lfe of it moiltil is lntrdly con- ducive to 601'11,118 r et.i ni, there are tho_se who feel the existence to be shallow and unsatis- fying. One at least I have known who, abjur- ing lighter evening recreations, made a study of religions, and after experimenting With every form of Christian observance and belief, found in Theosophy the solace she desired ! This, of course, was au exceptional case of a deeply thoughtful woman. Almost every graceful figure to be found in paintings, however adequately draped, has been evolved from a study from the nude. As popular examples, the figures in the late Lord Leigh- ton's, E. J. Poynter's, Waterhouse's, and Blair Leighton's pictures are always 'based upon un- draped studies. , Hence the necessity of " the altogether." English types of women find more favour with artists than French or Italian, and their figures last much longer. It is almost un- necessary to mention that no female model wears corsets ; she knows too well that en at- tenuated waist is of no value in studios, however dear it may be to the dressmaker. She also abjures tight boots, even V she does not, like Trilby, altogether forswear leather foot-gear in favour of cloth or cashmere boots,</p>
