      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT NOTICE.—Our correspondents are requested to carefully note that the alteration of our time of publication from Saturday morning to Friday afternoon will necessitate the forwarding of all M.S. to this lattice not law than Thursday.—ED." EL"</p>
        <p>Public Bowling Greens. The success of the three public greens at Burnley (a manufacturing town not quite so large as Warrington) reported this week by the public Press is gratifying and also again demonstrates clearly that with good manage- ment, even from a commercial basis, they are profitable to a community. It is also no secret that the public green in Victoria Park, Latchford, has for the short period it has been opened given satisfactory returns. It is the opinion of many in Warrington who have given the subject of public greens their careful thought and examination, and knowing the experience of other towns as well, that there should be no hesitation on the part of the Corporation to place one in the four quarters of the town. There is land already available for the purpose, tho Corporation having had the forethought to purchase land for open spaces. The subject should not require any further debate in the Town Council for it is past argument. It would also meet a want of healthy recreation of which Warrington in many ways is sadly lacking.—Yours truly</p>
        <p>E. ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>P.S.—The Burnley Corporation have handed over from the proceeds of their three greens £l5O for the provision of music in their town. "Music hath charms to sooth the savage breast." A little music in the vicinity of the Town Hall prior to the monthly Council meeting might tend to harmonise the proceedings. Apparently it is needed.—E. E.</p>
        <p>What Will the Voter Do? lam much obliged to " Free Critic " for his letter in reply to mine, but to my mind, he does not seem to keep to his text. The point of " Free Critic's " first letter was I think" Alien Labour," but my friend runs away from his subject and begins talking about the " House of Lords," Increased taxation," The South African War," Scc., Stu. With reference to "the subject," can he answer me this question : "If the Liberals are returned to power at the next General Election (when it conics) will they or will they not rescind Chinese Labour in South Africa ?" Personally. I think not. Had they wanted they could have stopped the measure in the House. Had they done so it would have spoiled their little game. What our Liberal friends, want is power—not power with honour but poWer at any price—Office (capital 0 please). That is the trick they want and Chinese Labour is their trump card—lt is all clap- trap fellow working-men (I speak as one of you). We are fools to ourselves. Some of us are working like slaves for our 18 bob per week (17s. 10d. I believe it is at a large works near Bank Quay) and then when some- one makes; a" shout " about slavery hundieds of miles away and which does not affect' us we are ready to go mad. It is only an " Election shout" this slavery busi- ness, and" Free Critic " knows it is, so does Mr. Cros- field, but like the rest of the Lib ?rals up and down the country they intend to play it for all it is worth. It is so easy to condem a measure, but is not often that any Party who makes an Election Cry makes a rectification of the measure when they come into Power. Even if Chinese Labour is wrong (which I don't for one moment admit) my argument is that this Liberal War- cry about " Slavery "is not a conscientious one, and if I may call one of your phrase " Free Critic " it is all "clap trap."—Kind regards "Free Critic" I still remain faithfully yours, Warrington, 10th October 1905.</p>
        <p>"A HIGHER CRITIC."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>As November Ist draws nearer, interest in the forth- coming municipal elections increases. Numerous ward meetings have been held, and the candidotes up to the present are : Town Hall Ward Mr. W. Benson (N.T• &amp; C)* Whitecross Ward Mr. James Evans (L)* Bewsey Ward Mr. P. Peacock (L)* Mr. E. Plinson (Labour)* Mr J. Pemberton (N.T.&amp; C)* Mr. J. G. Craik (incl.) Mr. John Roberts (C)* Mr. le. Stott (Labour)*</p>
        <p>Latchford Ward Fairfield Ward t. John's Ward Orford Ward.</p>
        <p>lio-W-leY Ward Mr. E. Pickwiance (t, St. Austin's Ward Dr. Joseph (N.T. &amp; U)* *Denotes retiring Councillor. Mr. A. Horrocks will not seek re-election in Howley Ward.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee for Fairfield Ward was held on Monday evening, Mr. George Walker presiding. It was decided not to offer any opposition to Councillor Craik who is seeking re-election. A meeting of the Conservative Committee was held on Tuesday eveniag at the club in Sankey-street. Mr. Robert Bolton in the chair. It was resolved not to oppose the candidature of Councillor Craik. Coancillor Craik has been nominated as follows:—:</p>
        <p>Proposed by Canon Willis, seconded by Canon Morley Stevenson and assented to by the Rev. T. W. Barber, Messrs. John Henry Draper, Thomas Humphreys. Joseph Sims, Arthur J. Jones, James T. Locker, W. H. Smalley and George Ball. Proposed by Mr. Samuel Taylor' seconded by Mr. John T. Whittle, and assented to by Messrs. H. J. Wilcox, John E. Harrison, Alfred Podrnore Arthur E. Boardman, Fred Miller, John T. Thornifey, James Piot Hollins, John S. Hill. Proposed by Mr. Peter Turner Wiuskill, seconded by Mr. Wilkinson Haigh, and assented to by Messrs Robert Woods, Thos. Middleton, Robert Smith, John L. Bate CharlesJ. Chamberlain, Benjamin Hall, Robert Lnnt and Walter Bradshaw. . .</p>
        <p>Proposed by Mr. John Eckersley, seconded by Mr John Hardiman, and assented to be eatience Eckersley Mary Hunt, Agnes Lyttigne, and Messrs. James Kilduff Henry Houghton, John P. Hazlelaurst, Wm. Dilworth and John Dilworth. Proposed by Mr. George Arnold Mounfield, seconded by Mr. Henry Maddock, and assented to by Messrs. James A. Forshaw, Dyson Whitham, Robert Bolton, William Butterworth, George H. Bottoms, William Povey, Joseph Allison and John Clayton. Proposed by Mr. Alfred Gibson, seconded by Mr James White and assented to by Messrs. Joseph Buckley, Thomas Austin, George Carter, Alfred Hough, William J. Moore. Henry Hickinbottorn, William Habberley, and Herbert Sykes. Proposed by Charlotte E. Yeomans, seconded by Annie G. Atherton, and assented to by Elizabeth Norcott. Elizabeth Smith, Edith M. Caldwell, Lily E. Caldwell, Sarah A. Porter, Annie Clayton, Elizabeth Howard, and Charlotte Cannel!.</p>
        <p>MR. BENSON AND TOWN HALL WARD. HIS POLITICAL "COLOUR."</p>
        <p>The Conservative Committee for Town Hall Ward met at the Conservative Club on Monday evening under the presidency of Councillor Forshaw. There were also .present : —Connelllore Ashcroft, Lord, Wilson, and Dr. Joseph, Mr. E. Draper, Mr. George Jolley. Mr. William Holbrook (agent), and Mr. Herbert Hayes (manager of the Junior Conservative Club). TheCIIAIRNIAN said Mr. John White had advised that Councillor Benson, the retiring candidate, be not opposed, and it was always wise to accept his advice. (Hear, hear). On the motion of Mr. MARRATT, seconded by Mr. A. B. TAYLOR., it was decided not to oppose Councillor Benson. . _ .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he took it that if any Liberal came out the Conservatives would give Mr. Benson their support. Some discussion took placte'as to the meaning of the resolution, Mr. GEORGE JoLLEr remarking that he could not understand it. He wanted to know what it meant. _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: That we do not oppose him. One or two members of the!committee expressed regret at the attack on the Mayor and Town Clerk at the recent Non-Traders' meeting at the Parr Hall. Councillor Lotto : Well, let the Mayor hold a meet in," and pitch into us. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN remarked that this was not a Non- traders' meeting, hut a meeting which had been called, to quote from a circular convening it, "to select a candi- date." _</p>
        <p>COURCiIiOr DR. JOSEPH said the resolution seemed to him to be a negative one, and it rested with the mem- bers individually to give Mr. bensen support. The association, however, were not bound as 'a body to support Councillor Benson. The CHAIRMAN: They are bound not to oppose him. Dr. JoSEPII : But that does not give any actual</p>
        <p>support. Councillor LORD : He will get it.</p>
        <p>Dr. JOSEPH : As a matter ot fact Councillor Benson is a thorough Conservative. I have known him many years, and he is a most worthy man. He will do every- thing as straight as a die, and I can most cordially sup- port his candidature; indeed, I Came here at great in- convenience to do so. Mr. GZORGE: JOLLY ; That will not satisfy me, un- less the Association do their utmost for him if he is opposed. (Hear, bear). Councillor Wilson thought the Non-traders had caused a great deal of dissatisfaction, but they should all be united in view of the approaching elections. He had been under the impression that Mr. Benson was a Liberal, and he was therefore, glad to find that he was an out and out Conservative. He contended that they should sink all personal differences and support him. (Hear, hear). Councillor FonsnAw,in reply to a vote of thanks said it had been quite a pleasure to him to attend the meet- ing, and he hoped that that would be the case for many years to come. He would do whatever he could for onservdtism, and if Councillor Benson was opposed by the opposite side he should also give him all the support he could. He (the chairman) believed in politics ruling in the Cout,il, as they got a respectable class of men to serve wl.on they made ttu; elections political. He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <p>hoped they would always have a Conservative majority, and work harmoniously together. The Parliamentary election would take place before long, and then they would have to put their shoulders to the wheel. (Applause). MR. E. PLINSTON TO CONTEST BEWSEY WARD. Mr. E. Plinston will contest Bewsey Ward as a Labour candidate in opposition to Councillor Peter Peacock. A circular issued to trade union branches by the Labour Representation Committee states :—" After careful and serious consideration, we have decided to contest one seat at the forthcoming Municipal Elec- tions. Various wards were suggested, but ultimately Bewsey Ward was selected. Messrs. 0. Buckley, E. Horrocks, and Ed.vin Plinston were numinated, but on the two former withdrawing, Mr. Plinston was unanimously adopted; and it is with confidence we place the nomination before you, feeling sure that his work on the Education Committee and the Trades Council will be sufficient guarantee for his standing as a direct Labour candidate. Considering the great need for Labour Representation on our Town Council, the committee regrets that it cannot put forward more than one candidate. We therefore ask all those Trade Unions yet outside the local Labour Representation Committee to join hands with those Societies al wady pledged to the only solution of the Labour problem, viz., Labour Representation. At the meeting of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening' Mr. 0. Buckley, in reporting the decision of the Labour Representation Committee, said the Trades Council were responsible in the first instance for Mr. Plinston's candidature—as they recommended his nomination. If the members of the Council did not put forth all their energies Mr. Plinston would not win. Councillor Stott was seeking re-election in Orford Ward and the committee thought it quite possible for the two candidates to find sufficient workers to fight the wards successfully. On the motion of Mr. EDWARDSON seconded by Mr Ilicluntorrolst the Council endorsed the candidature of Councilor Stott and Mr. Plinston. MR. PLIXSTON AND THE LIBERAL PARTY. THE " EXAM INER'S " CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>3lr.PtlxsToN thanked the Council for nominating him. He wanted to say definitely that he stood on tho plat- form of labour independent of either what appeared in newspapers or what was said outside. It was on the Labour programme and under the Labour flag that he I should fight Bewsey Ward. He had nothing whatever to do in regird to truckling with any party,whether Liberal, Conservative,or anything else. He was standing purely. as a Labour candidate, and he was fighting a Liberal I candidate. He was drawn over the coals for doing so, I but he could tell them that he had had and could claim greater support from Tory members of the Council on Labour matters be had had to bring before them than he had reeeived from Liberal Councillors. He had had nothing at all to do with working in connec- tion with Bewsey Ward as secretary for a period of six years. He had, he believed, been retained as secretary of the committee although he had done nothing since the late Mr. Malkin fought Mr. William I Rogers. From the time he had been in retirement from political warfare—in fact ever since he became a trade union official and became affiliated with that Council. He was willing to fight the battle and he asked for the support of trade unionists. Although there was money to fight against, although there was a combined force and an old-established organization, they could, if they worked, win a victory for Labour. Councillor STorr said it was the opinion of people who had spoken to him that it would have cleared the air if Mr. Plinston bad sent in an official resignation to the Liberal party. From the statement h 3 had made that night and also at the meeting of the Labour Representation Committee there should be no doubt as to his position. It had, however, been an error of judgment to omit sending in his resignation as a member of the Liberal party. If he had done so, the writer of the leaderette in last week's "Examiner " would not have been able to say that at the present time Mr. Plinston was a member of the Liberal party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>COUNCILLOR PEACOCK'S NOMINATION.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEACOCK'S NOMINATION.</p>
        <p>Tw.-) nomination papers have been handed in on behalf of Councillor P. Peacock. In the first he is proposed by John Barlow, seconded by Joseph Ward and assented to by Richard Travis, John James Fair- hurst, David Hewitt. John Coyne, James Gorman, Matthew Rogerson, John Nolan, Henry Mc.Nfahon. In the second paper he is proposed by William Seed, seconded by John Hill and supported by James A. Rushworth, William Guest, George Somerset, William Somerset, Richard D. Griffiths, John Rutter, John Rudge, and Samuel Holloway.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC MEETING.</p>
        <p>A meeting in support of the candidature of Councillor P.Peacock will be held in the Co-opemtise Hall,Wi uwick street on Wednesday. Addresses will be given by Councillor Peacock and Messrs. T. Barnes, S. Wright, G. Walker, J. Elwell, jun., and J. Morton. MR. PLINSTON'S CANDIDATURE.</p>
        <p>An open air meeting was held in Owen-street, on Thursday evening in support of the candidature of Mr. Edwin Plinston. Mr. Edward Horrocks, presided over a very small attendance. Councillor WRIGHT said that Councillor P. Peacock had absolutely no claim upon labour. If he had any respect for the interest of labour he would have made it his business to be present at the Council meeting at which the motion for the payment of a living rate of wages was'made and to have supported the motion both by voice and vote.No Liberal members could be labour members as it was impossible to have a combination of the two.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. PLINSTON delivered a brief address, in which he advocated absolute purity in municipal life ; the adoption of a minimum wage of 21s. a week for Cor- poration workmen; the adoption of a trade union rate of wages for all contracts ; the construction of artisans dwellings by the municipality ; and the adoption of a system of halfpenny tram fares.</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD. A meeting of the Whitecross Ward Conservative Com- mittee was held on Wednesday evening. The question of contesting the Ward was left in the Viands of a sub- committee, and the meeting was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>HOWLRY. WARD.</head>
        <p>HOWLRY. WARD.</p>
        <p>The meeting of the Conservative Committee of Howley Ward was also adjourned to enable several gentlemen to be interviewed with a view to the adop- tion of a candidate.</p>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE AND THE ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>At the weekly mPeting of the Progressive Labour League held on Tuesday evening under the presidency of Mr. Thomas Barnes, the November elections were discussed. It was unanimously resolved that the mem- bers of the League he asked to render all possible support to the following progressive candidates : NV hitecross Ward, Councillor James Evans ; Bewsey Ward, Councillor Peter Peacock ; Howley Ward, Mr. E. Pickavance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LABOUR REPRESENTATION ANOMALY.</head>
        <p>LABOUR REPRESENTATION ANOMALY.</p>
        <p>Official Reply to " Nunqualm's "Letter.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Warrington Trades and I Labour Council on Tuesday evening the secretary (Councillor Stott) briefly alluded to a letter signed " Nunqualm " which recently appeared in the "Examiner "under the heading of" Labour Represen- tation : An anomaly." The writer, Councillor Stott stated, alleged that the constitution of the committee had been broken as Mr. Walker, the committee's candi- date for Belfast, in his election address declared him- self to be a Unionist. He (Councillor Stott) sent a copy of the letter to Mr. Macdonald, secretary of the National Labour Representation Com- mittee, asking what was meant by the words " Unionist in politics " being mentioned in Mr. Walker's address. He had received the following reply:— Labour Representation Committee, Victoria Mansior.s 28,, Victoria-street, London, SW.—" Dear Stott,— Thanks very much for yours with enclosure, receipt for which I forward herewith. Re the matter of the Belfast election, Mr. Walker's position is a perfectly simple one. In a paragraph in his address relating to Irish Government, he stated that he !was r, Unionist. No one in Belfast and no one who had honestly read the address could mistake the meaning of the expres- sion. It simply meant that in respect to one question he held a certain view. I pointed out to him as soon as I saw the addresi how very open the expression was to misconstruction. It had never occurred to him before and Mr. Henderson, who happened also to see it before me, had not thought anything about it. The hubbub that the " Daily News " and other papers are dishonestly making about it is done for the purpose , of creating more trouble for the Latour Representation Committee. I was through the Belfast contest and on Ino bill issued and on no platform was Mr. Walker ever deseribed as a" Unionist and Labour Candidate " not even as a" Unionist candidate." His description was uniformly either " Labour candidate" or Labour Representation Committee candidate." He has signed our constitution and there is no man in Ireland who has stood more valiantly for independence in politics than Mr. Walker has. The expression " Unionist " has got a special meaning in Belfast and, uufortunately,ll4r. Walker forgot that it means something more on this side. With kind regards, yours very sincerely, J. RANISAY MACDONALD." Mr. GREALY said he knew the Belfast incident had I prevented a few thossand man from joining the Labour I Representation Committee. It had been stated that it was time the committee came out of their shell and let the public see what they were, instead of sitting on the I fence.</p>
        <p>Councillor Sro•rr said be understood the expression " Unionist " simply meant that Mr. Walker was not a Homo Ruler. The matter had been before the Labour Representation Committee and they decided that no "intentional blame" attached to Mr. Walker's action. The matter was not further discussed.</p>
        <p>111. "I have on my books," says a London beauty specialist 'an Ex-Cabinet Minister who sought my advice concerning a little bald spot on his head." Verily, as a London writer says, the golden age of English beauty has arrived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 14, 1905</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>INDIAN EMPIRE BAZAAR.</head>
        <p>INDIAN EMPIRE BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>GRATIFYING RESULT. ---- Saturday's Opening By Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>The handsome result of the three days' Indian Empire Bazaar held iu the Parr Hall in connection with St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket street, must be very gratifying to all who worked so hard for the success of the venture. The proceeds are estimated at nearly £BOO which will form the nucleus of a fund to be devoted to the Erection of a parish hail. On Saturday afternoon the bazaar was reopened by Mr. A. H. Crosfleld in the presence of a large gathering. Mr. W. Madden presided and qmongst those present on the platform were the Revs. Father Wilson and Dr. Mercer, Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, Mr. E. A. Cuuuor, Mr. Massey, Mr. W. Dilworth and others.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he presided over the bazaar as a matter of duty and also of affection for the Benedictine I 'order. He first became a Christian by being baptized by one of that Order. Then for some years they lost ' sight of him, but they seemed to be paying particular attention to him at the present moment. (Laughter.) Having expressed regret that Mr. Crosfield was suffering from a sore throat and therefore did not intend to address the gathering at length, Mr. Madden said he wished to pay a tribute to the great Benedictine Order. It was impossible for the world and for England in particular ever to repay the debt of gratitude that she 'owed to St. Benedict and his sons—(applause)—because I for three hundred years or more they had held the field and through the dear ages they peopled the world with light. Architecture reached its highest, point under the scientific knowledge, the holy zeal of the Benedictine monks who kept alive the light of faith and knowledge through dark centuries until the present Ime. (Applause.) They had done all that by quiet means. One of the minor English poets of modern times spoke of what was called "sweet reasonableness" and that expression of his had time after time been quoted with approval by an admiring world, as if it were suddenly startled into the acknowledgement of some new principle. But it was as old as the hills. Sweet resonableness was nothing but the worldy expres- sion for that great motto "Pax "or " Peace " which had for centuries distinguished the Bene- dictine order. There was no, religious body or coummunity of any kind in Warrington but knew what a power for good the Benedictine order had been in the town. Members of Parliament—he meant future mem- bers of Parliament,—(applause.)—Corporations and other bodies did much for the social well-being of the people but it was only those who plumbed the depths of society and touched the dregs of poverty amongst the people, like the faithful ministers of the churches, who really prepared the ground on which men like Mr. Crosfield and others could work. i These were the silent ministers to other people's wants. These were the men who made it possible. They were the ladders by which other men climbed to fame. They were content to do their work in quiet and peace, not seeking any reward in this world but grati- tude from the people amongst whom they laboured. He would ask that large gathering to show its appreciation of the efforts of the Order by making the bazaar a huge success, and sending Mr. Crosfield away with the feeling that he was proud to have had the privilege of assisting in the work. (Applause.) ' Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD who was cordially received, said the Chairman had kindly explained why he (11r. Crosfield) was—perhaps luckily for them—not in proper trim for detaining them at any great length. There were other reasons besides that to which Mr. Madden alluded why he should not do so. In the first place he was not' possessed of that delightful gift of humour which scintillated through the opening remarks which it had been so charming for thorn to listen to. They watild find another reason in a story he was going to tell them. Chestnuts were very plentiful at this time of the year, but he hoped he was not going to make them a present of one in telling them this story. It was about a very charitable old gentleman who went home and found a great load of hay upset before his front door. A little boy was trying to pitch-fork the hay back into the cart. The gentleman took compas- I sicn on him and said. " Here my boy, come in 1 and have some dinner before you go on with ' sour work." " Please sir." said the boy, "I *don't think me feyther'd like it." " Oh!" said the old gentleman, "never mind that. Come in and have some dinner." So he took the lad in and gave him a very good feed. Afterwards he produced a box of chocolates and offered them to the boy who hesitated about taking them, and again said," Please, sir, I don't think me feyther'd like it." "Tut, tut," said the old man, "that's all right." So the boy swallowed both his scruples and the chocolates. (Laughter.) But when a tempting picture book was offered him and the boy said he really thought his father would not like him to stay any longer, the old gentleman was struck by this and turning to the lad said, " You seem very much con- cerned about your father ; where is' your father ? " " Please, sir," the boy replied, " please, sir, he's under the hay !" (Prolonged laughter.) Well, that boy had a rattling good time. He was afraid they would he a good deal less pleasantly engaged in listening to him (Mr. Crosfleld), but still he hoped their feelings when he had finished would be less unpleasant than those of the old fellow under the "hay. (Renewed laughter). Another reason why they shculd not be delayed too long that afternoon was the fact that they had already had two excellent addresses, which he had had the pleasure of reading ; one of them delivered by Lady Acton and the other by Mr. Pierpoint. Might he plat take that opportunity of saying how very glad indeed theyall were in Warrington that although Lady Acton had lately undertaken new domestic duties, and although as he happened to know, in Switzerland she was taking a most active part in much of the work and many of the duties connected with the diplomatic work of which her distinguished husband had charge, she did not forget Warrington with which she had been associated all her life. He (Mr. Crosfield) was sure it would be a great pleasure to all Catholics, and not only to Catholics but to all Warringtonians, to see her Ladyship amongst them whenever she could come. (Applause.) •He had only one other reason to give them in favour of an address which he was afraid ought to be shorter than the one he had made already. A friend of his, who was not very far from him on the platform, had told him on another occasion whan he attempted to open a bazaar, that he was exactly like a square peg being driven into a round hole. (Laughter.) The friend also hinted that it was not for a mere miserable politician like himself (Mr. Crosfield) to rush in where angels feared to tread. (laughter)—and to venture to talk to them on difficult questions of theology. He would, however, like to add a word or two in spite of that warning, because some of his hearers might say, " Why are you, who come of an old Quaker stock, and who were brought up a member of the Church of England, why then areyou a privileged guest on a Roman Catholic platform to reader some small help if you can to a bazaar like this ?" He need not apologise, because he believed the object of that bazaar was one that must appeal to every- body. (Hear, hear.) But he had another reason besides that. He had always believed that there was a great common denominator, a great foundation for all the religious beliefs of all the civilised communities of the world. (Hear, heat.) The Chairman bad alluded to a remark about sweetness and light and he (Mr.Crosfield) believed that while Mr. Madden had spoken of a very ancient author of that very beautiful phrase, it was attributed in modern times to Mr. Mathew Arnold—or perhaps adopted by him. The same writer had also given us this very pregnant phrase about religion—he had spoken of the " unknown power which makes for righteousness." In his (air.Crosfield's) humble opinion it was upon that broad foundation that the various structures of modern religions were built up. If he might refer to a few typical personalities to illustrate that, they could begin with what had been called very beautifully " The pale belief and dins starlight faith " of John Stuart Mill. They might go on to the simple but very fervent belief that stimulated John Bright all his days in the noble work that he carried out ; end then they might proceed to the more elaborate faith which Mr. Glad- stone held to his latest breath, and to the frevent and beautiful faith of such great leaders of their own church —Cardinal Newman and Cardinal Manning. These men and the widely different degrees and measure of their religious beliefs illustrated the different kinds of superstructures which were created by the " unknown power that makes for righteousness " working on the faith and reason,or in some minds it might be, the hope of man. He might mention in passing that it was the strone conviction of Johu Bright,—because he once told his (Mr. Crosfleld's) father so—that in the greatest efforts he made in his life, as for instance the never to be forgotten speech in the Home of Conimons against the wicked and unnecessary Crimean War, he was in- spired. Was any one in that hall or out of it inn position to contradict Mr. Bright, or to prove that fervent belief of his an error ? He would also like to mention what a Warrington man of letters—Mr. William Davies—said to him some years ago. Mr. Davies was a man whose rare literary gift and singular chain of style would not be forgotten by any of those in Warrington who were privileged to know him. (Hear, hear.) Well, he remembered Mr. Davies saying on one occasion that nothing he had ever read brought the unseen world so neer to man as the ancient sacred writings of India. Referring to another very distinguished Warringtonia.n, he could tell thorn a little story of Mr. Glazebrook Ilvlands.(Henr, hear.)ln the old days his (Mr.Crosfield's) faher used often to take him on a Sunday evening to Thelwall to listen to mos interesting discussions between Mr. Rylands and the friends. who gathered round that old philosopher, that brilliant man of of science who had, he supposed, a wider range of learning and knowledge than perhaps any man who ever lived in Warrington—(hear, hear)— arid one evening the conversation turned on the diver- sity of religious belief, and Mr: I.lylaldB said he remembered one Sunday evening sitting on the slopes of the Great Orme's Head at Llandudno and watching the lines of children who came .frop the different churches and Sunday schools, winding their various ways up that great hillside. He said it reminded him vividly of the different religions and different nations of the world seeking by devious paths to find their way to Heaven. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Crosfield said that he respectfully submitted that the lesson to be learnt from</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <p>all this—and one instilled into him from a very early age—was just the great broad lesson of toleration. (Applause.) That was one thing that might be laid to heart, and he thought another thing impressed upon them more and more forcibly by modern history as the years went on was the increasing need in these days for more practical Christianity. (Rear, hear.) In the history of the great Churches, Protestant • and Catholio alike, there were pages which men would fain have re-written. But there were also noble and glorious traditions, traditions which the Chairman had referred to—(applause)—traditions which were an incentive to effort on the part of all the members of the different Churches to-day. He had always felt with the Chair- man what a great deal they owed to the Church that kept alight during the dark ages the sacred flame of knowledge and religion. (Applause). He (Mr. Cros- field) could not pretend to any very extensive knowledge of history, but he had read something of the Penal laws In the eighteenth century, and he had never read of those abominable and infamous laws, and the efforts made to oppose and resist them in Ireland without feel- ing very strongly indeed what the Irish nation owed to the Hedge Priests of that age who kept alive not only the religion of the people, but stood by them in their national life. (Hear, hear.) 1 To come down to oar own days, what a noble and splen- did example of what was called practical Christianity they had in the life of the great Cardinal Manning, so I beloved in many a thousanerhurnble homes in London 1 and throughout the land. (Hear, hear). There was a practical Christianity in the life work of those men who had striven for peace and justice and co-operation and good feeling amongst the different sections of society, and between the nations of the world. Because after all the efforts of these great men,and the duties of powerful churches, though they began with the United Kingdom did not end there. Such efforts in the great causes alike of internal and international peace would in the long run prove to be not only for the good of our own country and the empire, but for the common benefit: of mankind. (Applause). He had great pleasure in declaring the bazaar open, and was delighted to see such an excellent gathering. He hoped the results would be a great gratification to Father Wilson, and to everyone, especially the ladies, who had exerted themselves to make the bazaar a great success. (Applause) The Rev. Dr. MERCER in moving a vote of thanks to Mr. Crostield, expressed particular pleasure that Mr. Crosfield alluded to the work of Cardinal Manning. He would remind the gathering that the work of Cardinal Manning was being done by priests in every town and village of the conntry. The hearts of the priests were knit to the lives of their people and when they found poverty grinding them down, they did their utmost to help the people to help themselves and so to redeem them from their poverty. He thought the soundest way in which to help a man was to assist him to rescue his own independence. He did not believe in the scattering about of coin amongst workless men and women. That was not helping the genuine poor. He was particularly delighted with the applied Christianity which they found at Messrs. Crosfield's works and he believed that if every big works in the country would follow suit there would . quickly be an amelioratiou amongst the workingmen, 1 which was the one thing be had at heart. In connection with St. Mary's they were trying to provide a room not 1 for the lazy and idle loafers, but a place where the 1 young men and women could meet for their mutual advantage and betterment. Mr. A. E. CONNOR seconded. He remarked that Mr. Crosfleld's presence showed that the foremost towns- men did not hesitate to make any sacrifice when they knew that the object was for the well-being of the town and the bettering of the lives of the people. The vote was heartily accorded and Mr. Crosfield brietly responded. W. W. Drawortrit proposed and Mr. M ISSEY seconded a vote of thanks to the Chairman. The Rev. Faxnee Wit.sow in supporting said the object of the parish hall would be to draw young people from evil influences and unworthy amusements and to teach them their social duties in order that they might become useful, public-spirited citizens. The vote was heartily passed and Mr. Madden acknowledged the compliment.</p>
        <p>THE ENTERTAINMENT. The entertainments during the day were splendidly patronized. There were performances, by Mr. T. .J. Down's orchestra and hliss Turner's mandoline band, while dramatic performances and _concerts attracted large audiences to the crush room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ANOTHER SCEIESIE.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER SCEIESIE.</p>
        <p>At the church servi-c-e-Wt--gt:iftr-i's-'ahurch on Sunday night the Rev. Father Wilson announced that it had' been decided to complete the church tower, with the peal of bells. This work will cost probably about £1,500 or £2,000, and will greatly add to the appearance of a.° church. Messrs. Pugin, the architects for the church will draw up the plans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PASSIVE RESISTANCE. Ministers Summoned at Daresbury. THE " PRIEST RATE." •</head>
        <p>PASSIVE RESISTANCE. Ministers Summoned at Daresbury. THE " PRIEST RATE." •</p>
        <p>Tae Stockton He ith passive resisters ware again sum- moned to appear at the atresbury Potty Sessions, on Tuesday, for non-payment of the education part of the poor rate. The names with the amounts owing are B. Lloyd Jones, 75, C Lwdor street, (59. 61.) ; Henry Law, Whitefield-road (ss. 9d). ; the Rev. .LS. Hughes, Bedford-street (Is.;; and the Rev. Herbert Dunnico,2l. Fairfleld-road, (4s. 91)., all living at Stockton Heath. Mr. Jones did not appear. Mr. Edwin Braislaaw, assistant overseer for Stockton Heath, stated that the rate and costs altogether in Mr. Lloyd Jones's case amounted to 9s. The rate was levied on the 11th April by the magistrates. The education portion qf the rate had not been paid, and he asked for the usual order upon the defendant. The Rev. J. S. Hughes said he could answer for Mr. Jones.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. Henry Greenall) You would not be in order in doing that. You can speak for yourself afterwards. The usual order wee made. Mr. Law's case was then called on. __. • _ _</p>
        <p>The Defendant said he fidinitt-e-fa—certain portion of the rate had been withheld. The portion he hal with- held is the priest rate. The Clerk: Have you any legal objection to put forward?</p>
        <p>- - Defendant: Yes. I personally appear here because I wish to make a protest against what I believe to be a violation—</p>
        <p>The Clerk: I don't want to interrupt yo 1, hut you have no authority or power to make any statement here except in the form of a legal objection. I am perfectly aware of the fact that you have personal objections to the rate. All the magistrates have to do is to enquire as to the legality of the rate. You have no legal ob- jection, have you? Defendant: We would respectfully ask you to con- sider whether there must net be something seriously wrong with the law. The Clerk: We cannot go into that. Defendant : We hove to a imit it is the law of the land, but we protest as often as we can. The Clerk : The Bench quite understand that. Mr.llrtdshaw gave evidence. The Caairm in (Sir. William Long) : We have no option in our position as magistrates, as I daresav you know. We also know what you position is. You desire to make a personal protest. IVe are administrators of the law, and we can do no other than grant the rder. Defendant : IVe are quite aware of your position, and we can only ask you to discharge your duties as courteously and as considerately as you can. The Clerk : You may rely upon that bein" done. The order having bean made, the case of Rev. J. S. Hughes wits heard. The ro-v. gentleman •in reply to the Clerk admitted the legality of the rate and said the resisters were there simply to make a pro- test. He was glad to see that the notice was somewhat altered from the previous one, and that the fact that they were charged with not paying a part of the poor rate had been taken outa was only the educational part they protested:against. Mr. Bradshaw gave the necessary evidence, and the usual order was made by the Bench. _ . .</p>
        <p>The last else WAS that of the [toy. H. D Junius) Toe Clerk : t take it that you admit, the legality of the rate in the same manner. Do you? Rev. H. Dunnico : If one has to clothe one's ()hie Alan in a legal form, I don't admit the legality of it. Mr. 'Bradshaw stated that he asked for the usual order in this case also, as the education portion of the rate had not been paid. The Clerk : Have you any qaastiou to ask ? Don't mike a statement.</p>
        <p>Rev. 11. 'Jennie° : r have no cinestion to ask. The Clerk: It his beaa provad thit yau owe the rate.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Dunnico : I think I have a right to reply. The Clerk : Not unless yon have a legal objection. Rev. H. Dunnico : I wish to state my legal objec- tion, because lam afraid I don't admit the legality of the rate, for the simple reason that in my opinion it is contrary to English law to levy any rate in support of Roman Catholicism. The Clerk : I don't want to interrupt yon, but we have no power to go into that. The magistrates are simply here to administer the law. I know perfectly well what your reasons are. Rey. li. Dunnico : I hide myself behind the Corona- tion oath of the King, who pledges himself not to sup- po.t in any way Roman Catholicism in this realm. I don't see why I should. The Clerk : I know that is the reason, but you are not entitled to put that forward in a court of law. Rey. H. Dunnico : My objection is a conscientious one.</p>
        <p>The first round of the Lancashire Cup Competition 11 —an innovation of the present season—will be played: off to-day. It in no way clashes or has anything to du with the Northern Union Cup, and will, be coin- pleted before the end of the year. Warrington cannot ! complain of the draw for the first round, for Rochdale Hornets should not prove an insurmountable obstacle even at Rochdale. They did once put Warrington out of the Northern Union Cup Competition on the old Dane-street enclosure, hut Warrington should avenge that smirch on their record this time. The Athletic ground, Rochdale, has some pleasant recollections for Warrington people, for was it not there thatAradford i were put out of the semi-final six months ago after one of the severest ordeals the Warrington men ever Iwent through ? TRUSSES .—The new Water Pad Truss, never gets A stronger team will he placed in the field than has Hard.—The new Washable Waterproof Trusses always officiated during the last two games, and most of the clei.n, cool and comfortable.—The Triangular Fluid Pad old Cup-finalists have been called upon. This was Truss is effectual in most difficult cases. Trusses of. the only thing to do if the prospects of success were every known description.—Wntra&amp; WRIGHT. •trgicat to be made it all rosy. Experience counts for a lot Instrument and Artificial Limb Makers, 08, Es *show at. Liverpool. (Advt 2.) i m in these games, while current form is not much to go</p>
        <p>The order was made, and it was decided to issue all four distresses in one.</p>
        <p>by. The selected fifteen is as follows : Hallam, full- back ; Dickenson, Isherwood, Jordan, and Harris hreequarters ; Hackenhall and Brooks, half backs ; Boardman, Prest m, Jolley, Thomas, Harmer, Belton Swift and Cook, forwards.</p>
        <p>The Rochdale Hornets team has been selected as follows : —W. Rfgg, • full back ; A. J. Richardson, G. Fielding, A. C. Charles, and A. Gibson, three-quarters; Schofield and Kiley, half backs ; Turner, Richardson, Dearden, Hallworth, Lord, Thomas, Brook, Wood and Crabtree, forwards, /</p>
        <p>The "A" team is at home to Tyldesley in a Combin- ation match and should obtain an easy victory. Team, Knight, full back ; Short, Hewitt, Garside and Bibby threequarters ; J. Davies and Lloyd, half backs ; Ashton, Fairhurst, E. Lunt, FM-star and E. Hartley, forwards.</p>
        <p>Hartley is a new forward Warrington have secured from Bradford for whom he played last season. He was previously a Curnbarland County man and is a fine blifellow who should be useful. There is ajso a chance of Heath a well known Sllford scrummager coming to Warrington, He has got across with the Reds, and it only remaith for the latter to modify their teams for the deal to be completed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."l</p>
        <p>At present the Cup-holders are somewhat of a spent force and their progress in the League table inclines to be downward instead of upward. Misfortune has dealt rather severely with the Club of late, and as a consequence the team in the last two matches was only a shadow of its former self. The Barrow men, on Saturday, did not put up a very strong opposition and Warrington won with ease by 18 points to nothing, but on Monday they had a severe task in entertaining Kingston Rovers, the defeated of the Cup-final. War- rington's actual weakness was then made manifest, and the Hull men managed to secure the verdict by 8 points to 5 points after a hard game. This was a triumph they could not have anticipated and would be a very welcome solace for the disappointments of April 30.</p>
        <p>The greatest blow of all has befallen the Club this week end and it had a lot to do with the defeat on Monday, I refer of course, to Fish's serious injury in the county match at Wigan on Saturday. The evening papers that evening furnished Warringtonians with a shock, and the news was hardly, credited when the crack three-quarter's injuries were said t ) consist of a broken collar-bone. He has, generally, kept out of danger with the skill which characterises his play, but this time Fate was too much for the "irrepressible." He had scored Lancashire's only try and in trying to round the Cumberland men at top speed be was forced into the railings with the sad result mentioned above. In all probability we shall not see him in the field again before Christmas. The loss to the club is quite irreparable, and coming as it does on top of Kenyon's injury, it deprives the team almost entirely of attacking power. Fish was a spectator of Monday's match, with his arm in a sling, like a wounded warrior. Dai Davies, of Bolton Wanderers l and late of Swinton, W. Anderson of Wigan and Monies, the new Wigan forward were also present. The last named is a fine big fellow from Bridgend, and over 14 stone weight. I aria sorry Warrington gave such a poor show for the edification of the visitors.</p>
        <p>Just now. the Warrington Club is rather hard up for reserve talent, particularly at wing-threequarter and half back. Fish s loss makes the latter depart- ment weaker than it has been for years, and the authorities ought to strain every nerve to secure new blood. Pace is badly required, for neither Dickinson nor Harris are running quite so fast as in their palmy days, while none of the juniors show much sprinting power. I was much impressed , with the speed and workmanlike qualities of Fox, the 'Barrow right wing on Saturday. He gat precious few 'chances, but he showed that the big reputation he has made in North Lancashire is not founded on nothing. It is said that Barrow will not part with him, butt fear we shall read one of these days that he has been trans- ferred to Hull or Wigan or some place where the coffcrs are known to be well-filled. The Warrington treasure chest is also well stocked, and I don't think anyone would begrudge the investment of £BO or £lOO in a class man. f well remember Latham being transferred from Lancaster for £7O when he had just such a reputation as Fox of Barrow. Gifford is another good back belonging to the latter club, and hs ought to do almost as well at centre as in front of goal. He displayed plenty of pace as yell as punting power on Saturday. e •</p>
        <p>With Hockenhall arid Brookes in form, the hall back positions should not be difficult to fill, but bnh men are simewhat "off." In Saturday's and Mon- day's games, the " A " team halves, J. Davies and Garside, were given a chance. Davies was disappoint- ing as, though tricky at times, he was slow in getting the ball away from the scrummage. Young Garside showed promising form, but be is very slightly built for first-class football. Ile should train on, however into a useful man.</p>
        <p>Reverting to Nionday's match, which was the one serious encounter of the cwo, I may say that Warring- ton made a good fight considering the disorganised back division. Up to half-time they were exceedingly ragged bath on attack and defence, and both the Rovers' tries were given away. Later, however, the home players settled down and played a vigorous game. The forwards were splendid and secured the ball every time, and with Fish on the left wing Warrington must have won. Hockfinhall played like a " fish" out of water, which is hardly surprising con- sidering the way he has been changed about. Jordan was always active, while Harris did some really good work. The surprise-packet was Hallam, who seems to have suddenly lost his , form. He may be the victim of bad luck, but in nearly every recent match he has failed at a crisis and given a try away, while his fielding and punting are below par. The Rovers were smart all-round, and with such men as Barry, Gordon, West, and Jowett, they should do well this season. Jowett, the Welsh international; played a good threequarter game, and quite ran away from the Warrington men when he got a clear opening for the second and deciding try. Anthony Stark; proved himself still a fine forward and leader.</p>
