      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>WARRINGTON GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Proposed New Scheme.</p>
        <p>A sub-committee appointed by the Warrington Educa- tion Committee to consider the draft amending scheme for the government of the Warrington Grammar School - has completed its investigations and the report will be submitted at the meeting of the committee on MondaY• The sub-committee ore satisfied that the suggested amendment which provides for seven representatives of the local authorities out of fifteen members of the governing body together with the provision that addi- tional governors may be appointed by the local authori- ties in consideration of a money grant made by them, meets the requirements as laid down. The sub-committee recommend that the Board should be asked to appein,t not more than four co-optative members, and with regard to fees they are of opinion that a fee of ZS, particularly for young children, would act detrimentally' to the school, and recommend the substitution of minimum fee of .£l3 and a maximum of £lO. Witl reference to the appointment and dismiss!" of the assistant teachers, they suggest that the head master should have, subject t°, approval of the governors, the power of appointing an dismissing at pleasure all assistant masters in the school. The sub-committee also suggest that the governors should determine what number of assistant masters should be employed and also the salaries te be, paid, and suggested that the Boteler scholarships shal! be competed for by boys who have resided within the borough and have been educated for at least three YealS at any of the public elementary schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>LECTURES ON ENGLISH ESSAYISTS.</head>
        <p>LECTURES ON ENGLISH ESSAYISTS.</p>
        <p>First of the Course.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the Ca-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Friday evening at the first of a course of University Extension lectures by the Rev. W- Liwrence Schroeder, M.A., of Victoria University, Manchester, on " A trio of essayists : Lamb, liazlitt, and De Quincey." The Rev. Canon MORLEY STEVENSON, who presided, said they had in the past had many lectures on different phases of literature, chiefly on poetry or the drama and on one occasion Alan novelists, but he thought they had never had leillPares upon essays. Yet they all knew that the essay was perhaps the most ft nished product of literature, and one of the most di cult tests they imposed on any person to see whethe! he had the hall mark of literary culture and cultivatea expression. The Lecturer said Montaigne was regarded, rightlyr enough,as the real founder of the essay. The subjects be dealt with in themselves indicated the kind of mind and the essay indicated the method of attack. They were of the purely personal kind. They were not written to order,but rambled here.thare, and everywhere. They were the conversation of a clever,though tfu I, capricious i ndi- victual who was only compelled into straight paths by the demands of his hearers. So far as later writers approximated to Montaigne's immethodic ways an choice expression, so far did they attain excellence. We regarded the essay, continued the Lecturer, as mode for the personal expression in a very discursive fashion of thoughts and feelings generated by the chance contact of life and mind. There was no rea.see why the subject should be chosen, save that the writer so willed ; but most often the will was focussed ia moment of inspiration. The distinction in essays rae,B" be in the manner of the expression and tee style. The essayist must he himself. There must be no external restraint and no compelling force from the outside. His field was as wide as human life and he presented life through the intense medium of his ow? personality. The reader was never lost in the subject itself but always felt the personality of the man, e,e,a according to the expression of that personality in styl', so was the attraction of the writer. Style, marked 191 else and freedom, was first of all demanded from the essayist. Literature lived by the manner of its being' Secondly we demanded from the essayist sensibilitYi He mu,t be responsive to the subtle as we!: as the ostensible influences of life. In 31' nature he was essentially a poet, but he worked in tif prose medium. He must be of the meditative order of mind—a mind that could ruminate and allow itseld absolute freedom over any subject. Thirdly we demande_ from the essayist a certain intellectual power, and se experience of life ard a certain knowledge of previoas literature seemed to he necessary to the craft. Ite essayist must have the faculty of observation—the eV and ear must ever be open to the things of life. Thr man in the street, the king on his throne, the beg,s" the rich man, all phases of life and bein6 could afford material for the right kind ers observation, and the intellectual power Wae shown in the choice of the elements of experiet% , and in the combination which -set the direction of t' ; 'etls' essay. The intellect of the essayist must move qui , and unobtrusively. Knowledge must never be pe;rade-,„ for its own sake. With Lamb we were never conscioa', of the moving of the intellect. It all seemed so easY„ so beautiful, gracious and pleasant that we enjoYe: his work and did not ask questions. With De Quince't , they were sometimes irritated because he wanted to se. people right. The lecturer proceeded to trace the gress of the English essay 'and also touch!'.; upon the growth of the Press. Continuing, I'o said that of all the men one knew .t English literature, Lamb was absolutely the rae:t lovable. He always seemed to cheer one in the doh% moments of life. The early career of Lamb interestingly sketched by the Lecturer. It was, he charicteristic of his inherent nobility that Lamb forgot the person under whom he received his ea,o instruction—Mrs. Reynolds—for he allowed her. cb. W rte.t.„: annuity of £3O out of his own slender means. At S Hospital he made the friendships which praeW lasted the whole of his life, and that was another sp_ot did feature of his character. He (the lecturer) di know any other man in literature who, with the pre"tbe tions that Lamb received, endured everything for.. Tr. sake of friendship, so beautifully and so well be 1792 he became a clerk in the India House, remained 30 years. where</p>
