        <p>WOMAN'S. REALM. • DRESS—FASHION—HOME. THE PgrxcEss's DRESSES. Among the dresses order2l by the Princess of Wales for her visit to India are a large number. of muslin ones, thin, cool materials being indis- pensable in the Indian climate. In choosing these dresses preference has been clisrlayed for British materials and workmanship. Many of them are trimmed with lovely Limerick' lace, of which the Princess has a valuable collection. The Princes.s's favourite colour for day dresses seems to be the old-world., lavender tint, tinged with. a faint dash of delicate rose-pink. It was in a , costume of this rather unusual shade that the Princess made her first appearance in public after ,lurr betrothal to the Duke of York. A USEFUL COSTUME. Our sketch is intended especially to interest that army of girl workers whose livelihood de- pends on their, being at some place of business six days out of the Seven. Blouses are beloved of most girls, because, since they are mostly home made, and consequently fairly inexpensive, they can be renewed frequently. But tho but,- floss girl has little leisure to spend on making or washing blouses, and since nothing looks worse on a girl than a forlorn and passee blouse, we</p>
