        <p>very strongly to the provision that if the father welt TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL, unable to repay the loan advanced he %VAS diSfrallehiged• Toey also protested agaiust the provision of meals to school children being associated with Poor Law ,; administration, believing that the Educatiou Com- mittees ought to deal with the work. TARIFF REFORM. . On this question the Lancashire and Cheshire Federa- tion of Trades and Labour Councils—at a contereece representative of nearly every trades council in the area—unanimously passed a resolution condetnning l‘fr- Chamberlain's proposals and expressing the oPnlinn that Free Trade alone would not solve the ; industrial problems, but along with Free Trade theY must have the nationalisation of tbe laud, minerals and railways. It wt,s also a matter of satisfaction that se the recent Trade Unions Congress the proposals of the tariff refor ~era were almost unanimously conderaued by,. the delegates. OTHER QUESTIONS. Councillor Score emphasized the necessity of holding meetings of public bodies in the evening, in order that Labour representatives might attend. Touching efine the old-age pensions question, he contended that adequate pensions should be given to those who, bY their labour, had assisted in creating the wealth of the, country, instead of driving them into the Workhouse to end their days. In conclusion, he expressed the hop° - that the interest in the work of the Council, both 0t the part of delegates and affiliated members, week' increase. (Applause.) Mr. GREALEY moved the adoption of the report ale. also a vote of thanks to Conncillor Stott. Mr. Et.Laat in seconding pointed out that the Premier ' denied that the Unemployed Act recognized the datY Government to provide mor:: for the unemployed. The motion was carried. A special vote of thanks was accorded to Councillor Stott for his work on the Town Council. FINANCES. Mr. FARGIIER submitted the balance sheet which showed that the total income, including a balance brought forward of £2 78.2 d., was £3lBs. After paYi" expenses there was a balance in hv.rid of Gs. Id. Councillor &amp;row said that had it not been for arreare of contributions, the credit balance would have bee° almost as large as last year's ELEC HON OP OFFICERS. The following officers were elected :—President, Mr. T. Plumb ; vice-president, Mr. B. Flinston ; secretarYP Mr. F. Stott ; treasurer, Mr. F. Howliston ; execative committee, Messrs. Edwardson, Podruore, Plinston, Buckley and Hiekinbottorn. ' LABOUR REPRESEN rATIoN CONFERENCE. After some discussion the Council decided not to send a delegate to the National Labour Representation Committee's conference at Belfast. Mr. RucittnY moved that the Cminoil should submit a resolutic,btO the Conference "That all local Labour RepresentaLi'll/ Committees he allowed to become a filiced with the National Labour Representation Council " but this wen defeated.</p>
        <p>THE SECRETARY'S REPORT. In submitting the thirtieth annual report the Secre- tary (Colin&amp;llor Stott) congratulated he Council on the fact that during the year no branch htd withdrawn. It was also pleasing to report an increase of two branches to their number, and they trusted that during the corn- ing year the few remaining branches which still remained outside' would decide to become affiliated, so that the Council might be representative of the whole body of trade unionists in Warrington. Since becorniuga mem- ber of the Town Council he had recognized more than ever before how the influence of the Trades Council was weakened by a number of trade union branches refusing to co-operate with them. The number of affiliated branches had increased from twenty three to twenty-five, with an advance of ninety in membership. The attendance of delegates had been very unsatisfac- tory, and the council would advise branches to elect delegates who could and would attend and also insist on reports of the meetings being given regularly. No disputes of importance had taken place during the year. That was a lnatter for congratulation, as it would assist in removing the e:roneous impression that trade unions existed mainly to foster strikes and lock outs. The STATE OF TRADE in Warrington had continued very unsatisfactory, especially in the four chief industries—iron, , building, wire and leather. They learnt, however, from Press reports that there were signs of improve- ment in the iron and wire trades. They trusted that these forecasts would prove correct., for an improve- ment in any section of industry was bound to have a beneficial effect on other trades. Councillor Stott dealt at some length with the question of Corporation contracts, contending that the present standing orders were fir from being satisfactory to that Council and the general body of trade unionists. They did not, however, expect to get much improvement from the Town Comicil as at present constituted. TRADE UNFoN LAW. Trade Unionists, the SECUITFARIr continued, were still suffering from the disabilities of judge-made law, evidently ito,nroved by the Government. An attempt had beer: made—but unsuccessfully—during the year to place trade unions in the same position that they were thought to occupy prior to the • Taff Vale ',' decision. The whole question would have to be fought over again in the House of Commons, when they trusted there would be a strong force of direct labour representatives. EDUCATION. The Council had unanimously nailed a resolution in favour of the principle of free education, and had also considered the methods by which scholarships were granted. The opinion was expressed that the preient system was very unsatisfactory, the bulk of the scholar- ships being won by the children of fairly well-to-do parents and when scholarships were obtained by the children of the working classes, in many instances the parents could not afford to take advantage of them. The Council also passed a resolution asking for the whole cost of elementary education to be paid from the National Exchequer, so as Co relieve the industrial centres where the Education Rate was becoming a great burden. In Warrington a heavy expenditure would have to be met before the town was provided with sufficient and efficient elementary school accommodation. LA1301.7n REPRESENTATION. Ad.-nirable work was done by the Labour Representa- tion Committee representatives in Parliament during the last session. It was expected that over-fifty labour candidates wonlcl contest under the -Committee's auspices at the next general election, and they hoped to see a large number of them elected. The negotia- tions which were preceding at the time of the last annual meeting with the object of securing a Labour candidate for Warrington were broken off shortly after- wards. They still hell the opinion that Warrington was a very suitable constituency for a Labour candidate, being it compact and almost entirely industrial constituency. The principal reason for no Labour candidate having been seoured was the apathy, not only of the rank and file, hut also of the officials of the local trade union branches. Evidently there was need for much education on the question of Parlia- mentary Labour representation before any further attempt was m.tde to secure a Labour candidate for 'Warrington. Councillor Stott proceeded to express regret that the local Labour Representative Committee I " Glimpses of Australia and New Zealand " had not been successful as the promoters hoped it would This was the subject of a highly interesting lecturf be. They trusted, however, that the Trade Unionist of delivered in St. Paul's schema, Kendrick-street '37 Warrington would give the committee their undivided Monday evening by the Rev. R. B. De Wolf, ° support-, so that the workers might secure that repre-, Liverpool. There was a large attendance, the Eel' sentation on the Town Council and Board of Guardians to which they were entitled and which they could have W. Bracecamp presiding. Mr De Wolf is an accollr plished lecturer, possessing a rare fund of anfeh; when they made up their minds to claim it. !dote and humour, and hiB descriptions of RECENT LEGISLATION. Alluding, to the Unemployed AA, Councillor SToTT !beauties of " The land ; ed f the Southern eilDs: stated tbat altholigh it was very unsatisfactory to them were listen to with intense interest. Tile leetore was illustrated , with asplendid ser".4 a most important principle—that of state responsibility o lantern slides w enabled the andieree to f for the provision of work for unemployed men and women i f Ih. for! 't laid down. They trusted that the local distress committee to ho formed under the Act would be corn- I some conception of the magnificence of ilte scene:l4 which it was Mr. De Wolf's plenSwe to posed of room, and women who were determined to take see during Te full advantage of the powers conferred. Dealing with tear in Austra